\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In New York, Planning to Protect Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Zraick and Grace Ashford \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders said they were working to enshrine New York as a safe haven for women seeking reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders in Albany decried the news coming out of the Supreme Court on Tuesday, suggesting they were considering a range of options that would help to enshrine New York as a safe haven for women seeking reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe vs. Wade, as it appears poised to do, New York and other states with strong support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could see an influx of people seeking abortions traveling from states that ban the practice.\par \par \uc2 State Senator \uc2 Liz Krueger\uc2  of Manhattan has introduced a bill that would protect New York doctors who treat those patients by prohibiting law enforcement from cooperating with out-of-state investigations on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases.\par \par \uc2 Connecticut lawmakers passed a similar bill on Friday, and Gov. \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2 , a Democrat, has said he intends to sign it. The bill would also expand the pool of people who can do certain types of abortions, to include nurse-midwives, physician assistants and other professionals.\par \par \uc2 Other bills introduced in the New York State Senate include one sponsored by State Senator \uc2 Alessandra Biaggi\uc2 , who represents parts of the Bronx and Westchester, that would establish an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access fund and allow taxpayers to contribute to it, and another sponsored by State Senator Samra G. Brouk, of Rochester, that would require health insurers who offer maternity coverage to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another bill from Ms. Krueger would establish an Equality Amendment to the state's constitution, protecting a person's right to access reproductive care, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, \uc2 Andrea Stewart-Cousins\uc2 , said on Tuesday that it was ''an outrage that the Supreme Court is poised to reverse the rights of women in this country'' and that she expected to pass legislation to reaffirm \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before the end of the session.\par \par \uc2 ''Certainly, we do not want anybody who is going to be hunted in Texas to be hunted here,'' Ms. Stewart-Cousins said.\par \par \uc2 In their first appearance together, Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and her newly announced pick for lieutenant governor, Representative Antonio Delgado, denounced the leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe, and hinted the state would take action.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Delgado said that the court was ''reasserting a social order grounded in patriarchy and male dominance over a woman's body.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul told reporters to ''stay tuned'' for movement on the legislative and executive fronts to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Her office also said on Tuesday that it would appoint a representative of women's health service providers to the Public Health and Health Planning Council.\par \par \uc2 New York has strong protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the books already. In 2019, then Gov. \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2  signed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which enshrined the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event that Roe v. Wade were overturned, expanded access to abortions, and allowed abortions after 24 weeks to protect the mother's health or if the fetus was not viable.\par \par \uc2 State lawmakers in Albany were planning to rally at the Capitol at 5 p.m., and other officials, including Attorney General Letitia James, said they would attend another rally, in Foley Square in Lower Manhattan, at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220504ei540003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
