\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Key Passages From the Leaked Draft\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maria Cramer \par \uc2 530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The wording in the 98-page ruling by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . could still change. As drafted, it shows a majority of the court voted to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., who wrote the draft opinion for the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, said in it that nothing in the text of the Constitution supports a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  belonged to the states.\par \par \uc2 He wrote that Planned Parenthood v. Casey, which reaffirmed Roe in 1992, ''short-circuited democracy.''\par \par \uc2 In the draft, Justice Alito said the decision was ''not based on any view about when a state should regard prenatal life as having rights or legally cognizable interests.''\par \par \uc2 He also cited the history of punishing ''abortionists'' in the United States, and said there was evidence that the laws enacted against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were ''spurred by a sincere belief that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  kills a human being.''\par \par \uc2 Here are some key passages from his drafted argument, which could change before official publication.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito said Roe imposed a 'highly restrictive regime on the entire nation.'\par \par \uc2 At the time of Roe, 30 states still prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all stages. In the years prior to that decision, about a third of the states had liberalized their laws, but Roe abruptly ended that political process. It imposed the same highly restrictive regime on the entire nation, and it effectively struck down the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws of every single state.\par \par \uc2 He wrote that 'the Constitution makes no reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'\par \par \uc2 We hold that Roe and Casey must be overruled. The Constitution makes no reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and no such right is implicitly protected by any constitutional provision.\par \par \uc2 'Roe and Casey have enflamed debate,' he said.\par \par \uc2 Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak, and the decision has had damaging consequences. And far from bringing about a national settlement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, Roe and Casey have enflamed debate and deepened division. It is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the people's elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 He cited 'an unbroken tradition of prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' until Roe.\par \par \uc2 The inescapable conclusion is that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not deeply rooted in the nation's history and traditions. On the contrary, an unbroken tradition of prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on pain of criminal punishment persisted from the earliest days of the common law until 1973.\par \par \uc2 He argued that elected representatives can decide 'how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated.'\par \par \uc2 Our nation's historical understanding of ordered liberty does not prevent the people's elected representatives from deciding how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated.\par \par \uc2 Women are not without 'political power,' Justice Alito added.\par \par \uc2 Our decision returns the issue to those legislative bodies and it allows women on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue to seek to affect the legislative process by influencing public opinion, lobbying legislators, voting and running for office. Women are not without electoral or political power.\par \par \uc2 Television crews setting up in front of the Supreme Court on Tuesday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Tom Brenner for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220504ei540003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
