\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Where  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Could Be Banned Or Strictly Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison McCann and Taylor Johnston \par \uc2 676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 13 states would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately or very quickly.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states have so-called trigger laws, which were passed in the years since the Roe decision in 1973 and explicitly note they would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within their borders if the Supreme Court allowed it.\par \par \uc2 In a few states -- Kentucky, Louisiana, Oklahoma, South Dakota -- the bans would take effect immediately. In others, like Idaho, the ban would go into effect 30 days after the Supreme Court struck down Roe. Other states may require certification from the state attorney general or a legislative council for the ban to become law, a process that could take weeks.\par \par \uc2 All of these states make exceptions to the ban if the life or health of the woman is in danger, but many do not make exceptions for pregnancies that are the result of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Five states have a pre-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that could be enforced again.\par \par \uc2 Five states have decades-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books that were invalidated by the Roe decision but could be brought to life again, though enforcement in each state remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic governors of Michigan and Wisconsin have publicly stated their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and have vetoed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills. This month, Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan went so far as to file a lawsuit asking the state's Supreme Court to immediately resolve whether Michigan's Constitution protects the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In West Virginia, the pre-Roe ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to take effect. But in Arizona, the governor, \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , has said that a recent ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks would take precedence. And Alabama would probably seek to enforce a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , passed in 2019, instead.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina had a pre-Roe ban that was modified in the late 1960s, and legal experts in the state said that a 20-week ban, passed in 2015 but currently unenforceable, would take precedence.\par \par \uc2 14 states would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier than fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen states would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 22 weeks or earlier, and many of these may move to ban the procedure entirely in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 States' ability to set limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past the point of fetal viablility -- typically about 24 weeks of pregnancy -- was affirmed in a 1992 ruling, Planned Parenthood v. Casey, that the Supreme Court also appears set to overturn. Without Roe or Casey, state laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 20 weeks, 15 weeks or six weeks could take effect.\par \par \uc2 Many of the early gestational limits are currently enjoined in state courts, but Indiana and West Virginia's 22-week bans, for example, are in effect. The attorneys general in some states would be expected to ask judges to dismiss or uphold their bans if Roe and Casey were overturned, said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , a state policy analyst for the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . ''That may take a few days or a couple of months,'' she said. ''It doesn't feel like judges are bound by any time frame for ruling.''\par \par \uc2 In some states -- including Montana and Florida -- the highest courts have previously recognized the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under each state's constitution. Despite this, Florida recently approved a 15-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would take effect on July 1, and Montana approved a 20-week ban in 2019. In Kansas, residents will vote in August on whether the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be banned or tightly restricted in as many as 28 states in the weeks and months ahead. Twenty-two states are likely to remain unchanged, retaining broad access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some are also working to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, especially for medical providers. The Supreme Court's final decision is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 Giulia Heyward and Amy Schoenfeld Walker contributed reporting. Sources: Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . Note: These sources differ slightly in their assessment of which states are likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220504ei540003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
