\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Being Pro-Choice Doesn't Always Mean Having Choices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Monica Simpson \par \uc2 1,274 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As a queer woman who grew up in North Carolina, I learned at an early age that my Blackness could be a source of great joy -- but it could also pose a threat to my safety and autonomy.\par \par \uc2 In middle school, white boys laid their hands on me without my consent when I sharpened my pencil. To travel through town, I had to pass a building dedicated to Senator Jesse Helms, a champion of modern-day anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. It was all a daily reminder of the tight grip that whiteness had on my full liberation. I did not consent to that either.\par \par \uc2 Systemic racism is built into every facet of our society, including sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 1973 the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision in Roe v. Wade affirmed the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , barring states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the point of fetal viability. But too many states, especially in the South, interpreted and applied the decision as strictly as they could get away with, disproportionately affecting women of color.\par \par \uc2 In the decades since, lawmakers have enacted hundreds of dangerous restrictions that have made getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly impossible for many poor women and women of color. In 2021 alone, over 100 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills that restrict or ban abortions were passed in 19 states. This summer, the Supreme Court could deliver a lethal blow to Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As devastating as that outcome would be, it's important to keep in mind that Roe never fully protected Black women -- or poor women or so many others in this country. That's because Roe ensured the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without ensuring that people could actually get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . People seeking abortions in America must consider: Do I have the money? How far is the nearest clinic, and can I get there? Can I take off work? Will I be safe walking into the clinic? For more privileged people, these questions are rarely a deterrent. But for many women of color and poor people, they are major obstacles. That's how white supremacy works.\par \par \uc2 It didn't help matters that almost as soon as Roe was decided, lawmakers started rolling it back. The Hyde Amendment, which first passed three years after Roe, bans coverage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through federally funded programs like Medicaid. In addition, 34 states and the District of Columbia bar the use of their state Medicaid funds for abortions except in limited cases.\par \par \uc2 The Hyde Amendment has made it very difficult for many women to afford an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, and that affects women of color the most: In 2019 women of color made up a majority of women insured through Medicaid. As a result of all this, many women have had to carry unwanted pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 This has ripple effects on people's lives. According to the decade-long Turnaway Study, women who seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but are unable to gain access to one are four times as likely to eventually live in poverty as women who were able to get the procedure. Their families suffer, too. Black children are three times as likely as white children to grow up in poverty and live in a food-insecure household.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, women of color in states with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws often have limited access to health care generally and a lack of choices for effective birth control. Schools often have ineffective or inadequate sex education. In almost every aspect of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women of color today are more likely to experience racism and discrimination in the U.S. health care system. We have poorer health outcomes compared with white women. Black women are three times as likely to die of pregnancy-related causes as white women. And police violence cuts short the lives of too many of the babies we do have.\par \par \uc2 These are complex problems, and they will require complex solutions. As such, we can't afford to focus on our sexual and reproductive lives with a single-issue lens. We must consider the ways in which all social justice issues intersect and affect the way we are able to make decisions about our bodies and the creation of our futures.\par \par \uc2 One hurdle toward achieving that goal is that for decades, white-led \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations were the default, and the experiences of those leaders are not the same as the experiences of people of color. That's why reproductive justice organizations -- groups like SisterSong, focused on grass-roots organizing campaigns, promoting policy change and providing education for our communities -- have been calling for changes in leadership and representation. We have made progress on that front, but we need to build on it.\par \par \uc2 What we need is a culture shift.\par \par \uc2 My experiences navigating my sexual identity and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  inspired me to become an activist and organizer, but for many years the organizations I was part of were led by white men.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't until much later that I learned about a group of Black women who called themselves Women of African Descent for Reproductive Justice. In 1994 it took out an ad in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and Roll Call to proclaim to the world that our reproductive freedom cannot be boiled down to a single issue. When I was introduced to the reproductive justice movement, it was the first time I was exposed to Black women leading and owning their stories and bodies, and that was powerful.\par \par \uc2 Their work was the road map that grounded my own.\par \par \uc2 In my state, Georgia, Black women-led organizations have led the charge in pushing back against unjust laws that disproportionately affect our communities and challenge our autonomy, from voting rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. People of color don't have the privilege of focusing on only one issue -- everything is connected. Reproductive justice has always been more than just being ''pro-choice.'' To be pro-choice you must have the privilege of having choices.\par \par \uc2 The fight for reproductive justice must be led by those most affected. To build collective power, we need a deeper investment in B.I.P.O.C.-led organizations. We also need to normalize sharing our \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories, whether we had one or held the hand of someone at a clinic. And we need to work to elect, appoint and confirm officials who are aligned with reproductive justice values. It's not enough to just show up when an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law reaches the Supreme Court -- we need to bring that energy to our local school boards, state legislatures, attorneys general offices and every election.\par \par \uc2 It is our duty to hold everyone accountable at every level, every day, because our lives depend on it. And because Roe might soon be gone. But we can imagine a better world, one in which we have not just the minimum, but stand at the mountaintop: true reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 Monica Simpson is the executive director of SisterSong Women of Color Reproductive Justice Collective. Her organization is the lead plaintiff in SisterSong v. Kemp, challenging a Georgia anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Monica Simpson, the executive director of SisterSong Women of Color Reproductive Justice Collective. (PHOTOGRAPH BY David Walter Banks for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220413ei4d0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
