\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas to Drop Murder Charge in 'Self-Induced  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 '\b0\par\par\uc2 By Giulia Heyward and Sophie Kasakove \par \uc2 855 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A district attorney said on Sunday that the woman ''cannot and should not'' be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 The murder charge against a woman in Texas in connection with a ''self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' will be dismissed, a Texas district attorney announced Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Gocha Allen Ramirez, the district attorney of Starr County, said in a statement that, after reviewing the case, he will file a motion on Monday to dismiss the indictment against the woman, Lizelle Herrera, 26.\par \par \uc2 ''It is my hope that with the dismissal of this case it is made clear that Ms. Herrera did not commit a criminal act under the laws of the state of Texas,'' Mr. Ramirez said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Herrera was arrested on Friday and detained in Starr County, near the Mexico border, according to a local sheriff's official. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organization, Frontera Fund, said she was released on $500,000 bail on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 According to the sheriff's office statement, which was reported Saturday by The Associated Press, Ms. Herrera was indicted on the murder charge after she ''intentionally and knowingly'' caused the death of an individual by ''self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside of medical care and can include the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. But in dangerous cases, it can include those conducted with supplements, herbs or vitamins; multiple contraceptive pills or emergency contraception pills; or physical trauma.\par \par \uc2 Many details of the indictment remained unclear on Sunday, including whether Ms. Herrera was accused of having the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or aiding one, or how far along the pregnancy had been.\par \par \uc2 The indictment came months after the Texas Legislature passed several restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Mr. Ramirez said that ''in reviewing applicable Texas law, it is clear that Ms. Herrera cannot and should not be prosecuted for the allegation against her.''\par \par \uc2 He also acknowledged that ''the events leading up to this indictment have taken a toll on Ms. Herrera and her family. To ignore this fact would be shortsighted.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ramirez and Ms. Herrera's lawyer, Calixtro Villarreal, did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 It was not immediately known what statute Ms. Herrera was being indicted under. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that took effect in Texas in September, known as S.B. 8, prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks but leaves enforcement to civilians, offering them rewards of at least $10,000 for successful lawsuits against anyone who ''aids or abets'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Texas Legislature then enacted another law, S.B. 4, which establishes a criminal violation -- a state felony punishable by a $10,000 fine and up to two years in prison -- for providing medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills after 49 days of pregnancy, or for providers who fail to comply with a series of new regulations and procedures. That law also exempts pregnant women from prosecution.\par \par \uc2 One section of the Texas penal code exempts expectant mothers from being charged with murder in connection with ''the death of an unborn child.'' Most states instead target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is deemed illegal.\par \par \uc2 In most of the country, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited after fetal viability, generally at 22 to 24 weeks of pregnancy. Several states, however, are moving to ban abortions at much earlier stages in anticipation that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will soon overturn Roe v. Wade, the 1973 ruling that established a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that prohibited states from banning the procedure before a fetus is viable.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Texas Right to Life supported Mr. Ramirez's decision to drop the charge. S.B. 8 and other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in the state ''clearly prohibit criminal charges for pregnant women,'' the organization said in a statement. ''Texas Right to Life opposes public prosecutors going outside of the bounds of Texas' prudent and carefully crafted policies.''\par \par \uc2 Stephen Vladeck, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 , said that the district attorney's reversal reflected what Mr. Vladeck said was a misreading of the law.\par \par \uc2 ''I think what this really suggests is that this was a rash decision, by a local prosecutor who might not have fully appreciated what the law does and does not prohibit, as opposed to a piece of a broader campaign of hostility to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Mr. Vladeck said. But he added it is only a matter of time before more cases like this occur.\par \par \uc2 ''I think this case is also a sobering reminder of how much discretion prosecutors have -- even when they're wrong on the law,'' he said. ''And how difficult it is, especially for those less familiar with the system, those with fewer resources, for them to push back against prosecutorial mistakes, or overreach. And that's a phenomenon that goes far beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting. Jack Begg contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting. Jack Begg contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Protesters stood outside the Starr County Jail on Saturday after Lizelle Herrera, 26, was charged with murder. A Texas district attorney's office will dismiss the charges. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jason Garza/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220411ei4b0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
