\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Idaho Supreme Court Temporarily Halts  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vimal Patel \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court says Idaho's law cannot go into effect this month as scheduled while it reviews legal challenges. The ban prohibits abortions after fetal cardiac activity is detected.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho Supreme Court on Friday temporarily blocked a law modeled after one in Texas that relies on ordinary citizens to enforce a ban on abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy as a way of sidestepping challenges to its constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 The court's order prevents the law, which would allow family members of what it calls ''a preborn child'' to sue the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, from going into effect until the court can further review it. The law was scheduled to go into effect on April 22, one month after Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  signed it.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of the Idaho bill have said that Texas' novel strategy has been effective in preventing abortions. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have argued in court that the law, S.B. 1309, is ''an attempt to end run settled precedent'' and allow an unconstitutional ban to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Under Roe v. Wade, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, states cannot ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus is viable outside the womb, which with modern medical technology is about 23 weeks of pregnancy. But the court's six conservative justices appeared willing to abandon that decision when they heard oral arguments in December about a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The court also declined to stop the Texas law, which allows any civilian to sue someone who ''aids or abets'' a woman in getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho law, which focuses on allowing family members to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, establishes a minimum award of $20,000 and legal fees. Lawsuits can be filed up to four years after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Representative Steven Harris, a co-sponsor of the bill in the Idaho House, has previously said that the Texas law it was based on was very effective. ''It stopped physical abortions, chemical abortions in their tracks,'' said Mr. Harris, who could not immediately be reached for comment about Friday's decision.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Little signed the Idaho bill into law after the House overwhelmingly approved it, he expressed concerns about the wisdom and constitutionality of the measure and warned that it could retraumatize victims of sexual assault. Mr. Little, a moderate Republican, said the law could conflict with Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''While I support the pro-life policy in this legislation, I fear the novel civil enforcement mechanism will in short order be proven both unconstitutional and unwise,'' Mr. Little wrote at the time in a message to Lt. Gov. Janice McGeachin, who is also president of the State Senate.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood, which filed the petition to block the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, welcomed the Idaho Supreme Court's stay on Friday, which was signed by Chief Justice G. Richard Bevan.\par \par \uc2 ''We are thrilled that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain accessible in the state for now, but our fight to ensure that Idahoans can fully access their constitutionally protected rights is far from over,'' Rebecca Gibron, the interim chief executive of Planned Parenthood Great Northwest, Hawaii, Alaska, Indiana, Kentucky, said in a statement. ''Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers have made clear that they will stop at nothing to control our lives, our bodies and our futures.''\par \par \uc2 The Idaho law is part of a display of confidence from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and lawmakers across the country, with both sides of the debate anticipating that the Supreme Court could soon pare back or overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho Supreme Court temporarily blocked the state's restrictive \uc2  law. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Rebecca Boone/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220409ei490004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
