\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pills Are New Target in 50-Year  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,710 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last year, after Texas passed its strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, surgical abortions in the state dropped by half. Many women found a workaround: pills. The week the law took effect, requests for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot up to 138 a day from 11 a day at just one service that delivers the pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers in the state were already on it. That same week, they passed another law making it a felony to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail and requiring doctors to comply with new testing and reporting procedures to prescribe them.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the new front in the nation's five-decade-long fight, as both sides anticipate that by summer the Supreme Court could overturn or pare back the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who argue that women should be able to make their own reproductive choices, see the pills as a workaround in states that have already moved to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Women in those states would get abortions not in the back alleys of pre-Roe America, but via a post office box or a friend in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, or from an online pharmacy in India.\par \par \uc2 To anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who believe ending a pregnancy is murder, the pills are a back door to be locked shut with new restrictions and heavier criminal penalties. In the first three months of this year, legislators proposed more than 100 restrictions on medication in 22 states.\par \par \uc2 States such as Missouri are attempting to reach beyond their borders to stop their residents from going elsewhere to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by pill or by surgery. Connecticut and California, meanwhile, are rushing to protect their citizens who might be penalized for helping women in restrictive states obtain the medication. One pill manufacturer has sued to stop a Mississippi law that requires the pills to be picked up and swallowed in a doctor's office.\par \par \uc2 ''Being able to mail medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a way to get around state laws, so it's a real threat to states and to advocates who want to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  everywhere,'' said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, a professor and interim dean of the law school at \uc2 Temple University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attempts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have accelerated since the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  loosened its rules on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last year.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. had initially approved the use of mifepristone, the first of a two-pill regimen to terminate a pregnancy, in 2000, but required it to be provided in person. As the coronavirus pandemic pushed up demand for telemedicine, and in the face of a lawsuit from medical groups, the agency lifted a ban on obtaining the pills through a remote appointment with a doctor. It authorizes only a limited group of providers to prescribe the pills -- which are taken 24 to 48 hours apart -- and allows the medication to be used only in pregnancies up to 10 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group that publishes an annual playbook for like-minded legislators, declared legislation against medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the first among its most ''pressing priorities'' for 2022.\par \par \uc2 Already, 19 states prohibit pills from being prescribed by telemedicine or delivered through the mail. Nine additional states are proposing to do the same.\par \par \uc2 The shift in priorities reflects the change in how most abortions are carried out. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, 54 percent of abortions in 2020 were by pills.\par \par \uc2 About half the states are expected to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Supreme Court overturns or weakens Roe v. Wade, and bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  apply to medication and surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  alike. On Tuesday, the Oklahoma Legislature passed what could become the nation's most restrictive law, an outright ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except to save the life of a mother suffering a ''physical'' disorder or illness. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who violated the law would risk a 10-year prison sentence and a $100,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 Telemedicine requires doctors to follow the law of where the patient is, so doctors in other states cannot prescribe or provide pills to women in any state that has a ban.\par \par \uc2 Many of the new bills layer criminal penalties on top of the existing prohibitions, on the assumption that women will procure the pills illegally.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, S.B. 8, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks, requires civilian enforcement, incentivizing citizens with bounties of at least $10,000 to sue anyone who helps a woman get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . S.B. 4, the subsequent law against medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , establishes a criminal violation for delivering the pills, making it a state felony punishable by $10,000 and up to two years in prison. A bill in Iowa would ban the distribution of the pills entirely, with punishments of $10,000 and up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 A law passed last week in Kentucky establishes a new state certification program for pill providers and will require their names to be published. It also created an anonymous ''complaint portal,'' which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say will encourage harassment against providers.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists say they intend the new laws to raise alarms. They portray medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Wild West of what they call the ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry.''\par \par \uc2 Nancy Tate, the state representative who sponsored a 60-page omnibus bill of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that passed in Kentucky last week, said during the debate that she had gone online and discovered that it was easy to obtain the pills. ''Within 15 minutes, I had this product on its way,'' she said, ''in a nondescript package with no doctor visit, no consultation, no information at all.''\par \par \uc2 Mallory Carroll, a spokeswoman for the Susan B. Anthony List, which helps elect anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers, said: ''A lot of people who have been in the pro-life movement don't understand the threat level. The industry has already been moving from brick-and-mortar clinics to chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have tried to create doubts about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- calling them ''chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and pushing regulations requiring doctors to tell women that they can reverse a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they change their minds after taking the first pill.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  says so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal, in which a woman takes the hormone progesterone after the first pill, is ''not based in science'' and is ''unproven and unethical.'' The Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and medical groups have obtained preliminary injunctions against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal laws in three states, arguing that they violate the free speech of doctors by forcing them to provide what one judge called ''misleading'' information to patients.\par \par \uc2 Still, the legislation that passed last week in Kentucky says that ''more than 1,000 lives have been saved'' by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal. Under a Tennessee law temporarily blocked by an injunction, providers who refused to tell patients about it would face up to six years in prison and health centers would face a $10,000 daily fine if they refused to display signs telling women about the procedure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates say the laws against mailing pills will be hard to enforce, as laws generally avoid punishing women who get abortions. Still, they do not rule out the possibility that people might report women who take the pills.\par \par \uc2 The threat of fines on doctors and the added requirements for patients have a ''chilling effect,'' said Dr. Bhavik Kumar, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider at a Planned Parenthood clinic in Houston.\par \par \uc2 ''There are so many restrictions in place that this just adds to it,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law requires two visits with the same provider, which can make scheduling hard. The doctor must test the woman's blood for her Rh factor, a marker that could complicate future pregnancies, even though medical guidelines say the test is not necessary before at least eight weeks. If she is Rh negative, the doctor is required to offer to administer Rh immunoglobulin to her. Doctors also have to give women other warnings not supported by science; falsely telling them, for instance, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ''Some patients breeze past it,'' Dr. Kumar said. ''But if you have a patient with a strong family history of breast cancer, you can imagine what kind of weight that adds. There's no actual link there.''\par \par \uc2 While medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be much less expensive than surgical procedures, the tests and doctor visits can drive up the price and the time it takes.\par \par \uc2 Aid Access, a European service, has continued to send pills to women regardless of state laws. Still, it saw the number of requests for the pills drop after the law criminalizing them was passed, to 30 a day from 138 that first week.\par \par \uc2 Several new groups providing pills within the United States say they will send them only to women in places where pill delivery remains legal. These groups imagine more complicated scenarios: A woman in a state that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders pills to a friend in a state that allows them, who then forwards her the package.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jamie Phifer, the founder of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand, said she hoped the increasing use of telemedicine that the pandemic encouraged would also encourage acceptance of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that way.\par \par \uc2 ''Seeing the clinician on your phone for treating your urinary tract infection and seeing the clinician on your phone for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't going to be that different,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Over the next months, the F.D.A. will establish rules to certify pharmacies that want to dispense medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, the law professor, said pill providers could argue that the F.D.A. rules pre-empt the new state restrictions. The manufacturer of generic mifepristone, GenBioPro, for example, argues that the Mississippi rules requiring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be taken in a doctor's office constitute an ''impermissible effort by Mississippi to establish its own drug approval policy.''\par \par \uc2 In the first three months of this year, anti-\uc2  legislators proposed over 100 restrictions on medication \uc2  in 22 states. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GABRIELA BHASKAR/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220407ei460000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
