\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Near-Total Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Oklahoma\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Levenson \par \uc2 784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The measure is part of a wave of stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions enacted by legislators in Republican-led states.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Oklahoma on Tuesday approved a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it the latest Republican-led state to forge ahead with stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation as the Supreme Court weighs a case that could overturn Roe v. Wade later this year.\par \par \uc2 The measure, Senate Bill 612, would make performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency'' a felony punishable by up to 10 years in prison and a fine of $100,000.\par \par \uc2 The Oklahoma House voted 70 to 14 to send the bill, which passed the Senate last year, to Gov. \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 , a Republican whose office responded by noting that Mr. Stitt vowed in September to sign ''every piece of pro-life legislation'' that came to his desk.\par \par \uc2 If Mr. Stitt signs the bill, it would take effect on Aug. 26, according to the Senate clerk's office.\par \par \uc2 Its passage came after Oklahoma became a major destination for women from Texas who were seeking abortions after that state enacted a law banning the procedure after about six weeks, a very early stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''If allowed to take effect, S.B. 612 would be devastating for both Oklahomans and Texans who continue to seek care in Oklahoma,'' a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, including the A.C.L.U. of Oklahoma and Oklahoma Call for Reproductive Justice, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''Nearly half of the patients Oklahoma providers are currently seeing are medical refugees from Texas,'' the groups said. ''Now, Oklahomans could face a future where they would have no place left in their state to go to seek this basic health care.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Jim Olsen, a Republican from Roland, and the House author of the bill, said the measure was enacted in anticipation of a pending Supreme Court decision on a Mississippi law that bans abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 If the court upholds that law, it could upend Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that prohibited states from banning the procedure before fetal viability, or around 23 weeks.\par \par \uc2 From Florida to Idaho, Republican-led state legislatures have been operating as though Roe has already been struck down, advancing restrictions that aim to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal in as many circumstances as possible.\par \par \uc2 ''Obviously, I'm thrilled because we have the potential of seeing many lives of babies saved -- part of that depends on future court rulings'' like the one in the Mississippi case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, Mr. Olsen said.\par \par \uc2 He said the bill passed without any floor debate.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody debated and nobody asked any questions,'' he said. ''I was actually kind of shocked.''\par \par \uc2 Mallory Carroll, a spokeswoman for the Susan B. Anthony List, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said Oklahoma, as one of ''the most pro-life states in the country,'' was following other states in enacting laws to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 ''This latest bill passage is yet another sign of the continued pro-life momentum we're seeing nationwide as lawmakers and pro-life Americans await a decision from the Supreme Court in the Dobbs case,'' Ms. Carroll said.\par \par \uc2 Emily Wales, interim president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood Great Plains, said the bill was one of a series of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures advancing in Oklahoma, including one that mirrors the Texas law, which effectively deputized citizens to sue clinics and others who violate the law.\par \par \uc2 She said anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  members of the Oklahoma House approved the bill as lawmakers who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were outside the State Capitol, speaking at a ''Bans off Oklahoma'' rally.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood, which operates two of the four \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Oklahoma, planned to challenge the legislation in court, Ms. Wales said.\par \par \uc2 ''This ban is more in line with the traditional bans that have been blocked in the past,'' she said. ''So we are fairly confident that, as long as Roe remains the law of the land, there is a path to blocking this.''\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Tong, co-executive director of Trust Women, which operates a clinic that provides abortions in Oklahoma City, said the clinic ''will remain open so long as we can still be of service to the people of Oklahoma.''\par \par \uc2 ''We will not be deterred from providing compassionate health care to our patients -- many of whom are our neighbors, colleagues, and family,'' Ms. Tong said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights rally Tuesday at the Capitol in Oklahoma City. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAH PHIPPS/THE OKLAHOMAN, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220406ei460004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
