\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Women as Victims or Criminals? A Shift in Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Tactics\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mary Ziegler \par \uc2 1,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With Roe v. Wade on thin ice, state legislatures are producing a wave of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, some of them truly eye-popping. Missouri alone has in recent weeks tried to limit out-of-state travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , proposed treating the delivery or shipment of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as drug trafficking, and moved to make it a felony to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event of an ectopic pregnancy (in which a fertilized egg implants outside the womb), a condition that can be life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 In the past, many extreme bills like these would have captured the public's attention and then quickly disappeared, swept under the rug by lawmakers and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents who had easier-to-enact plans for dismantling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The past year has been different, in a few ways. For one, some fringe bills are actually going into effect. S.B. 8, the Texas law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy and allows people to sue those who ''aid or abet'' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for at least $10,000, started wreaking havoc in the state in September. A few years ago, S.B. 8-style bills were not on anyone's radar. Now, they're spreading, with Idaho passing its own version in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Not every bill being introduced will succeed; but those that fail will matter, in part by influencing the national conversation about women who have abortions. That's another way things have changed in recent months: Some of this new crop of bills reflect a change in how the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement portrays such women, depicting them as serious criminals rather than innocent victims of the ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry,'' as had been common in the past. That rhetorical and legal shift sends a powerful message about who is likely to face criminal punishment when Roe is decimated, as it is expected to be this summer.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  argument that women seeking abortions are victims who need to be protected gained currency in the 1990s amid a wave of clinic blockades, bombings and the murder of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement had an image problem. Even internal polls conducted by the movement suggested that many Americans believed it to be not only violent but anti-woman.\par \par \uc2 The movement had a constitutional problem, too. In 1992, the Supreme Court, in Planned Parenthood v. Casey, defied expectations by refusing to reverse Roe v. Wade in its entirety. The court had saved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights partly because a majority of the justices believed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access had allowed women to lead more equal lives. As a result, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups concluded that Roe would never be overturned unless the majority of Supreme Court justices no longer believed that women needed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be equal. The simplest solution, as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders saw it, was to prove that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually harmed the women it was supposed to help.\par \par \uc2 So the group \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  pioneered a new strategy, seeking to ''shatter the myth that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps women'' and establish that women were victims of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 1997 Dr. \uc2 John Willke\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  obstetrician, published an updated version of his pre-Roe ''Handbook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' renaming it ''Why Can't We Love Them Both?'' -- referring to fetuses and women who might abort them. In 1991, the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , the nation's largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, chose as its president \uc2 Wanda Franz\uc2 , a psychology professor who had started an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  research organization to study the ''psychological problems suffered by some women after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Woman-protective arguments underwrote dozens of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that were passed around the country over the past decade. Contested claims that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cause regret or even post-traumatic stress appeared in major Supreme Court opinions, such as Gonzales v. Carhart in 2007, and in mandated counseling laws in conservative states.\par \par \uc2 Yet in December, when the Supreme Court heard oral arguments in the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case in decades, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, rationalizations about protecting women from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were sidelined. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and lawyers for the State of Mississippi stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was simply unnecessary: Women had achieved more equal citizenship, but not thanks to widespread \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Just look at the newest Supreme Court justice, Amy Coney Barrett: She's enjoyed tremendous professional success and had a large family. Why can't everyone else do the same?\par \par \uc2 There were always going to be practical reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes to stop portraying individual women as victims. For one thing, states have already prosecuted women for conduct during pregnancy. But unless states do more to punish those who can become pregnant, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws will become difficult to enforce: If you ban the procedure, women will travel out of state or order pills online. So you have to stop them from doing those things, too. And individual women will be easier for conservative states to target than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or pill distributors in other states or countries.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states have a decision to make: If they want to strongly enforce criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, they will probably have to go after women. Missouri's recent bills suggest which way that decision will go.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2  and visiting professor at Harvard Law School, is the author of the forthcoming book ''Dollars for Life: The Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movement and the Fall of the Republican Establishment.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2  and visiting professor at Harvard Law School, is the author of the forthcoming book ''Dollars for Life: The Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movement and the Fall of the Republican Establishment.''\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Illustration by The New York Times; photographs by Scott Olsen and Douglas Sacha, via Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220321ei3l0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
