\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Argument on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  That Changed Everything in Colombia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catalina Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez Coral \par \uc2 968 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BOGOT\u193\'00\'C1, Colombia -- For decades \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists in Latin America looked to our counterparts in the global north to learn the best litigation and advocacy tools. We considered the incremental gains made in the years leading up to Roe v. Wade in the United States a blueprint for victory in our fight.\par \par \uc2 But as a feminist green wave, referring to the green bandannas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters wear, sweeps across the region, this summer the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  could roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Inspiration is now coming from the south rather than the north, thanks to the coordinated efforts of many Latin American activists.\par \par \uc2 Colombia had a blanket ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 2006, when the country's constitutional court mandated that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be legally accessible when a woman's health and life were at risk, a fetus had serious health problems or when a pregnancy resulted from rape. But some women faced barriers to accessing these legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, including onerous medical requirements to prove they qualify. Others who had abortions -- or who helped a woman obtain one -- could be sentenced to up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Last September, a lawsuit asking the Constitutional Court of Colombia to decriminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was filed by the Causa Justa -- or Just Cause -- movement, a coalition of which the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a part. We argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is essential health care that should not be regulated in the penal system. The court also asked Congress to create regulations to apply the ruling. In a transformative shift for the majority-Catholic country, we are now the third country in Latin America to decriminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last year, behind Mexico and Argentina.\par \par \uc2 The landmark ruling was issued as my colleagues in the United States are defending the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a case that could overturn a half-century of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . My colleagues in Europe joined in filing amicus briefs in cases before the \uc2 European Court of Human Rights\uc2  highlighting the harms of Poland's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A decade ago, few could have imagined that Latin America, which is home to some of the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the world, would become a benchmark for advances in sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Women across the globe are connected by our shared struggle to overcome the discrimination we experience simply because of our gender. The hard-fought gains in Latin America are the result of decades of organizing, mobilizing, working to shift the conversation around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, most importantly, working together to bring about change.\par \par \uc2 In Colombia, Causa Justa filed a lawsuit signed by the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , La Mesa por La Vida y La Salud de las Mujeres, Women's Link Worldwide, Cat\u243\'00\'F3licas por el Derecho a Decidir and Grupo M\u233\'00\'E9dico por el Derecho a Decidir on behalf of 90 other organizations and more than 130 activists. Teamwork is just one of the strategies that buttressed the fight for our fundamental rights, with feminists across the region tailoring their approach to adapt to local challenges.\par \par \uc2 But it wasn't enough to change our laws. How people thought and talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to evolve, too. The movement sought to socially decriminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We fought diligently to destigmatize and combat misinformation about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We took to the streets and social media with our messages. Advocates and celebrities came out on social media and in public events to support a woman's right to choose. In one video, artists, actors, activists and even religious leaders explained that women who seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut across social, political and religious lines.\par \par \uc2 Having local organizations take the lead was crucial. In Colombia, we wanted to understand what obstacles women faced in accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and who was criminalized. Mesa por la Vida y la Salud de las Mujeres, an organization that's part of the Causa Justa Movement, fanned out across the country to talk to women and document what was happening.\par \par \uc2 Researchers found that the majority of women who faced legal consequences for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or for helping someone get one were low-income and lived in rural and remote areas. They also found that women living in those areas were more likely to have complications from unsafe abortions. Not only was this information critical to the lawsuit -- it was also critical to changing the conversation about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 Our fight is far from over. In countries like El Salvador and Honduras, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still prohibited in all circumstances and penalized. Colombian activists remain united to ensure that the decision of the court is implemented. But as some of the largest countries in the region take a more progressive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is reason to be optimistic.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  rolls back the constitutional protection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, the move would reverberate around the world. Latin American women will continue building knowledge and working across borders in the fight to guarantee everyone's rights. Perhaps our approach can be useful to our sisters in the global north, to which we have looked for so many years for lessons.\par \par \uc2 Catalina Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez Coral (@catamartinezc) is the senior regional director of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America and the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 -rights posters outside of Colombia's Constitutional Court last month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Fernando Vergara/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220317ei3h00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
