\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Guatemala Withdraws  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bill After Protests\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jody Garc\u237\'00\'EDa and Natalie Kitroeff \par \uc2 818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Alejandro Giammattei\uc2  said he would veto the bill days after his allies pushed it through the legislature, a move that surprised analysts, human rights activists and officials.\par \par \uc2 GUATEMALA CITY -- Guatemala's congress on Tuesday shelved a bill that would have imposed up to 10 years of jail time for women who obtained abortions, an abrupt reversal that follows days of protests, legal challenges and widespread disapproval of the measure, including from a prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  also expressed serious concerns about enacting the legislation, which also banned same-sex marriage, in back-channel conversations with the Guatemalan government, according to two American officials who spoke on condition of anonymity to discuss sensitive policy matters.\par \par \uc2 The country's president, \uc2 Alejandro Giammattei\uc2 , said he would veto the bill days after his allies pushed it through the legislature, a move that surprised analysts, human rights activists and Guatemalan officials.\par \par \uc2 On March 9, the day after the bill passed, Mr. Giammattei attended a ceremony declaring Guatemala the ''pro-life capital'' of Latin America, where he said in a speech that he believed ''in respect for life from the moment of conception.''\par \par \uc2 But by Thursday, the president was distancing himself from the bill. In a short video address, Mr. Giammattei said the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceremony should not be linked to the legislature's approval of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, which he said ''suffers from technical deficiencies'' and ''violates the constitution of the republic.''\par \par \uc2 Officials and human rights activists say the reversal was probably prompted by the immediate backlash to the approval of the bill at home and abroad, which made signing it politically risky for Guatemala's profoundly unpopular president.\par \par \uc2 The bill, approved by the legislature last week, on International Women's Day, would have imposed among the harshest penalties for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of any country in Latin America and likely would have blocked any movement on L.G.B.T.Q. rights. It would have prohibited teaching students about sexual diversity or that gay or lesbian sex is ''normal,'' and barred schools from discussing L.G.B.T.Q. issues with children.\par \par \uc2 ''We expected the president to align himself with the law,'' said Regina Tam\u233\'00\'E9s, the deputy director of the women's rights division at \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 . ''He's a president that has done little to nothing to protect human rights in general, and much less those of women and L.G.B.T. people.''\par \par \uc2 In a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already illegal in every case except when a woman's life is at risk, the move to further restrict access to the procedure was immediately met with outrage by human rights groups.\par \par \uc2 In demonstrations that gathered momentum over several days, women, L.G.B.T.Q. activists and university students gathered in front of Congress to protest the bill. On Saturday, hundreds marched through the capital, with a small group of protesters setting fire to a Guatemalan flag outside the National Palace and chanting that the bill ''does not represent me.''\par \par \uc2 Even the Family Matters Association, one of the largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations in Guatemala, came out against the measure, urging Mr. Giammattei to consider the danger of ratifying a law that ''could put fundamental rights and guarantees at risk.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Giammattei has seen his approval ratings sink since taking office in 2020, surveys show. Nearly two-thirds of respondents in a recent CID-Gallup survey said his performance was ''bad or very bad'' -- the worst rating of the last 10 presidents in Guatemala, according to the analysis.\par \par \uc2 The Guatemalan leader has been implicated in two corruption cases, and his attorney general has ratcheted up tension with the Biden administration by targeting judges and former prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 ''The president needed to do something to reduce the demonstrations, lest they get out of control,'' said Congresswoman Lucrecia Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Mack, who voted against the law. ''He was probably scared that this was going to get louder because it generated a lot of outrage on social media and in the streets.''\par \par \uc2 A group of lawyers filed a legal challenge to the bill with the country's constitutional court, and opposition lawmakers sent formal objections to the president of Congress. The lawmakers pointed out particular problems with part of the measure that requires a physician to get the approval of another doctor before performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 Guatemala has about one doctor for every 1,000 people, the members of Congress said, which would make it extremely difficult for women at risk of maternal death to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the law.\par \par \uc2 A demonstration last week in Guatemala City against a bill that would bolster the prison sentence for women who obtain abortions and ban same-sex marriages. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Esteban Biba/EPA, via Shutterstock FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220316ei3g0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
