\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Idaho Becomes First State to Pass an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Based on Texas' Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The bill prohibits abortions after about six weeks, and allows family members of a ''preborn child'' to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Idaho on Monday became the first state to adopt a copycat of an unusual new Texas law that relies on ordinary citizens to enforce a ban on abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy as a way of getting around court challenges to its constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho House, led by Republicans, approved the bill, 51-14, and sent it to Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 . Mr. Little, a Republican, has already signed a separate law restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that passed last year.\par \par \uc2 The bill was the latest display of confidence from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and lawmakers across the country. Both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate anticipate that by summer, the Supreme Court could pare back or overturn Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under Roe, states cannot ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus is viable outside the womb, which with modern medical technology is about 23 weeks of pregnancy. But the court's six conservative justices appeared willing to abandon that decision when they heard oral arguments in December about a Mississippi law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The court has also declined to intervene to stop the Texas law, which took effect in September. On Friday, the Texas Supreme Court said it was unable to stop the ban because the law explicitly prohibits the state officials who were sued from enforcing it.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the sponsors of the Idaho bill said they were encouraged by those decisions.\par \par \uc2 ''Texas' clever, private course of action did good,'' said Representative Steven Harris, the bill's co-sponsor in the Idaho House. ''It stopped physical abortions, chemical abortions in their tracks.''\par \par \uc2 To those who objected that the bill was unconstitutional, Mr. Harris noted that ''Texas has already made two visits to the Supreme Court.'' And, he added, ''abortions are still being stopped in Texas.''\par \par \uc2 The Idaho bill differs from the Texas law in some key respects.\par \par \uc2 Texas allows any civilian to sue anyone, whether a ride-share driver or a doctor, who ''aids or abets'' a woman in getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks -- before many women are aware they are pregnant. It provides $10,000 plus legal fees for successful suits. The Idaho bill, similarly titled a ''heartbeat bill,'' allows family members of what the legislation calls ''a preborn child'' to sue the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, and establishes a reward of at least $20,000, plus legal fees. It allows lawsuits against providers for up to four years after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, considered the strictest in the nation, allows no exceptions for women who are victims of rape or incest. The Idaho bill provides an exception but requires women to file a police report and show it to the provider before they can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Neither state would prosecute women who have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last year, shortly before Texas passed its law, Idaho had adopted a law making it a crime to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity was detected. But recognizing that the law was probably unconstitutional, lawmakers attached a trigger, saying it would not take effect until an appellate court upheld a similar law in another state.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's attorney general, a Republican, had warned in an opinion this month that the new bill with civilian enforcement, too, would ''likely be found to violate recognized constitutional rights.'' He said it could also violate the separation of powers outlined in the Idaho Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The governor opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and signed the trigger law passed last year. If he signs the new bill allowing civilian enforcement, it would take effect 30 days later.\par \par \uc2 The bill identifies those who may sue as the mother, grandparents, siblings, aunts and uncles of the ''preborn child,'' as well as the father. It does not allow rapists to sue after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed.\par \par \uc2 Representative Lauren Necochea, a Democrat who opposed the bill, asked before Monday's vote whether the siblings of a rapist could sue the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Harris, the co-sponsor, indicated they could.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Necochea said the exceptions for rape and incest were ''not meaningful.''\par \par \uc2 ''This bill is not clever, it's absurd,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Data shows that abortions in Texas have dropped 60 percent since its law took effect in September. Some clinics in neighboring states have seen an 800 percent increase in demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as women cross state borders for the procedure. One of those states, Oklahoma, is considering its own six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 ''It is appalling that anyone could look at the chaos and harm in Texas over the past six months and think, 'I want that for the people in my state,''' said \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  said it would lobby the governor not to sign the bill.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho House passed the \uc2  bill, 51-14, and then sent it to Gov. Brad Little. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Darin Oswald/Idaho Statesman, via Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220315ei3f00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
