\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 OpEd; SECT\par \b \uc2 States Want to Ban Abortions Beyond Their Borders. Here's What Pro-Choice States Can Do.\b0\par\par\uc2 By David S. Cohen, Greer Donley and Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 \par \uc2 1,217 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A recently introduced Missouri provision would allow private citizens to sue anyone who helps a Missouri resident get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in another state.\par \par \uc2 The provision is part of a wave of state anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, some of it quite radical, that's being considered in the months ahead of the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization -- the case that's expected to severely compromise, if not entirely jettison, the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Roe v. Wade. The result of such an outcome would be that about half the states in the country would ban nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 What's alarming about the Missouri bill, and others like it, is that it suggests a new tactic in the coming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars: Some states will go beyond banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within their borders. They will try to impose their policy preferences on other states, in an attempt to stop their citizens from getting abortions anywhere at all. (A bill proposed last year would have applied Missouri law to many out-of-state abortions -- even when the patient's only connection to the state was having sex that resulted in a pregnancy there.)\par \par \uc2 Given that the federal government has so far failed to act decisively on this issue, it will be up to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states to determine the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and access. Some such states have protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their statutes or constitutions, and more have announced their intention to do so. But as the Missouri bill shows, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states must go further than keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal within their state lines.\par \par \uc2 A suite of bills introduced in California has begun to chart a path to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients. But consider these additional steps that states can take. States can pass laws and regulations that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who offer services to out-of-state patients. All states have statutes that require their civil and criminal courts to assist in another state's depositions, subpoenas and legal processes. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states could amend these laws; such states could prohibit their courts from cooperating with out-of-state civil and criminal cases that stem from abortions that took place legally within their borders.\par \par \uc2 To further protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, states could block their law enforcement agencies from cooperating with out-of-state investigations related to the provision of otherwise lawful abortions. And they could change their extradition laws to refuse to extradite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers as long as they are not fleeing justice.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers also will need protection from threats to their medical licenses and insurance status. States where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal can instruct their medical boards and in-state malpractice insurance companies to abstain from taking any adverse action against providers who give out-of-state patients abortions that are legal in the provider's state. This should apply to both patients that providers treat remotely with medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to patients who physically travel to their state.\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that these actions could threaten basic principles of cooperation and comity that are required for a country of 50 states to function as a united whole. But these principles already have been frayed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states like Missouri attempting to legislate outside their borders.\par \par \uc2 States can also take stock of their existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and remove any that unnecessarily impede access. For instance, many liberal states require minors to involve their parents in their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, and a few permit only doctors to provide abortions (even though it is safe for physicians assistants or nurse practitioners to offer such care). Also, most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states have needless reporting requirements that could be removed and gestational limits that could be extended -- or at least waived for exceptions, like maternal health or severe fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 Removing unnecessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  barriers, a suggestion pushed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates for years, takes on added importance now. In a post-Roe country, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states will see a dramatic increase in patients from places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. Already, the number of Texans traveling for abortions after S.B. 8, Texas' ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, has strained capacity and delayed care from Kansas to Minnesota. Increasing the number of providers, reducing administrative burdens and streamlining care will help clinics serve this influx of patients.\par \par \uc2 Pooling the services of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across state lines is another way to help with what will surely become an overburdened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ecosystem -- especially in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states that neighbor anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states. During the pandemic, many states enacted temporary measures that allowed out-of-state medical care professionals in good standing to treat patients in their states via telehealth. States could make the same allowance specifically for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 States seeking to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights need to think seriously about financing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. The legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is irrelevant to people who cannot afford it. More state Medicaid plans can cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and states can improve their reimbursement rate so that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are fairly paid for the services they provide, a necessary precondition for remaining open to serve patients from all over the country.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is already marked by race and class disparities, which will grow significantly when out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients must travel hundreds, if not thousands, of miles to find care. State and local governments could help by directly subsidizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics or supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds that assist with patient costs. After all, for decades, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states have financed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancies centers.\par \par \uc2 Finally, states should enact versions of so-called Safe at Home laws, like those in New Jersey and California, which protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' home addresses from public discovery. In a post-Roe future, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism is likely to rise, putting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers operating in safe states at risk of being personally targeted.\par \par \uc2 State legislators who support \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and justice need to take steps to ensure that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is not dependent on ZIP code and financial status and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are protected. Without these concerted actions from supportive legislators, the post-Roe reality will be much worse than it has to be.\par \par \uc2 David S. Cohen is a professor of law at Drexel University's Kline School of Law and a co-author of ''Obstacle Course: The Everyday Struggle to Get an \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America.'' Greer Donley is an assistant professor of law at the University of Pittsburgh Law School, where her scholarship focuses on reproductive justice, bioethics and F.D.A. law. Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 is a professor of law and the interim dean of Temple University's Beasley School of Law, where her scholarship focuses on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , family law and public health.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Shawn Thew/EPA, via Shutterstock FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220313ei3d00061\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
