\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Court Ends Challenge to Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike and Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling says state officials have no authority to enforce the law, which empowers private citizens: ''We cannot rewrite the statute.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court on Friday effectively shut down a federal challenge to the state's novel and controversial ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, closing off what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates said was their last, narrow path to blocking the new law.\par \par \uc2 The decision was the latest in a line of blows to the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has prevailed for five decades.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, which several states are attempting to copy, puts enforcement in the hands of civilians. It offers the prospect of $10,000 rewards for successful lawsuits against anyone -- from an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver to a doctor -- who ''aids or abets'' a woman who gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once fetal cardiac activity can be detected.\par \par \uc2 It is the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the nation, and flies in the face of the Supreme Court's landmark 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade, which prohibits states from banning the procedure before a fetus is viable outside the womb, which is currently about 23 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 By empowering everyday people and expressly banning enforcement by state officials, the law, known as S.B. 8, was designed to escape judicial review in federal court. Advocates of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had asked the Supreme Court to block it even before it took effect last September. The justices repeatedly declined, and said that because state officials were not responsible for enforcing the law it could not be challenged in federal court based on the constitutional protections established by Roe.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court left open the smallest of windows, saying in December that opponents of the law could file suit against Texas medical licensing officials, who might discipline \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who violate the law.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the justices of the Texas Supreme Court, all Republicans, said that those officials did not, in fact, have any power to enforce the law, ''either directly or indirectly,'' and so could not be sued.\par \par \uc2 The justices said the law had effectively tied their hands. They agreed that the state's licensing officials had the authority to discipline providers for violating other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. ''But we conclude that the Heartbeat Act expressly provides otherwise,'' the court said, using the title of S.B. 8.\par \par \uc2 ''The act's emphatic, unambiguous and repeated provisions'' declare that a private civil action is the ''exclusive'' method for enforcing the law, the justices wrote. They added, ''These provisions deprive the state-agency executives of any authority they might otherwise have to enforce the requirements through a disciplinary action.''\par \par \uc2 ''We cannot rewrite the statute,'' the justices wrote.\par \par \uc2 ''With this ruling, the sliver of this case that we were left with is gone,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas' attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , declared it a ''major victory.''\par \par \uc2 ''This measure, which has saved thousands of unborn babies, remains fully in effect, and the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plaintiffs' lawsuit against the state is essentially finished,'' he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and legal scholars said the Texas law would encourage other states not only to pass similar bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but to attempt to nullify other precedents they oppose.\par \par \uc2 The law allows no exceptions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in the case of women who have been raped or are victims of incest. It has thrown Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers into crisis, and similar legislation is pending around the country.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is considering a Mississippi law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, and in oral arguments in December, the six conservative justices on the court appeared inclined to uphold that law.\par \par \uc2 Several justices indicated that they would vote to overturn Roe v. Wade outright, as Mississippi has asked. And lawyers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups argued that even if the court only upholds the Mississippi law, it would effectively overturn Roe because of its central holding on viability.\par \par \uc2 Already, state legislatures are advancing bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as if Roe were overturned. Some have passed outright bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are to take effect immediately if the court rules to overturn Roe even ''in part,'' and others have prepared to ban the procedure at six, 10, 12 and 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said the court's decision on Friday would further embolden states to enact aggressive measures to restrict abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''The combination of the U.S. Supreme Court and Texas Supreme Court rulings on this unique law means that other states are going to see this as a way to insulate their own laws from judicial review,'' said David S. Cohen, a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The innovations of the Texas law -- its civilian enforcement and bounty system -- could be adapted to shield other kinds of laws, including ones making it a crime to travel to another state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs in the mail, said Mary Ziegler, a law professor and historian at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2 . Tennessee lawmakers have proposed a bill allowing civilian enforcement of a ban on the delivery of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 ''If conservative states want to do things that may not look constitutional even to this Supreme Court, they can use a bounty system to achieve that,'' Professor Ziegler said. ''The message sent by the Texas litigation was that if you have concerns that you might lose a constitutional challenge, that shouldn't hold you back. Because you can use this road map to keep the case out of federal court entirely.''\par \par \uc2 Kimberlyn Schwartz, a spokeswoman for Texas Right to Life, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents believed they were seeing real gains after decades of chipping away at the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We've known that this lawsuit all along was just invalid and should have been dismissed, and now the fact that we're on that trajectory now is encouraging,'' Ms. Schwartz said, adding that the movement ''is not going to let our foot off the gas yet.''\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the chief executive of Whole Woman's Health, the clinic that sued to stop S.B. 8, said ''the courts have failed us.''\par \par \uc2 ''This ban does not change the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, it just blocks people from accessing the care they need,'' she said. ''The situation is becoming increasingly dire,'' she said, as the surrounding states pass their own restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Data released in February shows that the Texas law cut the number of abortions in the state by 60 percent. Planned Parenthood clinics in neighboring states have reported an 800 percent increase in women seeking abortions. But that avenue, too, is likely to close soon.\par \par \uc2 Many women have traveled to Oklahoma for the procedure, but this week the State Senate passed its own six-week ban modeled on the Texas law. The Idaho Senate passed a similar law last week. Lawmakers in other states have proposed similar bans, but have held off in hopes that the Supreme Court decision, expected in June, will allow them to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely.\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Several states are trying to copy Texas, which allows civilians to sue anyone who ''aids or abets'' a woman who gets an \uc2  once fetal cardiac activity can be detected.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ilana Panich-Linsman for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220312ei3c00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
