\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Expecting Roe's Fall, States Race to Curb  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate anticipate that come July, the Supreme Court will have overturned Roe v. Wade and with it the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , handing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists a victory they have sought for five decades. But from Florida to Idaho, Republican-led state legislatures are not waiting: They are operating as if Roe has already been struck down, advancing new restrictions that aim to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal in as many circumstances as possible.\par \par \uc2 Under Roe, states cannot prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus is viable outside the womb -- around 23 weeks into pregnancy. But bills moving through legislatures are outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely, or at six, 12 or 15 weeks of gestation. On Thursday, Florida passed a 15-week ban even as opponents warned it was unconstitutional so long as Roe stands. In Oklahoma, a Senate committee approved a bill that would prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting 30 days after the ''probable'' start of a woman's last monthly period.\par \par \uc2 Some states are trying to ban or limit pills that induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had hoped would provide a safe and legal workaround. Several states have advanced laws like the one the Supreme Court allowed to take effect in Texas that puts enforcement in the hands of private citizens. Texas allows lawsuits against anyone -- from an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver to a doctor -- who knowingly ''aids or abets'' a woman getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the sixth week of pregnancy, rewarding $10,000 plus legal fees for successful suits. On Thursday, the Idaho Senate passed a bill that would award a minimum of $20,000 to family members who sue, including ''a sibling of the preborn child.''\par \par \uc2 The frenzy of activity in state legislative sessions over the past two months offers a glimpse of a post-Roe America, when the Supreme Court would return the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the states. While some states closer to the coasts -- California, Vermont, New Jersey among them -- have moved to enshrine a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , far more are trying to restrict it.\par \par \uc2 ''All gloves are off, and everybody's trying different things,'' said Sue Liebel, the state policy director for the Susan B. Anthony List, which helps elect anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers. ''It's an epitome of the American spirit and American creativity, trying to get around unsettled law. I think we'll see more of that. I think that's what states are looking for.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents were encouraged enough by the arguments before the Supreme Court in December that the general counsel for \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  told states that they ''should not hesitate to pass the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits possible.'' The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, counts at least 531 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions introduced in 40 states this year. Last year, states passed more than 100 laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most of any year since the court decided Roe in 1973.\par \par \uc2 On the newest frontier are bills to restrict access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  identified as first among the most ''pressing priorities'' for 2022 legislative sessions. In December, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  permanently lifted a requirement that the medication be obtained in person, allowing it to be delivered by mail. Guttmacher reports that more than half of all abortions in 2020 were done with pills. But many states are attempting to ban delivery of the pills, or require them to be picked up on a second or third visit.\par \par \uc2 The case before the Supreme Court, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, concerns a Mississippi law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the ''probable gestational age of the unborn human'' is more than 15 weeks. The state has asked the court not only to uphold that law but to overturn Roe v. Wade. That landmark ruling prohibits states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 The court's 1992 decision in Planned Parenthood v. Casey affirmed Roe, and said that states could not impose an ''undue burden'' on women seeking abortions before viability. For nearly two decades after Casey, state legislators who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoided passing gestational age bans anywhere short of 23 weeks. They recognized that a majority of justices supported Roe, and worried that the court might even expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Now, given the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leanings of three justices appointed by President Donald J. Trump, and the questions the justices posed, both sides believe that the court will say there is no constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That would leave supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with little legal recourse to fight the new state bans, unless Congress passed a bill establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Last week, Republicans in the Senate declined to consider such a bill.\par \par \uc2 Still, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators are taking a belt-and-suspenders approach, hoping that one or another new law will effectively outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the court rules.\par \par \uc2 Twelve states already have so-called trigger bans, which would prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the court overturned or struck the central ruling of Roe. But those states are advancing other restrictions in case the court stops short of that.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma, for example, already has a trigger ban. But the new proposal for a ban after 30 days would effectively rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given that the state requires women to wait 72 hours after consulting with a medical provider, and the average menstrual cycle is 28 days.\par \par \uc2 The same State Senate committee that passed that measure also approved three others: a Texas-style enforcement of a ban at six weeks that would take effect as soon as the governor signs it; an amendment to the State Constitution saying it does not provide a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; and an expanded trigger law that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if the Supreme Court overturns Roe ''in part.''\par \par \uc2 While Missouri and Tennessee have trigger bans, they also have cascading bans anticipating what gestational age limit the court might allow. Tennessee's ban blocks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six, eight, 10, 12, 15, 18, 20 and 22 weeks after the start of a woman's last monthly period.\par \par \uc2 ''It's like having a reserve price on \uc2 eBay\uc2 ,'' said Carrie Severino, the president of the Judicial Crisis Network, which opposes Roe. If the court says 15 weeks is an appropriate limit, but not six weeks, ''then the states say, 'Great, we have one of those.'''\par \par \uc2 Most bills have provisions to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of the mother. But even on that, states are cracking down. An Oklahoma proposal says that providers should not be allowed to terminate a woman's pregnancy ''based on a claim'' that she intends to kill herself if she cannot get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That bill also classifies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide.\par \par \uc2 And states with trigger bans are still passing restrictions that would make it harder to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the court declines to overturn Roe. An omnibus bill heading toward passage in Kentucky would require \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to individually cremate the remains of each fetus, which costs thousands of dollars. Planned Parenthood, which runs one of two remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state, has said that the expense would force it to stop providing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court could strike a compromise, upholding Mississippi's 15-week limit but stopping short of overturning Roe. Neither side considers that likely; dozens of state bans are working their way through federal courts, so the justices would soon face new cases asking what earlier time limits were allowed.\par \par \uc2 ''We'd just get dragged into another cycle of unending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  litigation,'' said Ms. Severino, of the Judicial Crisis Network. Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, too, told the justices during oral arguments that upholding the Mississippi law equates to overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Even if the court attempts some middle path, many trigger bans leave it to the governor or the state attorney general to determine whether Roe had been repealed ''in whole or in part.''\par \par \uc2 Polls show that about two-thirds of Americans do not think Roe v. Wade should be overturned. A Gallup poll in January found that two-thirds of Americans were dissatisfied with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and that most of those people said it was because the laws were too strict. It was a reversal from the previous two decades, when more Americans said they were dissatisfied because the laws were not strict enough.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say public opinion might give pause to some lawmakers who have supported restrictions in the past, now that stricter laws seem likely to take effect rather than be stymied in court. Legislatures in at least four states have rejected a six-week ban with Texas-style enforcement. Lawmakers in two of those states, South Dakota and Arkansas, argued to wait and see what happened with Roe. Florida legislators pursued the 15-week ban instead, casting it as a compromise, and Arizona is expected to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 A demonstration last December for \uc2  rights. Both sides believe the Supreme Court is likely to overturn Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The case before the court involves the Jackson Women's Health Organization, Mississippi's only clinic offering the procedure. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR/THE NEW YORK TIMES); Oklahoma City's Trust Women clinic. States are seeking to ban abortions at ever earlier periods. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220308ei380003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgo