\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ruling Lets Official Defend  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law in Kentucky\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After the state's political landscape shifted in 2019, state officials of different parties disagreed about who should represent the state in the case.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruled on Thursday that Kentucky's attorney general, a Republican, may step in to defend a state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law struck down by a federal appeals court after the governor, a Democrat, refused to pursue further appeals.\par \par \uc2 The question in the case was procedural, and the court did not address the constitutionality of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. The court has in recent months allowed a Texas law banning most abortions after six weeks to go into effect and heard arguments in a case from Mississippi about that state's 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Questions from the conservative justices in the Mississippi case suggested that they may be preparing to overrule Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thursday's decision, in Cameron v. EMW Women's Surgical Center, No. 20-601, concerned a Kentucky law that challengers said effectively banned the most common method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second trimester of pregnancy, dilation and evacuation.\par \par \uc2 The case started in 2018, when the state's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and two doctors sued various state officials to challenge the law. The state's attorney general at the time, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democrat, said his office was not responsible for enforcing the law and entered into a stipulation dismissing the case against him, agreeing to abide by the final judgment and reserving the right to appeal.\par \par \uc2 The state's health secretary, who had been appointed by a Republican governor, defended the law in court. A federal trial court struck the law down, saying it was at odds with Supreme Court precedent. The health secretary appealed, but the attorney general did not.\par \par \uc2 While the case was moving forward, Kentucky's political landscape shifted. Mr. Beshear, who had been attorney general, was elected governor. \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , a Republican, was elected attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear appointed a new health secretary, Eric Friedlander, who continued to defend the law on appeal. But after a divided three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, in Cincinnati, affirmed the trial judge's ruling, Mr. Friedlander declined to seek review from the full appeals court or the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cameron, the new attorney general, sought to intervene in the appeals court, saying he was entitled to defend the law. The appeals court denied his request, ruling that it had come too late.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., writing for the majority, said the appeals court had erred.\par \par \uc2 ''A state's opportunity to defend its laws in federal court should not be lightly cut off,'' he wrote. ''Respect for state sovereignty must also take into account the authority of a state to structure its executive branch in a way that empowers multiple officials to defend its sovereign interests in federal court.''\par \par \uc2 Only Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  dissented. ''I fear today's decision will open the floodgates for government officials to evade the consequences of litigation decisions made by their predecessors of different political parties,'' she wrote, ''undermining finality and upsetting the settled expectations of courts, litigants, and the public alike.''\par \par \uc2 In an argument last week in a case presenting a similar issue, Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , apparently forgetting that the court had not yet issued its ruling in the Kentucky case, appeared to disclose how it would come out.\par \par \uc2 ''Pretty similar to what we just allowed in that case of the attorney general,'' he said. ''You know, it was a different party. What was it, Kentucky?''\par \par \uc2 Daniel Cameron, the attorney general of Kentucky, in front of the Supreme Court last year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALEX WONG/GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220304ei3400044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
