\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Abbott and O'Rourke Win Their Texas Primaries, Avoiding Runoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Goldmacher and J. David Goodman \par \uc2 1,723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the Texas race for governor, Gov. Greg Abbott overcame several opponents on his right, and \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  sailed to the Democratic nomination.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas fended off two right-wing primary challengers on Tuesday, avoiding a runoff by a wide margin but still falling well short of the 90 percent of the vote he marshaled in the primary four years ago as a restive Republican base continues to push the party farther to the right.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abbott, who is running for a third term, left little to chance, spending $15 million in the last month alone to squash any primary threat. He will now face \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 , the former congressman who formally won the Democratic nomination, in the fall.\par \par \uc2 The results from the Texas primaries are providing the first pieces of the 2022 midterm puzzle: both an early indication of the strength of the two parties' ideological factions and the intensity of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's continued hold on the Republican electorate.\par \par \uc2 In South Texas, a fierce effort by progressives to defeat one of the last anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrats remaining in Congress, Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , was headed to a runoff. The race was a rematch from two years ago but this time the challenger, Jessica Cisneros, received a political gift when the F.B.I. raided Mr. Cuellar's home late in the race.\par \par \uc2 In the governor's race, Mr. Abbott faced backlash from the Republican base despite overseeing a sharp push to the right in state government over the last year that intensified in the campaign's closing days, including telling state agencies to investigate treatment for transgender adolescents as ''child abuse.'' The governor has been criticized by some on the right for his handling of the pandemic and the border and drew two notable challengers: Allen West, a former state party chairman, and Don Huffines, a former state senator, neither of whom ever gained significant traction.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Abbott faced boos at a Trump rally north of Houston in January and only won over the crowd by invoking Mr. Trump more than two dozen times in a six-minute speech.\par \par \uc2 Statewide, Mr. Trump has endorsed more than two dozen candidates, including Mr. Abbott, though most were expected to win before earning his backing. Texas has a two-step primary system: Any candidate who finishes below 50 percent will face off against the No. 2 vote-getter in a May runoff.\par \par \uc2 ''Big night in Texas!'' Mr. Trump said in a statement Tuesday. ''All 33 candidates that were Trump endorsed have either won their primary election or are substantially leading.''\par \par \uc2 One of the most intense races was the Republican primary for Texas attorney general, where the incumbent, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , has attracted the attention of federal investigators after some of his own top aides accused him of corruption.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton failed to avoid a runoff, even though he spent heavily to promote Mr. Trump's endorsement, including $1.8 million on one television ad that opened with uninterrupted audio of the former president praising him. Mr. Paxton's runoff opponent in May will be one of the scions of the Bush dynasty, \uc2 George P. Bush\uc2 , the state land commissioner.\par \par \uc2 The full picture of the 2022 landscape will be revealed through a series of state-by-state primaries held over the next six months, as polls suggest President Biden, who delivered his State of the Union address on Tuesday, and the Democrats face an increasingly challenging political environment.\par \par \uc2 But the Texas contests offered almost a sneak peek of the coming dynamics nationwide, including how strict new voting rules played out and the salience of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after a state law last year effectively banned most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. Later this year, a ruling from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is expected in a Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which could affect procedures in multiple states. Yet no Texas Democratic congressional candidate aired an ad focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to data from Ad Impact, an ad tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 After redistricting, Texas lawmakers erased nearly all the House seats that were competitive in the general election from the map in 2022, magnifying the importance of a handful of contested primaries in both parties. Republicans, in particular, are hoping to build on the dramatic gains the party made in South Texas and the Rio Grande Valley, particularly among working-class Latino voters in 2020, in the state's lone open, tossup seat.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, Republicans are energized by the chance to take back both the House, which the Democrats control by a historically narrow margin, and the Senate, which is equally divided with only Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's tiebreaking vote giving control to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's sagging approval ratings -- not just in Texas but even in Democratic strongholds like California -- and the lingering cloud of the coronavirus on life, the economy and schools have emboldened many Republican voters, candidates and strategists.\par \par \uc2 Despite the hostile national climate, Democrats have scored some notable recruiting successes, including two high-profile candidates who came up just short in 2018, Mr. O'Rourke and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who is running again for governor of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Rourke has already been traveling the state and raising money at a fast clip: $3 million in the last month. But Mr. Abbott, a prolific fund-raiser, outpaced him and entered the final days before the primary with $50 million on hand, compared to $6.8 million for Mr. O'Rourke.\par \par \uc2 Texas is where Mr. Trump suffered one of his rare primary endorsement defeats last year, in a House race, and while he has issued a range of endorsements, from governor down to Tarrant County District Attorney, he has mostly backed incumbents and heavy favorites.\par \par \uc2 Bigger tests of his influence loom later in the spring and summer, in the Senate contests in North Carolina and Alabama, and in the governor's race in Georgia. In that Georgia race, Mr. Trump recruited \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , a former senator, to attempt to unseat Gov. Brian Kemp, a Republican who refused to bend to Mr. Trump's efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 The biggest test of Mr. Trump's influence in Texas was the attorney general's race, where Mr. Paxton's three challengers represented the various Republican power centers vying to be the future of the party.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bush, the son of Jeb Bush and a statewide office holder who has held himself out as the most electable conservative in the race, was locked for much of the evening in a tight race for the second-place spot in the runoff. The other two Republicans were Eva Guzman, a former state Supreme Court justice, and Representative \uc2 Louie Gohmert\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Trump ally, Mr. Gohmert received an unexpected shout-out from Mr. Trump at the rally where Mr. Abbott was booed, despite the former president's endorsement of Mr. Paxton. Mr. Gohmert also posed with Mr. Trump during a photo line, but the Trump team did not send Mr. Gohmert the picture, because they did not want him to use it in the primary, according to a person familiar with the exchange.\par \par \uc2 The race was multidimensional. Ms. Guzman swiped at Mr. Bush, whose family dynasty has been weakened even among Texas Republicans. Mr. Bush responded in kind. Mr. Paxton traded attacks with Mr. Gohmert and, in recent days, went after Ms. Guzman as well.\par \par \uc2 ''We haven't seen a primary this consequential since the 1990s,'' said Brandon Rottinghaus, a professor of political science at the \uc2 University of Houston\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton finished behind the rest of the Republican ticket in 2018, raising some fears that his renomination this year could provide a rare opening for Democrats in November. Republicans have won every statewide race in Texas since 1994.\par \par \uc2 One primary north of Houston, to replace the retiring Representative \uc2 Kevin Brady\uc2 , emerged as a proxy race for the national battle for power raging within the Republican Party. A super PAC aligned with Representative Kevin McCarthy, the minority leader, spent heavily to elect Morgan Luttrell, a Navy SEAL veteran. The activist wing of House Republicans and the political arm of the House Freedom Caucus backed Christian Collins, a former aide to Senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 ''This is primary season,'' Representative \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia said at a recent rally for Mr. Collins. ''This is where we work out our differences. This is where iron sharpens iron.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Luttrell was far ahead in the early tally but it was not clear whether he would clear the 50 percent mark to avoid a runoff.\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, two primaries that drew national attention -- including trips to Texas by Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York and Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts -- pitted the party's ideological wings against each other.\par \par \uc2 In the race for an open seat, a self-described democratic socialist and Austin city councilman, Greg Casar, defeated State Representative Eddie Rodriguez. The other primary was the rematch between Mr. Cuellar and a young progressive lawyer, Ms. Cisneros -- a race in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been an issue for the district's large number of Catholic voters.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Ocasio-Cortez declared at a rally for Mr. Casar and Ms. Cisneros that ''Texas turning blue is inevitable,'' the clip was immediately picked up by Republicans, including Mr. Abbott, and wielded as an attack.\par \par \uc2 ''She was doing the work for Republicans,'' said Matt Angle, a Democratic activist whose political action committee aims to unseat Republicans in Texas. He noted that Texas Democrats are more conservative in their views on issues like guns and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than national party leaders.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a field trip for them,'' Mr. Angle added. ''For us, it's the future of the state.''\par \par \uc2 Nick Corasaniti contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nick Corasaniti contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott, a Republican, at a ''Get Out the Vote'' campaign event last month in Houston. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BRANDON BELL/GETTY IMAGES); Beto O'Rourke, a Democrat, campaigning for Texas governor in Tyler in early February. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MONTINIQUE MONROE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); From left: Representative Louie Gohmert, Eva Guzman and George P. Bush at a forum in Midland. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TAMIR KALIFA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220303ei3200009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
