\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 More Than Half of U.S. Abortions Use Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The data, released in a report Thursday, is a sign that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become the most accessible and preferred method for terminating pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 More than half of recent abortions in the United States were carried out with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, according to preliminary data released on Thursday, a sign that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increasingly become the most accessible and preferred method for terminating pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The report, issued by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, found that in 2020, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a two-pill method authorized in the United States for pregnancies up to 10 weeks' gestation -- accounted for 54 percent of all abortions. The figure represents a substantial increase from the institute's previous report, which found that the method accounted for 39 percent of abortions in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The increase in medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is most likely the result of several factors. The method -- which is less expensive and less invasive than surgical abortions -- had already become increasingly common before the coronavirus pandemic, driven partly by restrictions from conservative states that imposed hurdles to surgical methods, especially later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 As of 2017, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which collects data by contacting every known \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in the country, nearly a third of clinics offered only medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2019, according to data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , which did not include California, Maryland and New Hampshire, pills accounted for 42 percent of all abortions -- and 54 percent of abortions that were early enough to qualify for medication because they occurred before 10 weeks' gestation.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic fueled that trend, as medical groups filed a lawsuit asking the federal government to lift the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's requirement that the first of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, mifepristone, be dispensed to patients in person at a clinic or doctor's office. Citing years of data showing that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, the medical groups said that patients faced a greater risk of being infected with the coronavirus if they had to visit clinics to obtain mifepristone and pointed out that mifepristone was the only drug that the F.D.A. required patients to get in person from a medical provider but that patients were also allowed to take at home on their own without having the provider present.\par \par \uc2 A judge granted the request that summer, allowing patients to see a physician by telemedicine and receive pills by mail, but, after a challenge by the Trump administration, the Supreme Court reinstated the restriction early last year.\par \par \uc2 Under the Biden administration, however, the F.D.A. permanently lifted the in-person requirement in December and also said that pharmacies could begin dispensing mifepristone if they met certain qualifications. The F.D.A.'s action means that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become more available to women who find it difficult to travel to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider or prefer the privacy of being able to terminate a pregnancy in their homes.\par \par \uc2 As a result, while the new report from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  is preliminary -- only reflecting information from 75 percent of the clinics and including only percentages, not raw data -- the proportion of abortions carried out with pills is expected to increase further.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 80 percent of all abortions in the C.D.C.'s 2019 data occurred before 10 weeks' gestation, suggesting that there were many more women who might choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills over an in-clinic procedure if they could.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the growing interest in medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it a focus of the highly polarized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 In 19 states, mostly in the South and the Midwest, telemedicine visits for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are banned, and so far in 2022, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  report, 16 state legislatures have introduced bills to ban or limit medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the Supreme Court now considering whether to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or even overturn the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision, which legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , experts and advocates on all sides expect medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to play an even more pivotal role in the divisive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 Medication \uc2  had already become increasingly common before the coronavirus pandemic. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220225ei2p0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
