\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 With Aid From Neighbors, Colombian Feminists Decriminalize  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Turkewitz \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the United States faces growing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , activists in Latin America are increasingly relying on one another to knock down barriers in the region.\par \par \uc2 BOGOT\u193\'00\'C1, Colombia -- Decades of grass roots organizing, with meetings in living rooms and in the streets, online and across borders, have produced a tectonic shift on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America, a historically conservative region where access to the procedure has long been severely limited.\par \par \uc2 In just over a year, Colombia has joined Mexico and Argentina in knocking down barriers to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's all the more striking in contrast to the shift taking place in the United States, the country whose Supreme Court decision guaranteeing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- Roe v. Wade -- had been a seminal spark for many activists in Latin America.\par \par \uc2 As the United States faces growing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , feminist activists in Latin America are increasingly relying on one another for legal strategy, organizing tactics and inspiration, pointing out that their counterparts to the north might have something to learn from them.\par \par \uc2 ''It's now an inspiration going south to north,'' said Catalina Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez Coral, 37, a Colombian lawyer and member of Causa Justa, the coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups that brought the case recently considered by the Colombian court. ''We are going to inspire people in the United States to defend the rights set out in Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates in Colombia prepared to make their case before the country's highest court this year, they drew their tactics from their neighbors: They learned from lawyers in Mexico, adopted a song-and-dance performance similar to one in Chile and waved the green handkerchiefs that first emerged as a symbol of the movement in Argentina.\par \par \uc2 And when the country's high court ruled in their favor on Monday, largely striking down a measure that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime, they gathered outside the courthouse to celebrate and to thank those who had helped them make the moment possible: their partners across Latin America.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has plenty of opponents in the region, including the Colombian president, \uc2 Iv\u225\'00\'E1n Duque\uc2 , a conservative who condemned the ruling on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ''I am worried that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which goes against life, will become a regular practice,'' he told local media shortly after taking office. ''And that in a machista country, people will resort to it, and for many it will become a contraceptive in place of the condom.''\par \par \uc2 But Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists insisted this week that the tactics they used to lobby for the decriminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would now be used by advocates across the region, and beyond.\par \par \uc2 ''The whole world is watching us,'' said Natalia Goyeneche, 31, one of the many women by the court's steps on Monday as the ruling was announced.\par \par \uc2 In Colombia, feminist activists' call for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights went mainstream over the last two years, as their counterparts won big in neighboring countries.\par \par \uc2 The origins of the movement, however, go back decades -- at least to 1973, when a Colombian gynecologist accepted an invitation to visit the United States just as the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruled on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The doctor, Jorge Villarreal Mej\u237\'00\'EDa, had for years been horrified by the number of women he saw sick and dying from botched abortions in Colombian hospitals. In the United States, he toured some of the country's earliest legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. And he returned to Colombia convinced that he had to replicate them.\par \par \uc2 He started a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, Ori\u233\'00\'E9ntame, in the capital, Bogot\u225\'00\'E1, in 1977. And the organization in many ways helped pave the way for this week's decision.\par \par \uc2 Despite a law criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ori\u233\'00\'E9ntame began to openly help hundreds of thousands of Colombian women who had already begun ending their pregnancies -- explaining to authorities that they were helping women who were already suffering from failed or incomplete abortions.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, Ori\u233\'00\'E9ntame was frequently targeted by authorities who accused them of breaking the law, while vandals scrawled ''assassins'' outside the office. But tensions escalated in 1994, when the police knocked down the clinic's doors, guns drawn, and took off with patients' records, beginning a legal process that lasted a decade and could have landed Dr. Villarreal in prison.\par \par \uc2 That was when Cristina Villarreal -- Dr. Villareal's daughter, who was taking over the clinic -- decided they needed to go beyond providing medical help and begin uniting with feminist activists working to change the legal landscape in which they worked.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't make sense for us to continue on two different roads,'' she recalls saying of activists and medical practitioners. ''Now is the moment.''\par \par \uc2 Soon, Ms. Villarreal and others had formed La Mesa por la Vida y la Salud de las Mujeres, a coalition whose main goal was to eliminate the law criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For Colombia, she said, ''this was totally, totally radical and revolutionary.''\par \par \uc2 But putting the issue on the public agenda was difficult, and not just because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was opposed by the powerful Catholic Church and most of the public.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a country, as you know, that has been beaten by conflict and war,'' said Ana Cristina Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez V\u233\'00\'E9lez, 53, a co-founder of the organization, adding, ''That meant that for many years ending the conflict occupied the agendas of women's groups.''\par \par \uc2 Pushing for legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through Colombia's Congress seemed a dead end. More than a half-dozen attempts had failed when, around 2004, a Colombian lawyer named M\u243\'00\'F3nica Roa approached the Mesa and proposed a new tactic.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Roa had recently returned from the United States, where she was working for a legal advocacy group called the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She would go to the country's Constitutional Court -- famously more liberal than its Congress -- and argue that the law criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  violated women's rights to life, health and reproductive autonomy.\par \par \uc2 In 2006, she achieved a major victory for the movement, when the court ruled that the country must allow abortions when a woman's health was at risk, when a fetus had serious health problems or when a pregnancy resulted from rape.\par \par \uc2 A few other Latin American countries had similar decisions on the books, but public policy and opinion weighed so strongly against the procedure that in practice few women could obtain legal abortions. And so, following the 2006 ruling, Colombian activists began to teach women in other countries to use the exceptions allowed under the law.\par \par \uc2 ''Without a doubt, we learned from the Colombians,'' said Giselle Carino, an Argentine activist with the group F\u242\'00\'F2s Feminista.\par \par \uc2 But in Colombia, prosecutors were still investigating hundreds of women a year for having abortions, while many women were still dying following illicit procedures.\par \par \uc2 Around 2016, the women devised a new strategy -- one intended to change both the legal landscape and public opinion, with a strong public awareness component.\par \par \uc2 As the Colombians worked, Argentine feminists began a campaign to push legalization through Congress. Their effort included hundreds of thousands of women marching in the streets, united by an easily identifiable symbol: a green handkerchief.\par \par \uc2 Their use of scarves was itself inspired by an earlier generation of female activists, called the \uc2 Madres de Plaza de Mayo\uc2 , who wore white kerchiefs to protest the kidnapping and killing of their children by the Argentine dictatorship.\par \par \uc2 ''Part of our strategy was: How do we change the conversation in the country, how do we put this on the public agenda?'' said Ms. Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez, one of the Colombian activists. ''And that's what we learned from the Argentines.''\par \par \uc2 In late 2019, Argentina's Congress legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and two months later, just as the pandemic was beginning, Colombia's Causa Justa coalition was born.\par \par \uc2 This time, they had campaigns on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , TikTok, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and marched across the country. They had a radio campaign targeting areas with limited connectivity, and a reggaeton song that they performed in the streets.\par \par \uc2 They called on celebrities to openly support them. Suddenly, public officials were being forced to declare a position on the topic, and green scarves began to appear on necks, backpacks and bicycles all over Bogot\u225\'00\'E1.\par \par \uc2 And in September 2020, a group of lawyers from Causa Justa -- drawing on legal lessons from Canada -- sent a petition to the Constitutional Court, arguing that in practice, the existence of a law criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  violated a woman's ''fundamental right'' to voluntary termination of pregnancy under the exceptions laid out in 2006.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, judges voted 5 to 4 in their favor, decriminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few decades, Ori\u233\'00\'E9ntame has trained thousands of medical professionals across Latin America, many of whom have replicated the clinic in their own countries. Dr. Villarreal died in 2001.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all of the activists interviewed said that Roe v. Wade had been a touchstone for them, leading them to believe that the legal system could be used to help women gain greater control over their lives.\par \par \uc2 Many now struggled to understand why the United States appeared to be moving in the opposite direction.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a battle that is never completely won,'' Ms. Villarreal advised her counterparts to the north. ''You can't let your guard down.''\par \par \uc2 Megan Janetsky contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Megan Janetsky contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of \uc2  rights celebrating on Monday in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 in front of Colombia's Constitutional Court. The movement to decriminalize \uc2  in Colombia has roots dating at least to 1973. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NATHALIA ANGARITA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220224ei2o00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngbl