\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Major Shift, Colombia Decriminalizes  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Turkewitz \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court's decision follows years of organizing by women across Latin America for greater protections and more rights, and significant shifts in the region's legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 BOGOT\u193\'00\'C1, Colombia -- Having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer a crime under Colombian law, the country's top court ruled on Monday, in a decision that paves the way for the procedure to become widely available across this historically conservative, Catholic country.\par \par \uc2 The ruling by Colombia's Constitutional Court follows years of organizing by women across Latin America for greater protections and more rights, including access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and significant shifts in the region's legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's Supreme Court decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a similar decision in September and Argentina's Congress legalized the procedure in late 2020. Colombia's decision means that three of the four most populous countries in Latin America have now opened the door to more widespread access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It also comes as the United States has been moving in the opposite direction, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions multiplying across the country, and the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  considering a case that could overrule Roe v. Wade, the 1973 ruling that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 ''This puts Colombia on the vanguard in Latin America,'' said Mariana Ardila, a Colombian lawyer with Women's Link Worldwide, part of the coalition that brought one of two cases challenging the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''This is historic.''\par \par \uc2 The court's decision decriminalizes abortions in the first 24 weeks of pregnancy, and means that any woman should be able to seek the procedure from a health professional without fear of criminal prosecution. It also sets the stage for the Colombian government to regulate the process further.\par \par \uc2 The move is part of a cultural sea change across Latin America, spurred by grass-roots feminist movements and a younger, more secular generation.\par \par \uc2 In a region historically known for its Catholic faith and social conservatism, a growing push for women's rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access gained prominence a little more than a year ago when Argentina became the largest nation in Latin America to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Soon, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters across the region, from Mexico to Paraguay, Brazil to Colombia, were wearing or brandishing green handkerchiefs -- the symbol of Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement -- to show their solidarity with women's sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The handkerchiefs became symbols of the work that women's rights lawyers and activists had been doing more quietly for years.\par \par \uc2 The Argentina decision reverberated across Latin America, showing that it was possible to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in countries with strong Catholic and evangelical Protestant beliefs and a history of patriarchal ideals.\par \par \uc2 In September, Mexico's Supreme Court ruled that it was unconstitutional to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime, helping to clear the way for the legalization of the procedure in the country of about 130 million people.\par \par \uc2 The Constitutional Court magistrates in Colombia considered over the past few months two petitions that challenged the portion of the penal code that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime.\par \par \uc2 In one, a lawyer named Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Mateo S\u225\'00\'E1nchez Molina said the measure violated rights protected by the Constitution, including the right to human dignity, freedom and equality.\par \par \uc2 In another, brought by Causa Justa, a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, lawyers argued that criminalization had cast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such a negative light that it prevented even women with a legal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from getting one.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the coalition said, the existence of the criminal penalty led health professionals to deny the procedure to women who qualified for it. In others, women avoided seeking abortions in legal health centers out of fear they would be jailed, instead seeking risky alternatives in underground clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''These barriers affect mainly women living in rural and remote areas, low-income women, adolescent girls, women and girls living in situations of armed conflict and victims of gender violence, including physical and sexual violence,'' representatives of Causa Justa wrote in a summary of their petition. Making the procedure a crime in most circumstances, they argued, was therefore unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the court decided to hear the Causa Justa case first. The second case will be heard at a later date, but the first decision cannot be reversed.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and dozens of their opponents stood outside the courthouse in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 awaiting a decision on Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, both sides have carried out dueling public campaigns to try to sway the decision, and their rallies came to symbolize the country's cultural divide on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Women in the crowd danced with green handkerchiefs over their head as the news moved through the crowd, prompting Jonathan Silva, 32, to walk away angrily. An evangelical Christian who works for Unidos Por La Vida, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, Mr. Silva said the court had overstepped its power and it was a decision that should be made by elected officials.\par \par \uc2 ''What they're decriminalizing is the killing of human beings,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Until now, abortions had been legal only in limited circumstances, laid out by a 2006 Constitutional Court decision: when a woman's health was at risk, when a fetus had serious health problems or when a pregnancy resulted from rape. Anyone else who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- or who helped a woman obtain one -- could be sentenced to 16 to 54 months in prison.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists often said that this legal landscape created a two-tier system: Wealthier women in cities could get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they knew how to use one of the exceptions laid out in the law, while poorer women with less education had limited knowledge or means to do so.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors in Colombia open about 400 cases each year against women who have abortions or people who help them, according to the attorney general's office. At least 346 people have been convicted in such cases since 2006.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all of these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related investigations have been in rural areas, involving girls as young as 11, according to researchers with Causa Justa.\par \par \uc2 Illegal abortions can be unsafe and cause about 70 deaths a year in Colombia, according to the country's health ministry.\par \par \uc2 A recent survey by the nonpartisan firm \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  found that while 82 percent of Colombian respondents supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some circumstances, just 26 percent supported it in all cases -- and the court's decision is likely to cause friction as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists, policymakers, health care providers and others determine how it should be carried out.\par \par \uc2 The decision cannot be altered by other legal bodies.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's Constitutional Court is considered by many legal experts to be more liberal than the country at large, and many recent liberal shifts, including the legalization of same-sex marriage in 2016, resulted from the court's decisions.\par \par \uc2 The court is also considered a legal trendsetter in the region, said Francisco Bernate, a law professor at the University of Rosario in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1, and the decision is likely to attract the attention of judges across Latin America.\par \par \uc2 Activists in the United States are also following the changes in the region.\par \par \uc2 ''These struggles are connected,'' said Serra Sippel, the chief global advocacy officer at Fos Feminista, an alliance of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that works around the world, including in the United States. ''We in the U.S. can really learn a lot.''\par \par \uc2 Sof\u237\'00\'EDa Villamil and Megan Janetsky contributed reporting from Bogot\u225\'00\'E1.\par \par \uc2 An anti-\uc2  rally in front of Colombia's Constitutional Court in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 on Monday. The nation is historically conservative.; Supporters in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 cheered the decision to allow abortions in the first 24 weeks of pregnancy. It used to be punishable with prison. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NATHALIA ANGARITA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220222ei2m0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
