\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 A Glimpse of Post-Roe America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mary Ziegler \par \uc2 1,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In 2019 a wave of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws swept this country -- a common enough event in the United States, where hundreds of such laws have passed during the last decade. But these grabbed the public's attention in a way many others hadn't. Georgia banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, or about two weeks after a missed menstrual period. Ohio, Mississippi, Louisiana and Kentucky did the same, while Missouri banned the procedure at eight weeks. Alabama went the furthest, banning virtually all abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 Though most of these laws were quickly blocked by the courts -- they were obviously unconstitutional under Roe v. Wade -- the backlash to their passing was intense, especially in Georgia, a major hub of film and television production. Boycotts were threatened. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2  spoke out. The actress Alyssa Milano even tried to get a ''Lysistrata''-style sex strike off the ground.\par \par \uc2 Three years later, American \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  are on an even bleaker trajectory. A Supreme Court decision that's expected to come down this summer is likely to strike down Roe v. Wade, either in deed or in word, making it possible for states with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leadership to ban the procedure altogether.\par \par \uc2 It might seem curious, then, that legislators in some conservative-leaning states are spending these months before the likely downfall of Roe working to pass less extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures than they did in 2019. Now seems like the time for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators to go for broke. The fact that some of them are pursuing a different strategy offers clues about what a post-Roe America could look like, and how that landscape could be more complex -- and less predetermined -- than some Americans had assumed.\par \par \uc2 One of this year's unmistakable trends in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation is the 15-week ban. Legislators in Arizona, Florida and West Virginia are now considering bills -- which, as the name suggests, would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, in violation of Roe. At first blush, it might seem these states are simply copying the Mississippi law that the Supreme Court seems likely to uphold this summer, in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. But why would they hold back now, rather than try to get more draconian legislation through their legislatures? Florida even considered a six-week ban in recent months but did not end up acting on it. What is going on?\par \par \uc2 The answer is that 15-week bans are a way for Republicans to test public reaction in states that are still somewhat politically contested. Arizona has two Democratic senators and went for Joe Biden in 2020. Elections in Florida have been decided by a handful of votes. Their Republican governors might worry that if they go too far, they might trigger a backlash that could threaten their hold on power. Among the political realities at play: Some battleground states have constitutions that have been interpreted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while in others a majority of people likely oppose criminalizing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen-week bans are a trial balloon to see what voters will tolerate -- and they may end up being a step toward more bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of fertilization. You can bet these lawmakers will be watching the public reaction to the Dobbs decision this summer. If the reaction is relatively muted -- as it has been, in much of the country, to Texas's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which has now been in effect for nearly six months -- they likely will keep pushing more sweeping laws, until, perhaps, they reach an absolute ban. Conversely, if enough people revolt at the destruction of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, lawmakers in states like Florida may not feel as comfortable pushing further.\par \par \uc2 This raises the possibility that, at least for a time after Roe is decimated, there could be not two Americas when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but three: one in which almost any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a crime, one in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is broadly available and one in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is heavily restricted but not altogether unavailable.\par \par \uc2 At some point that third America, if it comes to be, also will be threatened. Because after Roe is gone, leaders in the national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement will push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be banned from the moment of fertilization. The movement's ultimate goal has always been the legal recognition of fetal personhood, which would functionally ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the nation. That would never be possible through the democratic process, but the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is now betting that it might be through the Supreme Court, with its new conservative supermajority. For decades, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders have downplayed the fetal personhood goal because doing so made it easier to win public support for their cause, help Republicans get elected and reassure Supreme Court justices anxious about public backlash. But with the makeup of this court -- which will not shift once President Biden replaces the retiring Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  -- they could well get their wish.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement will want to score as many wins as possible in red and purple states, hoping that 15-week bans will eventually lead to outlawing virtually all abortions.\par \par \uc2 The recent rash of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws reflects the uncertainty of America's future. The writing may be on the wall for Roe v. Wade, but when it comes to what happens next, the public might still have a say.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2 , is the author of ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Law in America: Roe v. Wade to the Present.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220220ei2k0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
