\par\par \uc2 THE ON POLITICS NEWSLETTER\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why Democrats Can't Be Counted Out\b0\par\par\uc2 By Blake Hounshell and Leah Askarinam \par \uc2 1,772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans like their chances in November. But politics can change quickly.\par \par \uc2 A ''red wave'' is building this year -- or so we're told.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are confident that the country's sour mood will sweep them back into power in Congress, mainly because Americans are fed up with the coronavirus and inflation. They think they'll pick up 30 or so House seats and four or five seats in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 ''It's crystal clear,'' said Corry Bliss, a partner at FP1 Strategies, a consulting firm that helps Republicans. He added: ''The red wave is coming. Period. End of discussion.''\par \par \uc2 But what if that's wrong? We asked about two dozen strategists in both parties what would need to happen for Democrats to hold the House and Senate in November. And while we're not making any predictions, it's possible that Democrats could retain control of Congress. Difficult, but possible.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have 222 seats in the House, and 50 seats in the Senate. That means Republicans need to pick up just six House seats and one Senate seat to take full control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Here's what needs to happen for Democrats to pull off an upset in 2022:\par \par \uc2 Biden voters show up\par \par \uc2 Pundits often make it sound like voters are judiciously studying each party's arguments and forming conclusions. But that's not really the way American politics works. Modern elections are much more about mobilization (getting your supporters to the polls) than persuasion (convincing the other side's supporters to switch), though both matter.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by more than 7 million votes in 2020. So for Democrats, winning in 2022 means figuring out how to get as many of those people as possible to vote, even though Trump won't be on the ballot this time.\par \par \uc2 ''Their primary motivation for voting in the last election was defeating Trump,'' said Guy Cecil, chairman of Priorities USA, which on Monday announced a $30 million program of digital ads aimed at reaching what he calls ''new Biden voters'' in seven swing states.\par \par \uc2 The last two elections -- the 2018 midterms and the 2020 presidential vote -- saw the biggest turnout in history. That means there's an unusual amount of uncertainty among insiders about which voters will show up in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Regaining a sense of normalcy\par \par \uc2 Every person we spoke with agreed: This is the biggest unknown.\par \par \uc2 While voters are upset about high prices today, inflation and the coronavirus could be down to manageable levels by the summer. Several strategists say it is also essential, politically speaking, that schools are fully open in September. If all of that happens, Democrats could enter the midterms as the party that defeated Covid and brought the economy roaring back to life, or at least fight Republicans to a draw on both issues.\par \par \uc2 But the White House is well aware that it's not really in control -- the virus is.\par \par \uc2 ''The script's not written yet for the remainder of the year,'' said Representative Brad Schneider of Illinois, chair of the New Democrat Coalition, a group of House moderates.\par \par \uc2 Biden finds a winning message\par \par \uc2 For months, Democrats have fretted that the White House was too slow to recognize inflation as a political problem, and was too mired in endless congressional negotiations. That's changing.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been speaking more frequently about the issue, at the urging of moderate Democrats. ''The president is recognizing his superpower, which is empathy,'' said Representative \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , a Democrat in a swing district in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Sean McElwee, executive director of the group Data for Progress, told us that the president should embrace what he calls ''solverism'' -- basically, being seen on TV every day tackling the problems that voters care about.\par \par \uc2 After a fall characterized by damaging infighting, Democrats have been working to bring more harmony to their messages. With the State of the Union address coming up, President Biden has a chance to rally the country around his vision and the improving economic numbers. But with the fate of Build Back Better now in question, what will he talk about, exactly?\par \par \uc2 Redistricting being more or less even\par \par \uc2 Democrats feel good about the maps that have been approved so far. For now, there are only three Democrats running in House districts that Trump won in 2020, and nine Republicans in districts that Biden won.\par \par \uc2 But a few unknowns remain. The Democratic-controlled State Legislature in New York is still weighing how aggressively to redraw the state's maps. Courts have yet to render final judgments in Alabama, North Carolina, Ohio and Pennsylvania. And in Florida, Republicans are divided between \uc2 Gov. Ron DeSantis\uc2 's maps and those proposed by the State Senate.\par \par \uc2 We do know that many of the House districts that are up for grabs in November are in the suburbs, which have shifted left in recent elections. That could help Democrats. Liberal strategists point out that Republicans won't be able to benefit from the massive margins that they run up in rural areas and they also note that the seats Republicans picked up in 2020 were the easy ones.\par \par \uc2 To which Republicans counter: Look at what happened in suburban Virginia, where Glenn Youngkin pared back the party's past losses to win the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade\par \par \uc2 In that Virginia race, the Democrat, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , spent millions of dollars portraying Youngkin as an extremist on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Democrats were convinced that the issue would help them with suburban women in particular, and McAuliffe predicted that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a ''huge motivator'' for voters. His campaign ran three different ads on the subject, which collectively aired more than 1,000 times.\par \par \uc2 It didn't work.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin danced around the issue, while saying he preferred to focus on the economy, jobs and education. According to exit polls conducted by Edison Research, just 8 percent of voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mattered most to their decision, the least of five preselected topics.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come roaring back as a voting issue if the Supreme Court issues a clear repudiation of Roe v. Wade this year. Should that happen, many Democrats say it could help their candidates in Senate races, where they can highlight Republican positions that polls suggest are out of the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates go hard right\par \par \uc2 Democrats are watching Republican primary campaigns closely, clipping and saving remarks that the candidates are making that could prove hard to defend in a general election. The need to cater to Trump's hard-line base of voters has made the Republican brand toxic, they say. But that's where the consensus ends.\par \par \uc2 Endangered Democrats want to localize their races as much as possible, and prefer to talk about kitchen-table issues like jobs and the economy. Nationally, Democrats are still debating how to communicate their alarm about the state of American democracy, which can come across as either abstract to voters or simply more partisan noise.\par \par \uc2 For now, Democrats are planning to use Jan. 6 as just one of several data points to portray Republicans as extremists on a range of issues, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and climate.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think this election is going to easily fall into the traditional pattern, and it's because of the radicalization of the Republican Party,'' said Simon Rosenberg, the head of the New Democrat Network.\par \par \uc2 Trump seizes center stage\par \par \uc2 After the Virginia governor's race, Democratic strategists launched various efforts to study the lessons of that campaign. One takeaway: Talking about Trump also energizes Republicans, which makes it tricky for Democrats to make the former president a central issue in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have also found that it's not effective simply to associate a Republican candidate with Trump, as McAuliffe did in Virginia. They believe they need to indict Republican candidates directly. But there's an ongoing debate about whether Democratic candidates need to do this themselves, or have outside groups run attack ads on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 The former president has endorsed dozens of candidates who in one way or another agree with his false notion that the 2020 election was stolen. On Sunday evening, he said it outright -- claiming, falsely, that then-Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  ''could have overturned the election'' on Jan. 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats manage to hang on to their congressional majorities, Trump will be a major factor.\par \par \uc2 What to read\par \par \uc2 Trump had a greater role than previously known in plans to use his national security agencies to seize voting machines, our colleagues report.\par \par \uc2 Marc Short, who was chief of staff to former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , has testified before the congressional panel investigating the Jan. 6 riot at the U.S. Capitol, Luke Broadwater reports.\par \par \uc2 Katie Rogers reports that the White House has chosen \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2 , the former Democratic senator from Alabama, to shepherd its Supreme Court pick through the nomination process in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Filings cleanup\par \par \uc2 As our colleague Shane Goldmacher was digging on Monday through the glut of campaign disclosures covering the last quarter of 2021, he noticed updates to some very old filings.\par \par \uc2 The filings, from as far back as 2017, were from the Keeping Republican Ideas Strong Timely & Inventive PAC. That's better known as KRISTI PAC, as in \uc2 Gov. Kristi Noem\uc2  of South Dakota, the former Republican congresswoman who created the committee.\par \par \uc2 Governor Noem filed amendments to no fewer than 16 old \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  reports this week. The amendments appeared mostly minor. But what is more interesting is that she was making those at all. It is the kind of cleanup that politicians typically do when they are considering a future run for president, mindful that opposition researchers will be looking for any slip-ups to feed to the press.\par \par \uc2 The KRISTI PAC treasurer, Kevin Broghamer, simply told the F.E.C. that the PAC had ''conducted a comprehensive review and reconciliation of all financial activity since January 1, 2017.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Noem, Joe Desilets, said that Broghamer had been asked to conduct the review ''to ensure the governor's committees were wholly compliant and amend any filings as needed. Unfortunately there isn't anything else to read into with the amended filings.''\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you think we're missing? Anything you want to see more of? We'd love to hear from you. Email us at onpolitics@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Success in Virginia by Republicans in the recent voting for governor has helped to stoke talk of a ''red wave'' in the midterms. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALYSSA SCHUKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220202ei220003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\