\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law, U.S. Is Different From Most\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many rich democracies have earlier cutoffs for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- but allow it later for a variety of reasons. And around the world, it has been much more common to expand access than restrict it.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . said last month that the United States was an international outlier in allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than halfway through pregnancy. That later cutoff, he said, places the United States in the company of North Korea and China.\par \par \uc2 It's true in some ways, but not all. Few countries allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without restriction until fetal viability, the cutoff set by Roe v. Wade, which was decided 49 years ago today. Because of medical advances, that is now around 23 weeks. And only around a dozen other countries allow abortions for any reason beyond 15 weeks of pregnancy, the threshold in the Mississippi law the Supreme Court is considering, which could overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 But in many countries, women can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the gestational cutoff -- for a wide variety of reasons, like health or economic ones. In some, it can be easier to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in many parts of the U.S. Also, peer countries tend to have more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, and cover the costs of abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''Under the status quo, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is still out of reach for many in the United States,'' said Risa Kaufman, director of U.S. human rights at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which fights \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in American courts and tracks international laws. ''That is in contrast to many countries, including in Western Europe, that provide access to subsidized, fully funded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, universal health care, contraception and broader social supports.''\par \par \uc2 The United States is an outlier in another way -- by considering rolling back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The Mississippi case is a direct challenge to Roe. If the court overturns Roe, more than 20 states are expected to ban or severely limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Just three countries have tightened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws since 1994, according to the center: Poland, El Salvador and Nicaragua. In that period, 59 have expanded them. In 2018, Ireland, which had mostly banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , made it legal on request until 12 weeks. Other countries, like South Korea, have decriminalized it.\par \par \uc2 ''The U.S. trend is very much the minority trend,'' said Joanna Mishtal, an anthropologist at the \uc2 University of Central Florida\uc2  who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in Europe. She said Poland is the only European nation ''that is consistently restricting both policies and real access to services that are still lawful.''\par \par \uc2 How \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is governed around the world\par \par \uc2 Worldwide, six in 10 women of reproductive age live in countries that broadly allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sixty-six countries, home to just over a quarter of women of reproductive age, either prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or allow it only if a woman's life is danger, according to the center's data on national laws that have been formally adopted.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-three countries, representing 35 percent of women of reproductive age, allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for various reasons, including protection of a woman's physical or mental health or consideration of her social or economic circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Some countries in this group are more permissive than others. In Britain, women must have two doctors approve their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but requests are routinely granted up to 24 weeks -- and beyond that for severe health reasons. (The \uc2 National Health Service\uc2  website says: ''The decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is yours alone.'') In Bolivia, however, a woman must show a grave health risk from her pregnancy, or show it was a result of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-four countries, home to 38 percent of women, allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason for a certain number of weeks. The most common threshold is 12 weeks. A dozen or so countries besides the United States allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any restrictions or conditions after 15 weeks, the cutoff in question in the Mississippi case. They include North Korea, China, Iceland, New Zealand, Singapore, Canada and Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 ''Through the lens of comparative national law, Mississippi's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime is more permissive than in most countries,'' wrote 141 international legal scholars in a brief to the Supreme Court in support of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Some countries have more access than the law suggests ...\par \par \uc2 Some of these countries, however, allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the cutoff if the woman has a valid reason, and law scholars say that in some of them, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability is as accessible as it is in the United States.\par \par \uc2 In Germany, for example, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted on request until 12 weeks, and until 22 weeks if, in the woman's view, it is necessary for her physical or mental health or for present or future living conditions. In Denmark, which also has a 12-week cutoff, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed after that time for factors including health; the person's age, income or housing; or her interests or occupation.\par \par \uc2 Other countries also allow exceptions after 12 weeks, but they are less broad. In France and Ireland, for instance, the exceptions are to prevent serious health issues or death, or if the fetus has a severe, incurable illness.\par \par \uc2 Gestational cutoffs, even if they have exemptions, can present obstacles to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. One strong piece of evidence is the large number of women who travel to less restrictive countries in Europe for later abortions, Professor Mishtal said.\par \par \uc2 ''Gestational age limits are serious barriers,'' she said. ''Even in places that are ostensibly liberal, that limit creates a real barrier to the people who need the service beyond that limit.''\par \par \uc2 In the United States, only about 8 percent of abortions happen after 13 weeks. One reason some women have later abortions is because of barriers in the U.S. It takes time to save money for the procedure -- or to arrange transportation, child care and time off work to travel to a clinic if there is not one nearby.\par \par \uc2 ... and other countries have less\par \par \uc2 In much of Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws may be more permissive than they appear on paper. But in other parts of the world, there is less access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the law permits. In sub-Saharan Africa, most countries ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but even those that say they allow it, like South Africa, have limited access.\par \par \uc2 ''Access to safe and affordable services in government hospitals can also be limited by a scarcity of health professionals that are trained to provide these legal abortions or have limited knowledge about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law,'' said Siri Suh, a medical sociologist at \uc2 Brandeis University\uc2  who has researched \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Africa.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Italy is broadly legal and free, but nearly three-quarters of doctors there have registered as conscientious objectors, and are exempted from performing abortions for religious or moral reasons. Other doctors feel pressure from colleagues not to provide abortions, Professor Mishtal and colleagues found. ''It has a chilling effect,'' she said. ''There is an enormous discrepancy between the law and the actual access.''\par \par \uc2 How the United States stands out\par \par \uc2 In other rich democracies, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is covered by public health insurance. So are other forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, including contraception. In Ireland, for example, the cost of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fully covered, the procedure can be performed by a general practitioner, and there is a government help line on how to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or reach a nurse during recovery.\par \par \uc2 The United States prohibits federal funding of abortions in most cases. Also, abortions tend to be provided only in special clinics, often far from where women live. In peer nations, they are more likely to be offered at ordinary hospitals and medical clinics.\par \par \uc2 Most European countries have arrived at their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws through legislation involving political compromise. In the U.S., the viability threshold originated in the Supreme Court. Michael New, a research associate at the \uc2 Catholic University of America\uc2  who supports more U.S. restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said this process difference may explain why the gestational limit in U.S. law is later than in most of its peer nations.\par \par \uc2 ''A legal debate, built upon precedent, can be very different from a democratic debate or moral debate,'' he said in an email.\par \par \uc2 The United States is also unusual in having such a wide range of laws by state, a feature that will be magnified if Roe is overturned. ''In California, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is fairly similar to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United Kingdom, whereas in Texas right now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is really among the most restrictive in the world,'' said Caitlin Gerdts, a vice president at Ibis \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a research group.\par \par \uc2 Illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming easier and safer\par \par \uc2 The very nature of illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide is transforming because of the availability of pills that can end a pregnancy. Historically, countries without legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tended to have high rates of unsafe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But pills that cause abortions are safe and easy to transport. The most effective studied protocol involves two drugs. But just one, misoprostol, an ulcer drug on the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 's list of ''essential medicines,'' causes a complete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in around 80 percent of cases or more, according to recent research.\par \par \uc2 In some countries, misoprostol is available over the counter at pharmacies. Activists in South America and Africa have increasingly helped women find and use the drug, outside the official health system. Groups in Europe provide online ordering of the pills, including to women in the United States, from pharmacies outside the country.\par \par \uc2 In a post-Roe world, states might seek to make such assistance, or the use of such pills, subject to punishment. In El Salvador, which prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but has widespread use of misoprostol, women who experience miscarriages are sometimes jailed.\par \par \uc2 ''The risks have also changed,'' Ms. Gerdts said, ''from physical to legal.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  protesters during the annual March for Life in Washington on Friday. Roe v. Wade was decided 49 years ago today.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kenny Holston for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220125ei1n00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
