\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 When Abortions Were Illegal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ilana Panich-Linsman and Lauren Kelley \par \uc2 1,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The end of Roe v. Wade is coming. The Supreme Court is expected to issue a ruling in June in a case from Mississippi called Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, and that ruling will likely overturn or gut Roe -- that imperfect but critical precedent that has given Americans the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1973. Since Jan. 22, 1973, to be precise.\par \par \uc2 The week of its 49th birthday, Roe v. Wade is hobbled. Not only is it unlikely to make it to 50, but for millions of women across the country who live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is almost unobtainable even now, it might as well already be gone.\par \par \uc2 Still, Roe's demise will be shattering, and there is much work to do in preparing for it. An important part of that work is studying what came before. That's not because what comes after will be the same; today, women have better access to information about safely managing their own abortions, and it is becoming increasingly easy to get hold of pills with which they can do so. The risks for these women are often more legal than they are medical.\par \par \uc2 Rather, we must remember the struggles of Americans who had abortions before the procedure was widely available -- like the women photographed below, many of them telling their stories for the first time -- because we will need to draw from their experiences in fighting back. Yes, a fight will be needed to regain access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for millions once Roe is gone. These women have waged that fight. They have waged it with their bodies, and they have the scars to prove it.\par \par \uc2 'I was ready to die rather than tell.'\par \par \uc2 Sandra\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1968\par \par \uc2 Sandra was 19 years old when she fainted at her job at a bank in Euless, Texas. She was taken to a hospital, where she was treated for a severe infection. She recalls being asked: ''You had something that was poisoning your system -- can you help us at all on this? Because there have been several women who have come in, girls, recently, with the same problem.'' ''I don't know what you're talking about,'' Sandra replied. Knowing that her husband didn't want a child, Sandra had gone to a sleazy motel and paid a man $500 to insert a catheter into her uterus. He injected her with something that ''smelled like bathroom soap,'' she said. She never told her husband about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At the time, she didn't tell anyone.\par \par \uc2 'The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a life-altering experience. It felt very self-determining and self-affirming.'\par \par \uc2 Loretta Ross\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1970\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Ross became pregnant at age 14 as a result of incest, her parents placed her in a home for unwed mothers and encouraged her to give the baby up for adoption. Ms. Ross decided not to adopt out her son, and soon after she had him, she went to \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , where she became vice president of her class. At age 16, she became pregnant again. This time, she knew she wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By then, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was available in Washington, but she needed parental permission. ''By the time I'd gone through fighting with my mom, I was in my third trimester,'' said Ms. Ross. ''I had to have a saline \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where they inject this huge -- it felt a mile long -- needle into your stomach, and induce labor.'' She had been pregnant with twins. ''I could have been a mother of three kids at 16,'' Ms. Ross said. ''All I can do is thank God that that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was available to me in 1970. Otherwise, my story would have a vastly different ending.''\par \par \uc2 'I couldn't handle more than one child.'\par \par \uc2 Jean Emma Frances\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1968\par \par \uc2 Ms. Frances and her husband at the time had a 1-year-old, and they knew they couldn't afford to have another child. So she called a work friend who told her he had been trained by a doctor to perform abortions. She had to visit him three times before the procedure worked. ''I don't even think he dilated me. I think he just inserted the catheter,'' she said. She ended up in the hospital with an infection.\par \par \uc2 'I would never, ever want anybody to go through what I went through.'\par \par \uc2 Arla Ralston\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1970\par \par \uc2 A Methodist minister referred Ms. Ralston and her boyfriend to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Chicago, but first she confirmed the pregnancy with her family doctor in Iowa. ''He was livid and yelled at me,'' she said, adding that he ''underlined 'unmarried' three times'' on the referral note he wrote for her. ''The procedure cost $350, a lot of money in 1970,'' she said. It was her first time on an airplane. Years later, she helped establish a family planning clinic in her hometown.\par \par \uc2 'I wanted to be able to make my own decisions.'\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Ramirez Watson\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973\par \par \uc2 Ms. Watson was in medical school in Mexico, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal in most cases. She had heard that abortions in the United States were now legal, so two friends drove with her from the city of Torre\u243\'00\'F3n to Texas. But when they got to the doctor's office, she was turned away. Consulting the telephone book, they went from doctor to doctor, but struggled to find anyone who would provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Finally, a nurse in a doctor's office agreed to insert a catheter into Ms. Watson. ''When she finished, she taped everything and then she said, 'Dress as quickly as possible.' She left the catheter in. She taped it to my leg. She wanted me like, boom, out the door,'' said Ms. Watson. When she went back to Mexico to continue her medical training, she hemorrhaged. ''I really felt like I was going to die,'' she said. She eventually became a doctor and moved to Texas. She has two daughters, five grandchildren and one great-grandchild, with another on the way.\par \par \uc2 'It was a practical decision, not a moral one.'\par \par \uc2 Katherine Deutch Tatlock\par \par \uc2 Had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1960\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know why I entered into this relationship, because I was not really attracted to him,'' said Ms. Tatlock, who was sharing a place in San Francisco with several roommates. ''I had too much to drink, it was late at night, and we had sex,'' she said. ''It was not a fun thing for me. I think I blacked out. It was the first time I ever had sex, and I got pregnant.'' She had always wanted a baby, but not like this. ''I was a baby myself,'' she said. The family doctor Ms. Tatlock saw laughed at her when she asked for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but suggested she go to the hospital, where she might be approved for the procedure. At the hospital, she went in circles with a psychiatrist for an hour before the psychiatrist finally said to her: ''You need to tell me whether you would kill yourself if you can't have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or kill your baby.'' ''I was crying. I had to lie. I said, 'Yes.''' Ms. Tatlock had her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She was OK. One of her roommates, who became pregnant weeks later and sought an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had a much harder experience: ''Her uterus was pierced by an object. As I'm telling this story, I'm trembling. It really was scary. She almost died in my arms.''\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220123ei1n0006q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
