\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Court Refuses To Intervene In Challenge On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In dissent, the three liberal justices said the majority, by allowing a time-consuming legal detour, had created ''a disaster for the rule of law.''\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court on Thursday rejected a request from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas that a federal judge be allowed to take prompt action on their challenge to a state law that bans most abortions after six weeks. The practical effect of the order, the three liberal justices wrote in dissent, was to let the law stay in place indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 ''This case is a disaster for the rule of law and a grave disservice to women in Texas, who have a right to control their own bodies,'' Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote in dissent.\par \par \uc2 The majority gave no reasons for its ruling, which followed a decision last month allowing the providers to sue at least some state officials to try to block or limit the law. That victory was an empty one, the dissenting justices wrote on Thursday, because the United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, refused to return the case to the trial judge and instead sent it on a legal detour to a state court.\par \par \uc2 ''It has been over four months since Texas Senate Bill 8 took effect,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''The law immediately devastated access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Texas through a complicated private bounty-hunter scheme that violates nearly 50 years of this court's precedents.''\par \par \uc2 ''Today, for the fourth time, this court declines to protect pregnant Texans from egregious violations of their constitutional rights,'' she wrote, referring to a series of rulings starting with one in September that let the law go into effect notwithstanding Supreme Court precedents that bar states from banning abortions before fetal viability, which is ordinarily around 23 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''One month after directing that the petitioners' suit could proceed in part, the court countenances yet another violation of its own commands,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''Instead of stopping a Fifth Circuit panel from indulging Texas' newest delay tactics, the court allows the state yet again to extend the deprivation of the federal constitutional rights of its citizens through procedural manipulation.''\par \par \uc2 Rather than sending the case back to the trial court, which was likely to enter an injunction, the Fifth Circuit asked the Texas Supreme Court to rule on whether the state officials specified in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision last month have the power to enforce the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 In a second dissent, Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2  said the appeals court's action amounted to defiance of his court's December ruling. ''The court of appeals,'' he wrote, ''ignored our judgment.''\par \par \uc2 Justices Breyer and Sotomayor joined each other's dissents, and Justice Elena Kagan joined both.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, said the Supreme Court ''is allowing the state of Texas to deprive people of a constitutional right.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law has novel features.\par \par \uc2 Usually, a lawsuit seeking to block a law because it is unconstitutional would name state officials as defendants. However, the Texas law, which makes no exceptions for pregnancies resulting from incest or rape, bars most state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizes private individuals to sue anyone who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or ''aids and abets'' it.\par \par \uc2 The patient may not be sued, but doctors, staff members at clinics, counselors, people who help pay for the procedure and even an Uber driver taking a patient to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic are all potential defendants. Plaintiffs -- who do not need to live in Texas, have any connection to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or show any injury from it -- are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees recovered if they win. Prevailing defendants are not entitled to legal fees.\par \par \uc2 ''This structure,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote, ''was designed to make it more complicated for courts to enjoin the law's enforcement on a statewide basis.''\par \par \uc2 In December, though, the Supreme Court allowed suits against state licensing officials like the executive director of the Texas Medical Board, who are authorized to take disciplinary actions against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who violate the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 ''After this court issued its judgment, however,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote on Thursday, ''the litigation stalled. The Fifth Circuit should have immediately remanded this case to the district court, allowing it to consider whether to issue preliminary relief. But Texas moved to certify to the Supreme Court of Texas the question this court had just decided: whether state licensing officials had authority under state law to enforce S.B. 8.''\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit, after hearing oral arguments, granted Texas' request.\par \par \uc2 ''By blessing this tactic,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote, ''the panel ignored this court's clear message that this case should proceed -- and proceed expeditiously.''\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' victory in last month's decision was limited and narrow, Judge Sotomayor wrote, adding that they have now been effectively robbed of what little they had won.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision shows that any hope that'' the December decision ''might protect the Constitution's guarantees in this case was illusory,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 ''As it turns out, Texas did not even have to amend its law to sidestep the minimal relief this court left available,'' she wrote. ''Instead, Texas wagered that this court did not mean what little it said in'' the decision ''or, at least, that this court would not stand behind those words, meager as they were. That bet has paid off.''\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators outside the Supreme Court last month. The majority gave no reasons for its ruling on Thursday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jim Lo Scalzo/EPA, via Shutterstock FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220121ei1l0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
