\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 F.D.A. Chief Nominee Advances After Split Vote by Senate Committee\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Jewett \par \uc2 1,030 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A split committee vote revealed concerns about the opioid epidemic and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, foreshadowing a likely close vote on confirmation by the full Senate.\par \par \uc2 A divided Senate committee on Thursday advanced the nomination of \uc2 Dr. Robert Califf\uc2  to become commissioner of the Food and Drug Administration to the full Senate, with some Democrats citing concerns over the nominee's commitment to combating the opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 The committee's vote of 13 to 8 suggests that the full Senate vote will be close. A date for the Senate's vote on confirmation has not been set.\par \par \uc2 Four Republican members of the Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions, including Senator \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2  of North Carolina, voted in favor of Dr. Califf's nomination. Senator Burr, the ranking Republican member, noted that the commissioner's role was one of the ''most significant public health positions in the federal government'' and that the agency had been without a Senate-confirmed leader for nearly a year. He urged other Republicans to support Dr. Califf, citing his ''unparalleled experience'' and willingness to support innovation in the medical field. G.O.P. committee members who voted against him mostly cited concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders, independent of Vermont, and Senators \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  of New Hampshire and Edward Markey of Massachusetts, both Democrats, indicated they planned to vote against confirming Dr. Califf for the post. They are all from New England states hard-hit by opioids, though Senator Sanders also criticized Dr. Califf's journey through the ''revolving door'' from the F.D.A. to private industry.\par \par \uc2 Senator Joe Manchin, Democrat of West Virginia, a state which was particularly devastated by overdose deaths, has condemned Dr. Califf's track record on opioids without directly saying how he will vote. His staff did not respond immediately to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, Dr. Califf would lead an agency that had been under intense scrutinyduring the pandemic, as it weighed the safety of coronavirus vaccines and treatments, and reviewed dozens of masks and tests.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, the agency has been criticized for what some consider the sluggish approval of rapid at-home tests, which has led to shortages as the Omicron variant has caused a spike in infections, and as demand has soared for tests for schoolchildren and workers. The agency said that it had concerns about the tests' sensitivity but not the overall accuracy.\par \par \uc2 High-risk patients who test positive for Covid-19 are also facing severe shortages of medications that are effective against the variant -- even as the agency remains the gatekeeper to therapies and diagnostics that could help.\par \par \uc2 On Dec. 14, Dr. Califf appeared before the Senate committee, answering questions about the pandemic, the opioid crisis and other topics.\par \par \uc2 As a cardiologist, Dr. Califf spent decades leading clinical trials at the Duke University Medical Center in North Carolina. Most recently, he led clinical policy and strategy for Verily, the life sciences arm at Alphabet, the parent company of \uc2 Google\uc2 . During the hearing, he emphasized the value of existing data, much in patients' electronic medical records, to help answer difficult questions about the evidence for drugs and devices.\par \par \uc2 Republicans asked how he would handle rules around medications to induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had been relaxed during the pandemic after years of tight restrictions. Two days after the hearing, the F.D.A. -- under \uc2 Dr. Janet Woodcock\uc2 , the interim commissioner -- announced that it would permanently lift restrictions, allowing patients to terminate a pregnancy in the first 10 weeks after a telemedicine visit with medications taken at home.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tommy Tuberville, a Republican committee member from Alabama, said in a statement that the recent decision informed his committee vote. ''I believe Dr. Califf will continue to advance this administration's pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda, and I cannot support his nomination,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2 , a Republican from Indiana, also said he opposed the nomination over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and opioid concerns. ''Under his leadership in 2016, the F.D.A. expanded access to the dangerous chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone and failed to take action to stem the tide of the opioid crisis ravaging our country,'' he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Califf was previously confirmed in a 89-to-4 Senate vote to lead the F.D.A. during the last year of the Obama administration. If confirmed again, he will face key decisions on tobacco control as he tries to balance e-cigarettes as a tool to help some quit smoking without creating a new generation of users. Scrutiny around accelerated approvals of drugs will be heightened, given the current debate over the agency's approval of the controversial Alzheimer's drug Aduhelm.\par \par \uc2 After Senator Sanders denounced Dr. Califf's ties to pharmaceutical firms, which include consulting fees and stock ownership amounting to as much as $8 million, the nominee told the Senate panel in mid-December that he would comply with ethics rules set by the F.D.A. and its parent agency, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Hassan of New Hampshire also voted against Dr. Califf's nomination. She had pressed him in December on the F.D.A.'s failure to update the labeling of opioid medications. Dr. Califf said the agency would continue to review the evidence coming in.\par \par \uc2 ''The evidence has been here for a long time,'' she responded. ''It's the evidence of thousands of people, hundreds of thousands of people, dying in this country.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Markey of Massachusetts, who is not on the committee that voted on Thursday, also issued a statement citing opioid policy failures and saying he would vote against Dr. Califf. The F.D.A. ''repeatedly rubber-stamped new prescription opioids,'' and ''acted too slowly to remove them from the market or place restrictions on their labels,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Senator Markey said he was dissatisfied after meeting with Dr. Califf late last year, noting that he ''did not commit to the decisive and comprehensive action necessary'' to regulate opioids.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Robert Califf led the F.D.A. during the last year of the Obama administration. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220114ei1e00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
