\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Mexico, Activists Plan to Help Women In U.S. Get Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Kitroeff \par \uc2 1,572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mexican activists plan to provide women in Texas and other U.S. states with information, support -- and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing pills.\par \par \uc2 GUANAJUATO, Mexico -- Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz spent years defying the law in Mexico, helping thousands of women get abortions. Now that Mexico has declared that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer a crime, Ms. Cruz and activists like her are planning to bring their mission to a country moving in the opposite direction: the United States.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have been multiplying across the United States for years, including just over Mexico's border in Texas. Now the Supreme Court is considering a case that could diminish or completely overrule Roe v. Wade, the 1973 ruling that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That would likely set off new restrictions in at least 20 states.\par \par \uc2 But in much of Latin America, where access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been severely limited, highly organized feminist groups have distributed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs for years, making it harder for governments to enforce bans on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cruz and other activists are planning to help shuttle Texans and other Americans seeking abortions into Mexico, and to build networks to ferry the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills north of the border or send them by mail -- something they've already started doing and now plan to expand.\par \par \uc2 ''We aren't afraid,'' said Ms. Cruz. ''We are willing to face criminalization, because women's lives matter more than their law.''\par \par \uc2 The strategy is highly contentious, because it involves foreign activists working directly to undermine American law. It also illustrates what activists on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate see as a new frontier of the battle: the government's ability to control \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when women can perform them in the privacy of their homes, with pills that are becoming more widely available than ever.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs can be delivered by mail, making permanent a measure enacted because of the pandemic and broadening access for women who find it difficult to travel to a provider to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 But several states ban the delivery of these pills by mail, or still require that the drugs be dispensed by providers in person, on top of other restrictions on their use.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, a new law bars doctors from providing pills to induce abortions after seven weeks of pregnancy, and adds penalties of jail time and a fine of up to $10,000 for anyone who mails or delivers the medication.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts say such laws may be challenged after the F.D.A. decision, but for now, these state measures could discourage American doctors from sending pills to parts of the country with restrictive regulations.\par \par \uc2 ''For the first time, Texas does have a way to protect women, through our criminal law, from people bringing dangerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills,'' said Joe Pojman, executive director of the Texas Alliance for Life, an organization that helped craft the measure. ''We'll have to wait to see how well it is enforced in the coming months.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups acknowledge that criminally punishing activists who distribute the pills, especially if they are from Mexico, may prove difficult. They would have to be caught and arrested in Texas, or extradited, experts say.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a really terrible, lawless attack on life,'' John Seago, the legislative director for Texas Right to Life, said of the Mexican activists' plan to help women in Texas get abortions, adding that such efforts would ''make it absolutely more difficult to do it, to enforce these laws.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, the leader of Aid Access, an Austria-based group that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to women across the world, confirmed she has been prescribing the medication to women in Texas -- who then receive the drugs by mail from a pharmacy in India -- even after the state's law went into effect this month.\par \par \uc2 The drug misoprostol, originally created to treat stomach ulcers, but which also induces abortions, has upended access to the procedure around the globe by giving women a safe, effective and often cheap method of ending their pregnancies in private.\par \par \uc2 Taking the drug, either alone or in combination with another called mifepristone, causes what is called a ''medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Across Latin America, networks of activists who work on the margins of the legal system deliver the pills to women and walk them through using the medication to end pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The groups, often in coordination with allies in the medical community, use a model now known as ''accompaniment,'' in which they disseminate pills and also provide medical counseling and psychological support to women in a deeply Catholic region where abortions are often shunned and outlawed.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of misoprostol and mifepristone was ''revolutionary,'' said Giselle Carino, the chief executive of F\u242\'00\'F2s Feminista, an international alliance of health groups. ''But it wouldn't have been so effective in saving women's lives without the feminist networks of accompaniment and health professionals willing to engage in civil disobedience.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cruz, the Mexican activist, helped found an organization called Las Libres, which means ''the free ones,'' in 2000. She began knocking on gynecologists' doors in her conservative state of Guanajuato, asking them to provide free abortions to rape victims.\par \par \uc2 A few years later, one of the doctors she had been working with came back from a conference with some news: There was a pill that could safely cause abortions at home. Misoprostol can be obtained without a prescription in Mexico, and the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has a protocol for administering it to perform abortions in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''That's when I knew we had a solution,'' said Ms. Cruz. ''We didn't need doctors anymore.''\par \par \uc2 At the time, the pills were prohibitively expensive. So, Ms. Cruz says, she came up with a strategy: A woman who could afford to buy the medication would keep the pills left over after her procedure, then pass the box on to the next woman who needed it, while coaching her through the process.\par \par \uc2 ''And that's how the first networks were formed, organically,'' Ms. Cruz said. The goal, she says, was never to just provide abortions. It was to turn every Mexican woman into someone who could help someone else get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The handful of employees who work with Las Libres have boxes of the drugs, which they buy or receive as donations, strewn everywhere -- in their cars, their homes, even their pockets. Each woman who reaches out to the group is assigned a ''companion'' to bring her pills and then follow her, through video calls, phone calls or WhatsApp messages, through every step of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A recent study of more than 900 people in Nigeria and Argentina, published in The Lancet medical journal, found that accompanying patients as they manage their own abortions is ''highly effective and safe.'' It led to complete abortions -- not requiring follow-up surgical intervention to finish -- for 97 percent of those surveyed. For women living under strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, it is also difficult to detect.\par \par \uc2 Erika Sandoval, 23, said she had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at home this month in Mexico after texting an activist with Ms. Cruz's group. She got the medication from the activist's brother, followed instructions delivered in a voice memo and finished the process two days later.\par \par \uc2 ''All this from a voice memo,'' she said in an interview two days after she took her last pill.\par \par \uc2 Sof\u237\'00\'EDa, also 23, said she took the medicine in the house she shares with her parents in Mexico, closing the door of her room while she texted with a woman from Ms. Cruz's group. When she finished, she said, she told her parents she had been feeling ill after eating tacos.\par \par \uc2 ''They made me chicken soup,'' she said. Her parents never realized what had happened, and Sof\u237\'00\'EDa asked to only be identified by her first name so they wouldn't find out.\par \par \uc2 In September, days after Texas began enacting a new ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, Mexico's Supreme Court ruled that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could no longer be treated as a crime. Ms. Cruz and her colleagues soon hatched a plan to work with Texas reproductive groups on making it easier for women in the state to end their pregnancies at home.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of activists are meeting in January to work out the strategy. One of them, Crystal P. Lira, said she has already brought pills from Tijuana to California, and then shipped them to Georgia, Indiana, Massachusetts and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cruz said the new Texas laws would not stop them from crossing the border with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs -- even if it means risking jail time.\par \par \uc2 ''If that's the only way that people will become conscious that what the government is doing is a major violation of human rights,'' she said, ''then yes.''\par \par \uc2 Sof\u237\'00\'EDa, 23, said she took \uc2  medication in the house she shares with her parents and texted with a woman from Las Libres.; Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz, the founder and leader of Las Libres, in the library at Las Libres house in Guanajuato, Mexico. At left, medication used for abortions at home. ''We aren't afraid,'' said Ms. Cruz. ''We are willing to face criminalization, because women's lives matter more than their law.'' (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MARIAN CARRASQUERO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211221ehcl00072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
