\par\par \uc2 JESSICA GROSE\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Overturning Roe Will Make Miscarriage Care Worse\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Grose \par \uc2 1,534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When you have your first bad sonogram, you fall into an abyss of maternity care. If you haven't experienced it, you might not know the contours of this purgatory, but I can tell you what it's like. Almost exactly seven years ago, the face of my obstetrician fell while performing an ultrasound for a very wanted pregnancy, and our collective mood shifted in an instant from buoyant to somber.\par \par \uc2 I learned that day that it appeared that my pregnancy was not progressing, because my doctor couldn't find a heartbeat. But he couldn't be certain; my period was quite irregular, and it was possible that he misdated the pregnancy and that it was still viable. So I had to wait. One week, then two. Dragging myself into the radiologist's office every few days to see if there was a heartbeat while attempting to work and parent my then-2-year-old and desperately trying not to cry most of my waking moments.\par \par \uc2 When my doctors were finally certain that the pregnancy would not go forward, I was given three options: I could continue to wait and see if my body would miscarry on its own without intervention, I could take medication and end the pregnancy at home, or I could have a surgical procedure to empty my uterus, known colloquially as a D. and C. (The last two options are the same choices offered to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients.)\par \par \uc2 I chose the D. and C., mainly because I wanted to get this awful experience over with as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 Years later, I am at peace with the pregnancy loss; the fetus had a chromosomal issue called Turner syndrome, which ''may cause up to 10 percent of all first-trimester miscarriages,'' according to the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 . I know now that miscarriages are common. An estimated one-quarter of all pregnancies and around 10 percent of known pregnancies end in miscarriage before 20 weeks. Thankfully, I was able to have another healthy child later. But that two-week wait remains painful to think about.\par \par \uc2 And yet I'm thinking about it in the aftermath of the Supreme Court's ruling on Friday allowing federal court challenges to Texas' restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, S.B. 8, but leaving the law in effect, essentially outlawing abortions after six weeks in that state. That's because in countries where elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed or extremely restricted, women are not given the choices I had when they miscarry.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions create a chilling effect on medical professionals who are understandably concerned about being prosecuted for anything resembling elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And so doctors in countries with restrictive laws ''don't always provide all the relevant information concerning the pregnancy, especially if they see there are complications and they're afraid women can take drastic measures,'' said Irene Donadio, a senior adviser at the International Planned Parenthood Federation.\par \par \uc2 I asked Dr. Isabel Stabile, a gynecologist in private practice in Malta and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activist, what first-trimester miscarriage care looks like in her country, where there is a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with no exceptions. ''The short answer to this question is in Malta it's always a wait and see. Women are never given the immediate option of being hospitalized and having a D. and C. nor having pills so we can proceed with a spontaneous miscarriage. The medical and surgical options are never offered as a first line,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She said that in Malta, if your body is holding on to a miscarriage for three or four weeks, you may eventually be given pills to end the pregnancy. By that time, Dr. Stabile said, you may be at greater risk of a rare but serious blood-clotting complication called disseminated intravascular coagulation, which can be avoided by ending a miscarriage earlier. ''It's known as a complication. One should at least be monitored,'' Dr. Stabile said. But in general, the drawn-out nature and ''uncertainty of the wait'' are the biggest stresses for Maltese women, she said. ''You have no idea how long that will be. Will it be one day, one week or one month?''\par \par \uc2 In cases like mine, when there is no detectable heartbeat, the trauma may primarily be to women's mental health. But when there is a detectable heartbeat and there are other pregnancy complications, there are physiological stakes, including that women can and have died. In Poland, which has some of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Europe, a 30-year-old woman named Izabela died of septic shock this year in Pszczyna after doctors declined to intervene to save her life. The fetus's heart was still beating, so physicians may have been afraid to break the country's laws because the penalty is spending three years in prison, according to reporting in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Izabela isn't the only one. In 2012, before Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions were rolled back in 2018, a 31-year-old woman named Savita Halappanavar died in similar circumstances. In 2016 in Italy, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal until about the 12th week, a woman named Valentina Milluzzo died of sepsis because her doctor refused to help her end her twin pregnancy, even after one twin no longer had a heartbeat. ''According to the woman's relatives, the gynecologist, who was a conscientious objector'' -- something that's legal under Italy's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law -- ''refused to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the second twin after the loss of the first fetus. He remarked that 'as long as it [the fetus] is alive, I will not intervene,''' wrote Elena Caruso in the academic journal Feminist Legal Studies.\par \par \uc2 If you think this wouldn't happen in the United States, think again, because there is evidence that it is already happening. At Catholic hospitals, which are expected to follow directives set by the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops to never allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, women may not be getting the full slate of medical options when they present with an ectopic pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In September, Ghazaleh Moayedi, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Texas, sounded the alarm in these pages. ''Pregnancies that face complications will now be at greater risk. Under this new law, the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exception allowed is for a medical emergency. That might mean if a woman will imminently lose an organ or die without intervention. But how we judge that risk will play out individually with each hospital's policy, in each clinic,'' she wrote. ''I can think of no other area of health care in which we would wait for someone to worsen nearly to the point of death before we offered intervention. It's just unconscionable.''\par \par \uc2 When I read some of the testimony of reproductive choice opponents, I think about how certain they sound about what life is. Situations like mine, which existed in a liminal state for weeks, defy easy categorization and require intimate and informed medical care, not blunt force laws that don't consider these delicate scenarios.\par \par \uc2 And when I look at polls in which a low percentage of American voters list \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a top issue, I wonder if those voters have considered how a lack of access to these procedures may affect them, even if they would never have an elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those two weeks were among the worst of my life, and thinking about anyone having to prolong that kind of purgatory just about breaks my heart.\par \par \uc2 Want More on Managing Loss?\par \par \uc2 Politics may be preventing some miscarrying women from accessing mifepristone, a medication that may help make the process quicker and less painful.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, The Times ran a guide about recognizing symptoms of miscarriage and coping with the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Meghan, the Duchess of Sussex, wrote about her miscarriage and how to comfort those grieving all sorts of losses.\par \par \uc2 Tiny Victories\par \par \uc2 Parenting can be a grind. Let's celebrate the tiny victories.\par \par \uc2 I had a challenging workday. When I picked my son up from school, he could tell that I was preoccupied and asked me what was wrong. After telling him in brief what was going on, he launched into how he would give me a hug or go on a walk with me to make me feel better. I said, ''Honey, that is really nice of you. Remember, it is not your job to fix me when I feel sad or frustrated.'' He replied, ''Well, you've kept me alive for 11 years. That deserves something.''\par \par \uc2 -- Sarah Piper, Atlanta\par \par \uc2 If you want a chance to get your Tiny Victory published, find us on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @NYTparenting and use the hashtag #tinyvictories; email us; or enter your Tiny Victory at the bottom of this page. Include your full name and location. Tiny Victories may be edited for clarity and style. Your name, location and comments may be published, but your contact information will not. By submitting to us, you agree that you have read, understand and accept the Reader Submission Terms in relation to all of the content and other information you send to us.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Eleanor Davis FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211220ehck00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
