\par\par \uc2 Magazine Desk; SECTMM\par \b \uc2 The Thread\b0\par\par\uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Readers respond to the 12.5.21 issue.\par \par \uc2 RE: THE \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  I DIDN'T HAVE\par \par \uc2 Merritt Tierce wrote about getting pregnant at 19 and having a child at 20.\par \par \uc2 I hesitate to write this about something so intensely private, something that is really nobody else's business, but I was so moved by Merritt Tierce's piece because in many ways I am her and took the route she did not. In 1976, I was 18 years old, a sophomore in college and only the second person in my extended family to go to university. I still remember the exact moment of being on the hall telephone in my dorm when I got the news. I knew immediately that I was neither mature enough nor financially able to become a parent at that point in my life. As I left the clinic, I felt a wave of relief wash over me. I have never regretted it, and I am to this day a staunch defender of a woman's right to choose whether and when to bear a child. This decision has such profound consequences for a woman's life that nobody -- especially not the state -- should have the arrogance to assume they are better equipped than she is to make it.\par \par \uc2 Suzanne L'Hernault, Florham Park, N.J.\par \par \uc2 Merritt Tierce's uncompromising and nuanced look at her reproductive decisions is a true breath of fresh air in an overheated debate. I was able to make a diff erent choice at age 21, and I was provided, in 1977, with all the emotional support I needed to follow through on what was a hard but necessary decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I knew that if I continued with the pregnancy, my life wouldn't be a disaster. But I was very sure that my life would take a different path from the one I needed to follow, and that I was not in any way prepared to be a mother. I later had a son, when I was ready, whom I love dearly, and who was wanted from well before his conception. Merritt places our modern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate within the context of ordinary women's lives. I want to thank her for her honesty and eloquence, letting us in for a moment to walk with her through what must have been a diffi cult and frightening experience.\par \par \uc2 Maren Halvorsen, Sequim, Wash.\par \par \uc2 What a gorgeous and courageous piece of writing this is. I felt it like an emotional echo. I was 19 and pregnant and unmarried when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal and dangerous. I have never given voice to the feelings I had -- and have -- about having a child I was not ready for either emotionally or any other way. I also loved my son dearly. He died during the AIDS pandemic. I have never stopped loving him or atoning for inadequate mothering. I am so grateful that this piece gives voice to so many of us who found ourselves in this space, and all those who still do. Thank you, Merritt, for having the courage to speak for us.\par \par \uc2 Susan Black, Berkeley, Calif.\par \par \uc2 The story by Merritt Tierce is beautiful and poignant and real. She writes with clarity and insight into the complexity of her decision about her unsought pregnancy, and its ramifi cations for her relationship with her children. I come from the opposite side of the same conundrum. I had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at age 22, after contraception failure.\par \par \uc2 My devoutly religious family had severed ties with me over my relationship with a man who came from a diff erent religious background. I was completely alone and living in poverty, shunted from doctor to doctor, required to wait for two agonizingly long and unnecessary months. Medical staff treated me with disdain and judgment. The procedure was long and painful. I fell into a severe postpartum depression. I had no control over how I felt and was in a void. It took many years, half a lifetime, to recover, but however awful it was, I never doubted that I made the right choice. My decision was tragic, but exactly right. Some years later, I devoted every moment to the joy of being a mother. Merritt Tierce's story confirms my belief that pregnant women may not be able, or be ready, to give enough of themselves to a child. I honor her decision. It could so easily have been me. This is the story that I have never told. Merritt Tierce's essay allows me to reflect and speak about my experience.\par \par \uc2 Denise Pugash\par \par \uc2 I want to thank Merritt Tierce for her incredible eloquence and courage. I just fi nished reading her piece -- my heart rate is fi nally declining, my body has stopped shaking and I am not sobbing anymore. I have never read a piece that has resonated so much with my life experiences. Unlike Ms. Tierce, I was much older and (God forbid I write this out loud) newly married when I had my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I also saw my future being erased if I continued with the pregnancy. As Ms. Tierce conveyed in her article, denying a woman the choice to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when she does not have adequate mental, physical and/or fi nancial resources to properly raise that child could have negative repercussions for both. I worry about all the young women whose futures may be erased and lives suppressed if we lose our right to choose.\par \par \uc2 L. Mauro, Saint Paul, Minn.\par \par \uc2 Send your thoughts to magazine@nytimes.com.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211219ehcj0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
