\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Shifting Trends For Abortions Since the '70s\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margot Sanger-Katz, Claire Cain Miller and Quoctrung Bui \par \uc2 1,311 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The typical woman having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  today is already a mother. She is in her 20s and has taken some college classes. Very rarely is she past her first trimester of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The portrait of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America has changed along with society, and is far different from what it was when Roe v. Wade established the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973.\par \par \uc2 The typical patient is less likely to be a teenager and more likely to be poor, and unmarried. But in the reproductive lives of women (and transgender and nonbinary people who can become pregnant) across America, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not uncommon. The latest estimate, from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, found that 25 percent of women would have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of their childbearing years.\par \par \uc2 ''There isn't one monolith demographic who get abortions,'' said Ushma Upadhyay of the Bixby Center for Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 . ''The same people who become pregnant and give birth are the same people who have abortions at different points in their lives.''\par \par \uc2 As heated as the issue has become in recent years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, calculated among women ages 15 to 44, has been falling. Americans are having half as many abortions as 30 years ago. Researchers say a variety of factors -- including better contraceptive use and less sex among teenagers -- is leading to fewer unintended pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The data offers a broad outline of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States today -- and who may be most affected if Roe v. Wade is diminished or overturned by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Number of Previous Children\par \par \uc2 Six in 10 women who have abortions are already mothers, and half of them have two or more children, according to 2019 data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . ''One of the main reasons people report wanting to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is so they can be a better parent to the kids they already have,'' Professor Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 Number of Weeks Pregnant\par \par \uc2 Much of the political debate about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America focuses on abortions performed late in pregnancy, but the overwhelming majority of them occur in the first trimester. Forty-three percent of all abortions occur in the first six weeks of pregnancy, and 92 percent in the first 13 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In 1973, the Roe decision provided a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, around 23 weeks. If it's overturned, at least 22 states are likely to ban abortions altogether, or much earlier in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi law before the Supreme Court concerns a ban on abortions after 15 weeks. In the 47 states counted, about 21,500 women a year, accounting for 4 percent of American abortions, had the procedure after that time. The women who receive later abortions are more likely to be poor or young or to have serious health complications.\par \par \uc2 During oral arguments on the Mississippi case, Chief Justice John Roberts seemed interested in changing the legal standard to protect some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but allow a limit like 15 weeks. No other justice appeared interested in the solution, and lawyers on both sides declined to suggest how a compromise could work legally.\par \par \uc2 Age\par \par \uc2 A majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients are in their 20s. Just 9 percent are under 20, and around a third are over 30. Teenagers used to make up a higher share, but there has been a sharp decline in teen pregnancy in the United States, starting in 1991, and a corresponding drop in abortions among teenagers. Today, American women of all demographics are having children later in life.\par \par \uc2 Education\par \par \uc2 Increasingly, women say they are delaying having children until they can finish school, establish a career and support themselves. The share of women attending college is climbing rapidly. For women who do not feel they have many educational or career opportunities, early parenthood is often embraced as a primary route to fulfillment, social science research has found.\par \par \uc2 Income\par \par \uc2 About half of women who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2014 were below the poverty line, with another quarter very close to poverty. Guttmacher surveys show low-income women have been a growing share of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in recent decades. Several smaller studies of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients have shown similar results. Researchers say this shift reflects improved access to effective contraception among higher-earning women, and a recognition of the growing costs of raising children among poorer women. It may also reflect the growing presence of charities that help poor women pay for abortions in states where public programs don't.\par \par \uc2 ''It's people who don't have access to health care, access to contraception, who, when facing an unintended pregnancy, don't have the resources to have another child,'' said \uc2 Rachel Jones\uc2 , a principal research scientist at Guttmacher.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  History\par \par \uc2 Six in 10 women who have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are having one for the first time. Another quarter have had one previous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Less than a fifth have had two or more. ''It's simply not the case that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is something large numbers of people turn to as a form of birth control,'' said Caitlin Knowles Myers, an economist at Middlebury who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Marital Status\par \par \uc2 Nearly half of those who have abortions are single. A third are living with a partner, and 14 percent are married. Cohabiting but unmarried people are overrepresented in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers, while married people are underrepresented, based on their share of the population. One of the groups with the largest declines in unintended pregnancies and in birthrate in recent years are unmarried mothers, according to data analysis by Professor Myers.\par \par \uc2 Type of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Women seeking abortions early in their pregnancies have two options: a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a medical one, in which they can take pills that cause a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 Medical abortions are a growing share. In 2019, about 44 percent of women who sought abortions in clinics before 10 weeks of pregnancy chose pills. (The share of women who use medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably higher, because the C.D.C. data excludes California, which has a higher share of medication abortions than other states.) The \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  is considering regulatory changes that would make it easier for more doctors to prescribe the pills. If that happens, they may become an even larger share of the total.\par \par \uc2 Abortions late in pregnancy or among women with serious medical complications sometimes involve other types of surgery. These procedures represent much less than one percent of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 Location\par \par \uc2 The states with the highest share of women who obtained abortions in 2019 were Democratic-leaning. That may reflect differences in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and the cultural acceptance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some regions. In some Republican-leaning states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already inaccessible to much of the population. In Missouri, Mississippi, North Dakota and South Dakota, there is only one clinic per state, and for some people it's not feasible to travel far to reach one.\par \par \uc2 ''The same states that restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have restrictions or increased barriers to contraception,'' Professor Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the recent declines in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate have been larger in states with widespread \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access than in those that have added restrictions, where abortions were more likely to have increased, Dr. Jones said. The declines in Democratic states may be in part because of increased access to contraception and other health care, leading to fewer unplanned pregnancies, she noted.\par \par \uc2 Around 33 percent of abortions are performed in the 22 states that would be likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe were overturned. There would be fewer abortions in those states post-Roe. But some of those women would travel out of state, and some would end their pregnancies using extralegal means.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211215ehcf0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
