\par\par \uc2 CHARLES M. BLOW\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 We're Edging Closer to Civil War\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charles M. Blow \par \uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Friday issued a decision allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas to continue challenging a new law that bans most abortions in the state after about six weeks of pregnancy. But while the conservative majority didn't close the door on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas completely, the degree to which it is cracked allows in only a sliver of light.\par \par \uc2 For now, the law in question, S.B. 8, remains on the books. Anyone who assists in providing an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- from the provider down to the person who gives a woman a ride to the clinic -- can still be sued. Roe v. Wade has essentially been overturned in the state, and soon that astonishing reality may not only become permanent there but may also spread to other states.\par \par \uc2 A key component of women's rights and body autonomy is being snatched away as we watch.\par \par \uc2 In a dissent on Friday, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote: ''This is a brazen challenge to our federal structure. It echoes the philosophy of John C. Calhoun, a virulent defender of the slaveholding South who insisted that States had the right to 'veto' or 'nullif[y]' any federal law with which they disagreed.''\par \par \uc2 I found the invocation of South Carolina's Calhoun striking. Yes, he was a strong believer in nullification, the idea that states could nullify federal laws, but he was also a raging racist who went further than the slave owners who saw slavery as a ''necessary evil,'' seeing it instead as a positive good.\par \par \uc2 In 1837, Calhoun railed in a speech on the Senate floor that slavery had ''grown up with our society and institutions and is so interwoven with them that to destroy it would be to destroy us as a people.'' He continued:\par \par \uc2 But let me not be understood as admitting, even by implication, that the existing relations between the two races in the slaveholding states is an evil: Far otherwise; I hold it to be a good, as it has thus far proved itself to be to both and will continue to prove so if not disturbed by the fell spirit of abolition. I appeal to facts. Never before has the Black race of Central Africa, from the dawn of history to the present day, attained a condition so civilized and so improved, not only physically but morally and intellectually.\par \par \uc2 He would reiterate that slavery was, ''instead of an evil, a good -- a positive good.''\par \par \uc2 In fact, Calhoun's stance on slavery and states' rights was so severe that he has been called the father of secession and the man who started the Civil War, even though he died 11 years before the war commenced.\par \par \uc2 In Calhoun's view, the states had the right to control and oppress Black bodies as they saw fit, regardless of any actions to the contrary on the federal level. States, he felt, should be able to choose whether or not they wanted slavery.\par \par \uc2 I see too many uneasy parallels between what was happening nearly 200 years ago and what is happening now. I see this country on the verge of another civil war, as the Calhounian impulse is reborn.\par \par \uc2 There are enormous, obvious differences, of course. The civil war I see is not the kind that would leave hundreds of thousands of young men dead in combat. That is not to say that we aren't seeing spats of violence but rather that this new war will be fought in courts, statehouses and ballot boxes, rather than in the fields.\par \par \uc2 And this war won't be only about the subjugation of Black people but also about the subjugation of all who challenge the white racist patriarchy.\par \par \uc2 It will seek to push back against all the ''others'': Black people, immigrants, Muslims, Jews, L.G.B.T.Q. people and, yes, women, particularly liberal ones.\par \par \uc2 In some ways, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battle now being waged in the courts is a test case. Can the states make an argument that a civil right can be reversed on the state level? Can they make the case that all that the Constitution has not explicitly spelled out should be reserved for the states?\par \par \uc2 The Constitution has been silent on quite a bit since it was written in 1787, and the last time it was amended was nearly 30 years ago, in 1992, when the states ratified the 27th Amendment. When did Congress first approve that amendment? In 1789! Having not gotten enough states to ratify it after passage, it simply languished for 200 years.\par \par \uc2 All of us should be very worried about what we see happening with these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases -- not just women who might need abortions or relatives and friends of women who might need them.\par \par \uc2 We should worry about whether or not we are at an inflection point for an age of regression.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) , and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEFANI REYNOLDS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211213ehcd00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
