\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Public Is Not as Polarized as Parties\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nate Cohn \par \uc2 1,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Despite decades of partisan fighting in Washington, Americans are not as neatly divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as politicians and activists.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the most polarizing issues in Washington. Congressional Democrats and Republicans all but unanimously back their party's view on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and many highly engaged activists feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 But the public's view of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far more complicated.\par \par \uc2 Despite decades of partisan fighting, Americans are not as neatly divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as politicians and activists. There are Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Democrats who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a surprisingly large group of voters who appear to have muddled or conflicted views. Overall, 26 percent of voters hold a different view on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the presidential candidate they supported in 2020, according to data from an AP VoteCast election survey of more than 100,000 voters.\par \par \uc2 No issue quite compares to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least not in its emotional and moral stakes. Yet by some measures, more voters hold views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at odds with those of their presidential pick than on other hot-button issues, including gun control, coronavirus mask mandates or a border wall.\par \par \uc2 The relatively large number of voters who split with their party on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may simply be a reflection of how the Supreme Court's decision in Roe v. Wade often kept the issue from the center of political debate. But it may also suggest that many voters just don't feel as strongly about the issue as one might assume.\par \par \uc2 The findings in the AP VoteCast election survey are a reminder that American politics are not always as polarized as we imagine. The bitterly partisan fight unfolding in statehouses and courthouses, even in the Supreme Court's split decision on Friday over the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, can obscure how many Americans of all parties struggle with the weighty moral and ethical questions raised by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As recently as 30 years ago, Democrats and Republicans had very similar views of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 1991, 42 percent of Democrats thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal whenever a woman sought one, compared with 41 percent of Republicans. Although attitudes about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have gradually tracked more sharply along partisan lines since then, there are still many voters who hold a mix of views that diverge from party allegiance or affiliation.\par \par \uc2 Less engaged and moderate voters are especially likely to hold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views at odds with their party. According to the 2018 General Social Survey, 92 percent of college-educated liberal Democrats believe it should be possible for women to obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if she wanted for any reason, compared with just 55 percent of more moderate Democrats. Similarly, 39 percent of moderate Republicans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, according to \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The complexity of Americans' views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is perhaps a reflection of the role religion plays in it. Only a handful of other political issues are more closely tied to people's personal religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 At least 65 percent of evangelical Christians believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in most or all cases, according to the AP VoteCast data, compared with 29 percent of nonevangelical voters. That 36-point gap is far larger than the 21-point gap between evangelical and nonevangelical support for \uc2 Joseph R. Biden\uc2  in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the conflicted voters tend to be relatively religious Democrats and less religious Republicans, including Black evangelical Democrats who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or relatively secular white working-class Trump voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The importance of religion brings a clear regional dimension to the political stakes of the issue. Evangelical and religious voters are disproportionately concentrated in the South. That includes conflicted Democrats: Only 59 percent of Southern Democratic-leaning voters say most abortions should be legal, according to \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 . Conflicted Republicans, meanwhile, are likeliest to live in the North and especially the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, the electoral risk might be most pronounced in these Northern battleground states, where a sizable share of their voters believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal. About 37 percent of Donald J. Trump's supporters in Pennsylvania and Michigan believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly legal, according to the AP VoteCast data. It's a large enough number to create a plausible electoral vulnerability for Republicans advocating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, but it's a small enough number that the party would most likely support new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions if the Supreme Court allowed it.\par \par \uc2 There's nothing new about these cultural issues holding Republicans back in the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Republicans struggled to break through in the region for a generation, as the religious right helped the party in the South but not in the less evangelical Northern battleground states. It was Mr. Trump's new brand of incendiary politics, focused on issues like immigration and crime, that helped Republicans gain an advantage in the region by polarizing American politics along educational rather than religious lines.\par \par \uc2 Although renewed attention on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might cut against some Trump-era trends, it could tend to reinforce others, like deteriorating Democratic strength among nonwhite voters. Much like white voters, Black and Hispanic voters are largely divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though they are far more likely to vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 Of course, just because voters are conflicted on an issue doesn't mean they are bound to break to the other party. Many of these voters are partisans, despite their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , precisely because they care more about other issues.\par \par \uc2 It's even possible that many of these conflicted voters aren't conflicted between their views and their party's views, but simply conflicted on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. The number of Americans who support or oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can vary widely, depending on how pollsters phrase the question. And while polls routinely show that a majority of voters think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly legal, polls often show significant support for the kinds of restrictions that might be up for debate if the Supreme Court overturns Roe.\par \par \uc2 The confusion is perhaps best illustrated in a series of poll questions asked by Gallup. Each result alone could seem to vindicate either side's view of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Together, however, they raise doubts about whether a majority of voters have a clear position on the issue at all.\par \par \uc2 According to Gallup, voters overwhelmingly think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should generally be illegal during the second trimester. But a different Gallup poll found that voters opposed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 18 weeks, in the middle of the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 In another set of perplexing findings, Gallup found that 60 percent of Americans thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in the first three months of pregnancy, only to see support drop to 45 percent simply by adding the condition: ''when the woman does not want the child for any reason.'' Apparently, many people who support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still recoil at the idea of it under many circumstances.\par \par \uc2 These seemingly incompatible findings suggest that a large number of voters either have complex and nuanced views or are so conflicted that even subtle changes in the wording of a question can yield huge changes in the results.\par \par \uc2 Attitudes on \uc2  don't always align with partisanship. Many voters hold conflicting views. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211212ehcc0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
