\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New Reality Persists for Texas Women, Despite Rulings on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman and Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,439 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Texas statute that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy was seemingly undercut by two court rulings, but the reality on the ground has not changed.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- Within the span of a few hours, a Texas judge and the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  appeared to undercut Texas' six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, ruling in separate opinions late Thursday and early Friday that aspects of the law were unconstitutional under state law and that federal challenges could go forward.\par \par \uc2 But the victories for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters were largely hollow, and providers in Texas were not cheering on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Neither decision altered the reality on the ground in the state. Both left in place the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, the most restrictive in the country, which effectively bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, because the court once again failed to protect our most basic human rights, more people will continue to suffer as this case winds its way through the legal system,'' said Dr. Bhavik Kumar, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in San Antonio, in a call with reporters hosted by \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 State Senator \uc2 Bryan Hughes\uc2 , the lead sponsor of the law in the Texas Senate, called Friday's ruling ''a total victory for life.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expected to rule next summer on the constitutionality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a separate case from Mississippi challenging the central holdings of Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, for Texas women, the new normal will continue. Clinics that have been forced to turn away women will continue to do so. A surge in travel to out-of-state providers for abortions is unlikely to abate. And desperation is likely to deepen among women seeking abortions beyond six weeks of pregnancy. In some cases, clinic staff members have reported women begging them to meet after hours in the parking lot to give them \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 So many women have been seeking assistance to get abortions outside of Texas that one group that helps with travel, Fund Texas Choice, tripled the size of its small staff and still found itself overwhelmed trying to keep up with the demand. From a few dozen calls a month, it now receives about 300, said Anna Rupani, the executive director of the fund.\par \par \uc2 ''We're servicing 50 to 60 percent of the calls that we get,'' she said. The fund has had to spend more money on each woman -- about $1,000 on average now -- because women have had to travel much further to access abortions outside of Texas since the law took effect on Sept 1. Most round trips are about 1,100 miles, she said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban relies on an usual enforcement mechanism: Private citizens are tasked with enforcing its provisions, and state officials are barred from doing so. It was designed to deny \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups a state official to bring a case against, and was successful in preventing the groups from stopping the law from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Supreme Court ruled that a challenge to the law could proceed against certain state officials, but not others.\par \par \uc2 Significantly, the court did not allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to name state court clerks or judges as defendants -- a blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in their attempt to halt legal actions brought against them by private citizens under the law.\par \par \uc2 The court allowed cases to go forward against members of state medical licensing boards, arguing that those state officials could potentially take enforcement action against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers for violating the law.\par \par \uc2 But even if those officials were later ordered not to take enforcement action, it would not remove the teeth from the law -- the threat of lawsuits by private citizens.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't alleviate that burden that has been in place, which is the potential legal liability to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers,'' said Aimee Arrambide, the executive director of Avow Texas, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group.\par \par \uc2 And the potential burden is not just on providers or clinics, but on nurses, staff members, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, and even family and friends who help facilitate the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision followed one issued late Thursday by a state district court judge in Texas, who found that the enforcement mechanism represented an ''unlawful delegation of enforcement power to a private person'' and unconstitutionally granted standing to those who were not injured, and that the law violated due process under the federal Constitution.\par \par \uc2 But the state court opinion did not include an injunction, and left the law in place. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said they would not change their behavior until a final decision had been reached at the Texas Supreme Court. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group behind the law, Texas Right to Life, immediately appealed the decision.\par \par \uc2 The twin rulings were a disappointment to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and a validation of the unique legal gambit employed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 ''At the end of the day, we're celebrating,'' said John Seago, the legislative director for Texas Right to Life, which lobbied for the law. ''The reality is the law is still having its effect. The law is still forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a heartbeat is present.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, known as Senate Bill 8, bans abortions once cardiac activity can be detected in the embryo, usually at around six weeks, before many women are even aware they are pregnant. At this stage of development, there is no heartbeat, only electrical activity in developing cells.\par \par \uc2 The law flouts the standard set out in Roe v. Wade, the decision that prevented states from banning abortions before fetal viability, the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 23 to 24 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But it has evaded legal challenge because of its unique structure. And with the law in effect, the threat of potentially limitless and costly legal actions has ensured that Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have been following the law.\par \par \uc2 The decisions arrived just over 100 days since the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban took effect in Texas, but did little to alleviate the fog of suits, countersuits and overlapping court rulings that have left clinics and women in the state reeling.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm remarkably disappointed by the decision today,'' said \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the president and chief executive officer of Whole Woman's Health, which operates four clinics in the state, on Friday. ''Officially on the books it's considered a win, but the win is so narrow.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hagstrom Miller said her clinics have been open and compliant with the law since it went into effect. They are seeing about 25 percent of their usual patient load, who are receiving abortions before six weeks, and turning away the rest.\par \par \uc2 The severe restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas have forced women to find clinics in nearby states -- New Mexico, Colorado and Oklahoma are top destinations -- inundating them with calls and patients.\par \par \uc2 At Trust Women, a clinic with locations in Oklahoma City and Wichita, Kan., the Texas law has pressed resources to the breaking point. In Oklahoma, about 60 percents of patients are now from Texas. The Kansas clinic is receiving patients from Oklahoma who can't get appointments because of the influx from Texas.\par \par \uc2 ''We're turning away patients every single day,'' said Rebecca Tong, Trust Women's acting co-executive director.\par \par \uc2 The backlog has meant women have to wait until later in their pregnancies to see providers, Ms. Tong said, leading to more surgical abortions rather than medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 That demand has been so sustained that the clinics are planning to license more physicians, add staff, and double the number of days per week that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -care clients are seen.\par \par \uc2 But among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and advocates, concern has grown with each court argument and decision that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights may soon be curtailed more broadly. That could make assisting Texas women with access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  much more difficult.\par \par \uc2 ''This paves the way for other states to pass laws like this, because they know the Supreme Court is not going to act,'' said Ms. Rupani of Fund Texas Choice, adding that in the future \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be banned in most or all Southern states. ''We can't move Texans across the entire nation. That's not sustainable.''\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights rally at the Texas Capitol in October. Travel to out-of-state providers for abortions has surged since the restrictive law took effect on Sept. 1. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211211ehcb0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
