\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Lets Texas Law Be Challenged\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law, which bans most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, was drafted to evade review in federal court and has been in effect since September.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court ruled on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas can challenge a state law banning most abortions after six weeks, allowing them to sue at least some state officials in federal court despite the procedural hurdles imposed by the law's unusual structure.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court refused to block the law in the meantime, saying that lower courts should consider the matter.\par \par \uc2 The development was both a minor victory for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and a major disappointment to them. They had hoped that the justices would reverse course from a Sept. 1 ruling that had allowed the law, the nation's most restrictive, to go into effect, causing clinics in Texas to curtail performing the procedure and forcing many women seeking abortions to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 ''We will continue to seek justice in the shred of the case that the court has allowed to go forward and seek every other legal means to stop this catastrophic law,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the providers challenging the law.\par \par \uc2 The decision provided further evidence that the Supreme Court's newly expanded conservative majority is intensely skeptical of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. At an argument in a separate case last week, the court seemed prepared to uphold a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks, a decision that would be flatly at odds with the court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedents.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  welcomed the court's decision in the Texas case.\par \par \uc2 ''We celebrate that the Texas Heartbeat Act will remain in effect, saving the lives of unborn children and protecting mothers while litigation continues in lower courts,'' said Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 She added that the group was anxiously awaiting a ruling in the Mississippi case, calling it ''the biggest opportunity in generations.''\par \par \uc2 The decision in the Texas case was fractured, and the majority endorsed only a narrow path to challenge the law. It allowed suits only against state licensing officials like the executive director of the Texas Medical Board, who are authorized to take disciplinary actions against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who violate the Texas law, known as Senate Bill 8.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers ''have plausibly alleged that S.B. 8 has already had a direct effect on their day-to-day operations,'' Justice \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  wrote for eight of the nine justices. ''And they have identified provisions of state law that appear to impose a duty on the licensing-official defendants to bring disciplinary actions against them if they violate S.B. 8.''\par \par \uc2 Only Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  dissented on that point.\par \par \uc2 But Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . and the court's three liberal members -- Justices \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan -- wrote that the limited victory for the law's challengers may well prove to be inadequate.\par \par \uc2 In partial dissents, they wrote that they would have allowed more comprehensive challenges, authorizing suits against the state's attorney general and court clerks. Justice Sotomayor wrote that the court's ruling could be evaded by a tweak to the Texas law, which generally bars state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizes private individuals to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it.\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor suggested that the court had given Texas lawmakers a road map to avoid review in federal court.\par \par \uc2 ''The dispute is over whether states may nullify federal constitutional rights by employing schemes like the one at hand,'' she wrote. ''The court indicates that they can, so long as they write their laws to more thoroughly disclaim all enforcement by state officials, including licensing officials.''\par \par \uc2 Marc A. Hearron, a lawyer who argued on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the Supreme Court, said that even a successful suit against state licensing officials would give the providers only limited relief from the effects of the law.\par \par \uc2 A court could forbid licensing officials from taking disciplinary action against doctors, nurses and pharmacists who violated the law, Mr. Hearron said. But such an injunction, he said, would not bar lawsuits from private parties seeking monetary damages from clinics, doctors and others who provide abortions or help women obtain them.\par \par \uc2 ''Make no mistake,'' he said. ''While the court allowed our legal challenge to proceed against some state licensing officials, an injunction against those officials will not block Texas' bounty-hunting scheme.''\par \par \uc2 In his own partial dissent, Chief Justice Roberts wrote that the Texas law was a calculated attempt to undermine the authority of the Supreme Court. The law is ''contrary to this court's decisions,'' including Roe v. Wade, he wrote, and ''has had the effect of denying the exercise of what we have held is a right protected under the federal Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 ''The clear purpose and actual effect of S.B. 8 has been to nullify this court's rulings,'' the chief justice wrote, adding that ''it is the role of the Supreme Court in our constitutional system that is at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights reacted to the ruling with dismay.\par \par \uc2 ''The court has abandoned its duty to ensure that states do not defy its decisions,'' Ms. Northup said.\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch stressed that the court's ruling was tentative and limited to procedural questions.\par \par \uc2 ''In this preliminary posture,'' he wrote, ''the ultimate merits question -- whether S.B. 8 is consistent with the federal Constitution -- is not before the court. Nor is the wisdom of S.B. 8 as a matter of public policy.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch wrote that there were other ways to challenge the law beyond suing state officials in federal court, including by raising its unconstitutionality as a defense in a state-court lawsuit brought under the law. He also noted that a state court judge had ruled in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 That judge found that several procedural aspects of the law violated the Texas Constitution, including ones allowing people who had suffered no injury to sue and setting a minimum recovery of $10,000.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas welcomed the state-court ruling but said that only a favorable decision from the Texas Supreme Court or the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  would allow them to start providing the procedure again without fear of crippling financial liability.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision in the Texas case came less than two weeks after the court heard a direct challenge to the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in 1973 in Roe, in a case about a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks. Roe prohibits states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 23 to 24 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The court's six-member conservative majority seemed prepared to uphold the Mississippi law, and several justices indicated that they would vote to overrule Roe outright. A decision in the case is not expected until late June.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law flouts Roe's viability line by barring abortions once fetal cardiac activity can be detected, usually around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The law was challenged by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the Biden administration. In addition to the court's decision in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' case, it also issued a brief, unsigned opinion on Friday dismissing the Justice Department's appeal, with only Justice Sotomayor noting a dissent.\par \par \uc2 The court's earlier encounter with the law left the justices bitterly divided along the same basic fault line as on Friday, with Chief Justice Roberts joining the court's three more liberal members in dissent.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion in that earlier ruling, issued just before midnight on Sept. 1, was unsigned and consisted of a single long paragraph. It said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who had challenged the law in an emergency application had not made their case in the face of ''complex and novel'' procedural questions. The majority stressed that it was not ruling on the constitutionality of the Texas law and did not mean to limit ''procedurally proper challenges'' to it.\par \par \uc2 Each of the dissenting justices issued an opinion in that earlier ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''The court's order is stunning,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''Presented with an application to enjoin a flagrantly unconstitutional law engineered to prohibit women from exercising their constitutional rights and evade judicial scrutiny, a majority of justices have opted to bury their heads in the sand.''\par \par \uc2 The case returned to the Supreme Court on two separate tracks. After the court rejected the providers' request for emergency relief, the Justice Department filed its own challenge to the law, one that it said was not subject to the procedural barriers the providers had faced.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers also returned to the court, asking the justices to use an unusual procedure -- ''certiorari before judgment'' -- to leapfrog the appeals court and decide whether they were entitled to sue.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court agreed on Oct. 22 to decide both cases, putting them on an extraordinarily fast track. It heard arguments just 10 days later, on Nov. 1, focusing on the question of whether the providers and the government were entitled to sue in light of the law's unusual structure.\par \par \uc2 The justices differed about whether the court had moved swiftly enough.\par \par \uc2 ''The court should have put an end to this madness months ago, before S.B. 8 first went into effect,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''It failed to do so then, and it fails again today.''\par \par \uc2 ''The court's delay in allowing this case to proceed has had catastrophic consequences for women seeking to exercise their constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch responded that ''this case has received extraordinary solicitude at every turn.'' The emergency application that gave rise to the court's Sept. 1 order, he wrote, was resolved in days. The latest opinions, he added, were issued fewer than 50 days after the court agreed to hear the case.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's newly expanded conservative majority is skeptical of \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY T.J. KIRKPATRICK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211211ehcb0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
