\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Rules Against  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A state judge said the approach inappropriately granted standing and denied due process. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said they would expand services if the State Supreme Court upheld the ruling.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- A state district court judge in Texas ruled on Thursday that the unique enforcement scheme of a restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law violated the State Constitution by allowing any private citizen to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or others accused of breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 In a 48-page opinion, Judge David Peeples found that the approach, which had been seen by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups as its greatest strength, unconstitutionally granted standing to those who were not injured, denied due process and represented an ''unlawful delegation of enforcement power to a private person.''\par \par \uc2 While deemed an important victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said on Thursday that they would not immediately resume performing the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The decision came in response to a number of lawsuits brought in Texas state court by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and others against Texas Right to Life, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that had lobbied for the law. The group immediately filed a notice of appeal on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ''The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry's lawsuit abuses the judicial system and turns this court into a mere platform for airing criticisms against the boldest pro-life law to take effect since Roe v. Wade,'' Kimberlyn Schwartz, a Texas Right to Life spokeswoman, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, known in Texas as Senate Bill 8 or the ''heartbeat law,'' prohibits abortions after cardiac activity has been detected, usually around six weeks of pregnancy. By expressly prohibiting state officials from enforcement, and relying instead on individuals, the law avoided the usual process of legal scrutiny and went into effect in September after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to step in.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court heard arguments about the law last month as part of an expedited process and is expected to rule soon.\par \par \uc2 The court has also been weighing arguments over a Mississippi law in a case seen as a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that prevented states from banning abortions before fetal viability. The Mississippi law bans the procedure after 15 weeks, or about two months before viability.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have said they were abiding by the new law because of the threat of legal action from any individual against someone who performs or ''aids and abets'' the procedure, including clinic staff or even the person who provided transportation to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''We have said all along that in order to fully restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas, we need a decision in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  or the Texas Supreme Court,'' said Julie Murray, a senior staff attorney at Planned Parenthood. ''Today's decision is a step in the right direction, but it is not enough relief for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.''\par \par \uc2 In his opinion, Judge Peebles wrote that the Texas Legislature did not have a right under the state's Constitution to allow people who had not suffered any injury from a violation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to come into court and sue. He also found that the granting of awards of ''no less than $10,000'' to anyone successful in their suits violated the right to due process under the 14th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Judge Peebles wrote that the law's mechanism of delegating enforcement power to private citizens, if found constitutional, could be applied to all manner of contentious issues, including guns, same-sex marriage, freedom of speech and climate change.\par \par \uc2 ''We are a diverse and creative people and it seems na\u239\'00\'EFve to hope that these procedures will be cabined voluntarily,'' he wrote.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said that if the ruling were upheld by the State Supreme Court, they would again begin providing abortions beyond six weeks of pregnancy because the law could not be enforced.\par \par \uc2 The number of abortions performed in Texas fell by roughly half in the weeks after the law went into effect, a fact that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have credited to the law's unique structure.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators outside the Texas Capitol protested a state law that bans abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ilana Panich-Linsman for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211210ehca00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
