\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 California Lawmakers Position the State To Become a 'Refuge' of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Thomas Fuller \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new legislative proposal includes the recommendation to fund the procedure for low-income women who come to California for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 Political leaders in California said on Wednesday that they would work to make the state a sanctuary for women seeking abortions if a Supreme Court ruling allows the procedure to be banned in more conservative parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 The proposal, which was released with the backing of \uc2 Gov. Gavin Newsom\uc2  and the leaders of California's two legislative chambers, calls for increasing funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and dozens of other measures to make it easier for clients to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and providers to get paid. It also includes a recommendation to fund the procedure for low-income women who come to California for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 Toni G. Atkins, the head of California's Senate, whose office helped prepare the proposal, said the details of how abortions would be funded for out-of-state women would have to be negotiated in the State Legislature next year but could be a mixture of state and private funds.\par \par \uc2 ''We consider this an incredibly critical moment in the history of women's rights,'' Ms. Atkins said in an interview. ''We want to let people know that we are going to be part of the solution, that we are a beacon.''\par \par \uc2 California's proposals have been partly spurred by developments at the bitterly divided \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , which last week appeared to be on the verge of upholding a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a ruling expected next year that could reverberate across the country.\par \par \uc2 Many of the recommendations in the proposal call for increased funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services regardless of how the Supreme Court decides. But political leaders recognize that the state could see much greater strain on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers if the court rules to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-one states have anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws already in place that could be put in effect if Roe is overturned, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that advocates \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists were unsparing in their criticism of California's efforts. Lila Rose, the California-based president of Live Action, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said the state's proposal was evil. ''More like a slaughter house than a sanctuary,'' she wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . ''Horrific.''\par \par \uc2 Mary Rose Short, the director of outreach for California Right to Life, an organization that is against terminating pregnancies and tries to change the minds of women who are headed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, said the proposal to fund abortions for out-of-state women showed that California prioritized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over childbirth.\par \par \uc2 ''We are ignoring any woman who would like to give birth but feels financially constrained,'' Ms. Short said.\par \par \uc2 About 15 percent of the nation's abortions are performed in California, slightly more than the state's 12 percent share of the national population. The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  estimates that the number of abortions performed in California could increase significantly if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Atkins, who represents San Diego and began her career at a women's health center, says California has for decades been accustomed to providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to women from Mexico, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was decriminalized in September.\par \par \uc2 She said she was ''incredibly optimistic'' that the Legislature would increase state funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services next year. Medi-Cal, the state's medical system for low-income residents, covers nearly half of all abortions performed in the state.\par \par \uc2 Erin Mellon, the acting communications director for Mr. Newsom, said it was too early to comment on whether the governor's proposed budget next year would include such funding.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mellon referred to comments Mr. Newsom made this month where he said California was a ''place of refuge.''\par \par \uc2 ''Not only for 40 million Californians but also for women and girls all across this country and will remain so proudly,'' Mr. Newsom said.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago Mr. Newsom issued an official proclamation that California was a ''reproductive freedom state.''\par \par \uc2 The recent report was prepared by Ms. Atkins's office in partnership with \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations such as Planned Parenthood that teamed together under an umbrella group called the California Future of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Council.\par \par \uc2 In a statement released on Wednesday, the head of Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California, Jodi Hicks, said her organization had ''already been feeling the impact of restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws,'' serving 7,000 out-of-state clients over the past year, including patients from Texas after a law went into effect three months ago that essentially banned abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A rally in Los Angeles. About 15 percent of all abortions in the country are performed in California. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MORGAN LIEBERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); A plan that State Senator Toni G. Atkins supports would ease access to \uc2  services. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211209ehc90003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
