\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Line of Questions Shows A Chief Justice Feeling For the Middle Ground\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The chief justice, known for his incremental approach to contentious issues, explored on Wednesday whether the court could uphold Mississippi's law without totally throwing out Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Much of the discussion of a Mississippi law that would ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy has suggested that the Supreme Court faces a binary choice: it could strike down the law and fully reaffirm Roe v. Wade, as the law's challengers want, or it could gut the idea that the Constitution protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at all, as Mississippi has urged.\par \par \uc2 But during the Supreme Court's oral arguments on Wednesday in a lawsuit challenging the law, Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . appeared to be exploring whether he could find something of a middle ground -- one that would allow the court to uphold the Mississippi law without also proclaiming that the Constitution offered no protection of any right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under Roe and a 1992 case that reaffirmed it, Planned Parenthood v. Casey, it is unconstitutional to ban abortions before ''viability,'' the point at which a fetus can survive outside the womb, which is usually about 24 weeks into pregnancy. On Wednesday, Chief Justice Roberts repeatedly returned to whether the cutoff could be earlier.\par \par \uc2 ''If you think that the issue is one of choice, that women should have a choice to terminate their pregnancy, that supposes that there is a point at which they've had the fair choice,'' he said, and went on to question why 15 weeks would be an inappropriate line. ''Because viability, it seems to me, doesn't have anything to do with choice. But, if it really is an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?''\par \par \uc2 How Chief Justice Roberts handles the case could have outsized importance. Compared to some of the other five members of the court's conservative bloc, he is broadly seen as more likely to be concerned about the institutional impact on the court if it makes a wrenching and politically contentious change in the law. He also has the power to assign himself to write the opinion if he votes with the majority.\par \par \uc2 Known for crafting narrow and incremental decisions, Chief Justice Roberts distinguished on Wednesday between an outright ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a ban on the procedure that was stricter than the current standard. At one point, he remarked that he thought moving the cutoff line to 15 weeks -- nine weeks earlier than where it is now -- was ''not a dramatic departure from viability.''\par \par \uc2 He also told the solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , who was supporting the challengers to the law, that the arguments she had made regarding problems that would result from overruling Roe ''sounded to me like being based on a total prohibition.'' He said he wondered how strong those arguments ''would be if there isn't a total prohibition.''\par \par \uc2 Similarly, addressing arguments about the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on women and their ability to have an equal place in society, Chief Justice Roberts asked \uc2 Julie Rikelman\uc2  of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a lawyer for the challengers, whether it would make a huge difference to move up the deadline to make a decision from 24 to 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''Are you suggesting that the difference between 15 weeks and viability are going to have the same sort of impacts?'' he asked.\par \par \uc2 But both Ms. Prelogar and Ms. Rikelman urged the court to keep the line at fetal viability, which Ms. Rikelman said was ''objectively verifiable and doesn't require the court to resolve the philosophical issues at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts also questioned Mississippi's solicitor general, Scott Stewart, about whether the ''viability'' line was really central to Roe, as the court said it was in the 1992 case reaffirming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The chief justice noted that fetal viability had not been part of the legal arguments in Roe, and noted that the once-confidential papers of Roe's author, Justice Harry A. Blackmun, suggested that putting the line at viability was arbitrary.\par \par \uc2 A major issue is whether it is proper for the Supreme Court to reopen the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights question under the legal doctrine of stare decisis -- under which it should hesitate to overturn precedents -- even if its current members would have decided those cases in a different way. The chief justice's line of reasoning might allow him to claim that eliminating the viability line did not really amount to overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 But Chief Justice Roberts did not offer a fleshed-out rationale for why allowing states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an earlier point in pregnancy -- but apparently still not allowing them to prohibit the procedure entirely -- would be more principled than keeping it at the point of fetal viability. And the idea with which he seemed to be playing received pushback.\par \par \uc2 At one point, he put forward the proposition that most countries that permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have an earlier cutoff line, and said the United States was like North Korea and China in using fetal viability as the standard. But Ms. Rikelman told him his understanding of other countries' laws was incorrect.\par \par \uc2 In fact, she said, while it is true that many Western countries that are similar to the United States have nominally earlier limits like 12 or 18 weeks, in reality they also allow abortions up to viability because those limits contain sweeping exceptions for ''broad social reasons, health reasons, socioeconomic reasons.''\par \par \uc2 The arguments also focused on whether upholding the Mississippi law without also declaring there is no constitutional right at all to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a middle ground, or rather would just open the door to a floodgate of stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and lawsuits challenging them.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rikelman warned Chief Justice Roberts that there would be no principled stopping point if the court upheld the Mississippi law, so states would ''rush to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at virtually any point in pregnancy.'' Ms. Prelogar similarly predicted that if the Mississippi law was upheld, states would immediately enact bans at 10, eight or six weeks to push further.\par \par \uc2 But Chief Justice Roberts brushed aside that concern and a similar objection by saying, ''I'd like to focus on the 15-week ban,'' and that ''the thing that is at issue before us today is 15 weeks.''\par \par \uc2 At another point, he sought an explanation from Mr. Stewart about why Mississippi had shifted from focusing on whether the viability cutoff was constitutionally required -- as it did in its petition asking the Supreme Court to take up the case -- to arguing more sweepingly for Roe and Casey to be flatly overruled.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stewart told him that the state's legal team shifted gears in part because the more modest argument that viability was not the appropriate standard had ''lost in every court of appeals.''\par \par \uc2 Julie Rikelman, at left, of the Center for \uc2 , was questioned by Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. about the Mississippi law's 15-week cutoff. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GABRIELA BHASKAR/THE NEW YORK TIMES; ALYSSA SCHUKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211215ehc20000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
