\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Case Makes a Novel Argument, but the Reality Is More Complex\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller \par \uc2 1,572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One argument in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case before the Supreme Court is that balancing work and family is now less of a challenge, but research shows becoming a mother still has a large economic impact on women.\par \par \uc2 The case that could lead to the end of Roe v. Wade includes a novel argument: that the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary because it has become much easier for women to combine work and family.\par \par \uc2 In Roe, the Supreme Court said that an unwanted pregnancy could lead women to ''a distressful life and future,'' and in a 1992 case, Casey v. Planned Parenthood, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were necessary for ''women to participate equally in the economic and social life of the nation.''\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the court is hearing a case from Mississippi regarding a law banning most abortions after 15 weeks. In a brief to the court, lawyers for the state argued that those ideas about women's lives had been obviated by ''the march of progress.''\par \par \uc2 ''In these last 50 years,'' Mississippi's attorney general, Lynn Fitch, said in a statement, ''women have carved their own ways to achieving a better balance for success in their professional and personal lives.''\par \par \uc2 Mississippi makes other arguments that are likely to be more pivotal to the case, including that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law should be up to the states, and that because of scientific advances, fetuses can survive outside the womb earlier. The argument about economic opportunity is novel, though, because it focuses on the effects on women.\par \par \uc2 ''The argument is: You can have it all,'' said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the Florida State University College of Law. ''They're saying there's no need because people like Amy Coney Barrett exist. Women can have big families and successful professional lives and there's no need to choose.'' (Justice Barrett, who joined the court under President Trump last year, has seven children.)\par \par \uc2 The argument serves another legal purpose, she said: If the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is ''decades out of date,'' as the state's brief said, there is a justification for overturning precedent.\par \par \uc2 A large body of social science and policy research tells a more complex story. While women receive more education than men, are increasingly likely to be primary breadwinners and have made significant gains in public and professional life, becoming a mother still has a significant economic impact. So does being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The people most affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are poor women -- who also have the hardest time combining paid work and child rearing.\par \par \uc2 Public policy\par \par \uc2 In its argument, Mississippi's lawyers wrote that ''numerous laws enacted since Roe -- addressing pregnancy discrimination, requiring leave time, assisting with child care and more -- facilitate the ability of women to pursue both career success and a rich family life.''\par \par \uc2 These policies don't exist or are weak, on a federal level or in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The United States stands out for its absence of national paid leave -- it is one of six countries in the world, and the only rich country, without it. It provides 12 weeks of unpaid leave, yet nearly half of workers don't qualify, and many more can't afford to take unpaid leave. Some states have paid family leave, but Mississippi does not.\par \par \uc2 On child care assistance, the United States again stands out. While rich countries spend an average of $14,000 a year on child care subsidies for toddlers, most families in the United States receive several hundred dollars in the form of a tax credit. Child care is unaffordable for nearly half of American families, according to the Treasury Department, and 71 percent of Mississippi families. Democrats are currently considering a bill that would provide four weeks of paid leave, subsidized child care for most families and universal pre-K.\par \par \uc2 The Pregnancy Discrimination Act of 1978 said employers could not treat pregnant women differently, and a 2015 Supreme Court ruling required them to make accommodations for pregnant women in some circumstances. But federal protections are weak in many cases, and a large analysis this year, of complaints to the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  and survey data, found that pregnancy discrimination remains rampant.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  scholars made another point: There might be more public policies supporting mothers if there weren't strong \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Without Roe, they said, there could be more of a recognition of the differences between women's and men's reproductive lives, and more policies to accommodate pregnancy and child rearing.\par \par \uc2 Correlation or causation?\par \par \uc2 Another argument made by Mississippi and those supporting the state's case is that there is no way to prove a causal link between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and women's ability to play an equal role in society.\par \par \uc2 ''It may be possible to claim anecdotally that a particular woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to preserve her opportunity to pursue a particular job or degree,'' wrote 240 female scholars and members of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in a brief to the Court. ''But it is impossible to claim that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is specially responsible for the progress that American women have made.''\par \par \uc2 A group of 154 economists also wrote to the court, saying the idea that children no longer affect women's trajectories is ''premature and false.'' Also, the group said, sophisticated economic methods have demonstrated causal effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Researchers were able to compare similar people who either could or couldn't get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on a policy change, as Caitlin Knowles Myers, an economist at Middlebury and an author of the brief, has described.\par \par \uc2 Studies found, for example, that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for young women with unplanned pregnancies led to higher earnings, more education and a higher chance of being a professional or manager than seen in women denied access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The effects were greatest among Black women.\par \par \uc2 One of the best-known examples of the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is the Turnaway Study. It included women who went to 30 clinics in 21 states, and compared those who were just under or over the gestational cutoff for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In follow-up research, scientists looked at their credit reports for several years before and after the unwanted pregnancy. Being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased the chance that women were living in poverty, were unemployed, had overdue debt or had experienced bankruptcies or evictions.\par \par \uc2 ''There is long-lasting economic impact of being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Diana Greene Foster, a demographer at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who led the study. ''The thing I learned most from my study is that they're making rational decisions taking into account their life circumstances. If somehow magically every pregnancy was supported and all lives would be rosy, people would be taking that into account. Instead, people are looking at their circumstances and thinking things are going to be really hard. And all the things they think are right, because we see those outcomes among the people who are denied abortions.''\par \par \uc2 Other recent research finds that having a child has continued to affect women's economic outcomes. Around the world, gender gaps in pay and promotions widen when children are born. In the United States, the employment rate of women relative to men drops 20 to 25 percentage points when children are born, and their earnings drop 35 points, according to Henrik Kleven, a professor of economics and public affairs at Princeton.\par \par \uc2 ''We have so much evidence that the impact of parenthood is highly unequal for men and women,'' he said. ''There is still some gender inequality on average between men and women without children, but the impact solely of parenthood explains most of it.''\par \par \uc2 The declining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate\par \par \uc2 A third argument in support of the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is unnecessary is that the expansion of women's rights predated Roe, and women's progress has continued even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates have fallen.\par \par \uc2 ''Women's motivation, determination and talent, plus state laws supporting that, is what has led to women's advancement in society, not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Teresa Stanton Collett, a professor at the \uc2 University of St. Thomas\uc2  School of Law and one of the authors of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  brief.\par \par \uc2 People in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say women are planning their educations and careers knowing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access exists, should they need it. This is an argument known as reliance -- that people make choices based on existing rules.\par \par \uc2 The court used it in deciding Casey v. Planned Parenthood: ''For two decades of economic and social developments, people have organized intimate relationships and made choices that define their views of themselves and their places in society, in reliance on the availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event that contraception should fail.''\par \par \uc2 Whatever decision the court makes about the Mississippi law, the case, along with other recent state restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has cast doubt on whether women can still make plans based on that reliance, Professor Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 ''Mississippi is trying to redefine whether they can reasonably do that,'' she said. ''It's a combination of saying women don't need this anymore, and even if they do, they can't reasonably expect it to be there.''\par \par \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett last year during her confirmation hearing for the Supreme Court, with her husband and children behind her. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIN SCHAFF/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211202ehc20004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
