\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Ruling Could Make  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  the Leading Issue in Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carl Hulse \par \uc2 1,357 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Both sides anticipate that a Supreme Court decision scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would roil next year's elections, with Democrats sensing an advantage.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- A Supreme Court ruling to weaken or overturn the landmark Roe v. Wade decision in the middle of next year's midterm election campaign would immediately elevate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into a defining issue and most likely reinvigorate efforts to overhaul the court itself.\par \par \uc2 Even as the justices weigh the case of the Mississippi law barring most abortions after 15 weeks, the political clash is already intensifying, with Democrats warning supporters that the court is poised to reverse access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  50 years after it was recognized as a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 ''What is fundamentally at stake is that every woman in our country should be able to make her own health care decisions and chart her own destiny and have the full independence to do that,'' said Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , Democrat of New Hampshire, who is seeking re-election in a race with significant implications for control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 As the court heard arguments in the Mississippi case on Wednesday, it appeared that the six conservative justices were likely to uphold the state's law despite the precedent set in 1973 by Roe, which held that states could not bar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, now judged to be around 22 to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Several of the justices suggested that they were willing to go another step and overturn Roe entirely, leaving states free to impose whatever bans or restrictions they choose. The court is likely to release its decision in the case at the end of its term in June or early July, just as campaigning in the midterms is getting into full swing.\par \par \uc2 While the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Supreme Court has traditionally been seen as more of an energizing issue for Republican and evangelical voters, Democrats say that situation could be reversed should the court undermine Roe, raising the possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be banned or severely limited in many states.\par \par \uc2 That outcome, Democrats said, would transform the long fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from theory to reality and give new resonance to their arguments that a Democratic Congress is needed to protect access to the procedure and seat judges who are not hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no question that should the decision be one that would overturn Roe v. Wade, it will certainly motivate our base,'' said Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan, the chairman of the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee. ''Quite frankly, we know that a majority of the people in this country continue to believe it should be the law of the land.''\par \par \uc2 ''It will be an incredibly powerful issue,'' Mr. Peters said.\par \par \uc2 Republicans see advantages as well, saying it will validate their decades-long push to limit if not outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and show that they should not back away from their efforts when they are succeeding.\par \par \uc2 ''Today is our day,'' Representative Steve Scalise of Louisiana, the No. 2 House Republican, told \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents outside the Supreme Court on Wednesday. ''This is what we've been working for.''\par \par \uc2 Aware that a decision undermining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has political risks for them as well, Republicans say the fight will be just part of their 2022 message as they seek to tie Democrats to inflation, the chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan and other subjects where they see a greater edge.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a lot of issues out there,'' said Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , Republican of Florida and the chairman of the National Republican Senatorial Committee, suggesting the significance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will vary from state to state. ''Everybody's going to take a position.''\par \par \uc2 But it was quickly clear that some Republicans would embrace the drive against Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm pro-life. I'm anti-Roe v. Wade,'' Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 , the Louisiana Republican who is seeking a second term next year, said in a fund-raising appeal sent hours after the court debate. ''There is not much else I can say other than that.''\par \par \uc2 In addition to the congressional elections, how the justices dispose of the case holds potentially grave implications for the court itself. The stature and credibility of the court were prominent subtexts of Wednesday's arguments, with Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  pointedly asking how the court would ''survive the stench'' of overturning Roe in what many would see as a blatantly political act.\par \par \uc2 After Senate Republicans in 2016 blocked President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  from filling a Supreme Court vacancy with almost a year left in his term, progressives began calling for adding seats to the court or setting term limits on the now-lifetime appointments to offset what they saw as an unfair advantage seized by Republicans. Then, when Republicans seated Justice Amy Coney Barrett just days before the 2020 election, those calls intensified.\par \par \uc2 However, President Biden, a former chairman of the Senate Judiciary Committee, has been lukewarm to the idea of tinkering with the court, and a commission he formed to study the idea is not expected to embrace significant changes.\par \par \uc2 But demands for expanding the court or instituting other changes are likely to be reignited if the justices reverse what much of the country sees as an important precedent after hardball politics played a major role in constituting the court's conservative membership.\par \par \uc2 ''This push will go into hyperdrive if the court upholds Mississippi's ban, let alone overturns Roe outright,'' predicted Brian Fallon, the executive director of the progressive group Demand Justice.\par \par \uc2 Given Mr. Biden's struggles and the tradition of voters turning on the party that controls the White House in midterm elections, Democrats see the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight as a potential way to attract the suburban voters -- particularly women -- who helped elect Mr. Biden and Democratic majorities in 2020 but moved away from Democrats in elections this year.\par \par \uc2 ''We're talking about rolling back the clock on health care for women 50 years,'' said Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  of Michigan, a member of the Democratic leadership. ''Obviously a whole generation of women have been able to get the health care they need and make their own reproductive choices, and I think you'll be shocked to fully see what this means.''\par \par \uc2 Anticipating an adverse Supreme Court ruling, House Democrats this year passed on a party-line vote a bill that would incorporate Roe into federal law. The Senate is expected to vote on it at some point to put Republicans on the record, but it has no chance of passage since it will be blocked by a Republican filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Party strategists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue has already demonstrated salience in Nevada, another key race in the battle for Senate control. Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who is seeking re-election, is a strong proponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, while a leading Republican opponent, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and as attorney general joined efforts to limit the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In New Hampshire, a state with a history of strongly favoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ms. Hassan and fellow Democrats have repeatedly criticized state Republicans for cutting off funding to Planned Parenthood and instituting new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions such as mandatory ultrasounds for those seeking to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Despite the decision by \uc2 Gov. Chris Sununu\uc2 , a Republican, not to challenge her next November, Ms. Hassan is still likely to face difficult opposition given the political climate. She vowed in a statement on Wednesday that she ''will not be shy about contrasting my record of protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their support for policies that take away women's liberty.''\par \par \uc2 Her Democratic state colleague, Senator Jeanne Shaheen, told reporters this week before the court hearing that ''we cannot allow Republican lawmakers to turn back the clock on women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rights, which is precisely what the Mississippi case seeks to do.''\par \par \uc2 ''It is time to sound the alarm,'' Ms. Shaheen said.\par \par \uc2 Outside the Supreme Court on Wednesday, when the justices heard arguments on the Mississippi law that bars most abortions after 15 weeks. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Gabriela Bhaskar/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211202ehc20003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscale