\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Its Day in Court Nearing,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinic Carries On\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rick Rojas \par \uc2 1,754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JACKSON, Miss. -- The patients that morning included a teenager accompanied by her mother, an employee from a nearby hospital wearing scrubs, a college student in a sorority T-shirt, and a woman who had left home well before sunrise for the seven-hour trip from Texas.\par \par \uc2 They had all come seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the last clinic operating in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Waiting for them was Dr. Cheryl Hamlin, an obstetrician-gynecologist from Massachusetts, part of a rotation of out-of-state doctors who do the work that local physicians will not.\par \par \uc2 ''I am required by the state of Mississippi to tell you,'' Dr. Hamlin said, ''that having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase your risk of breast cancer.''\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't,'' she added without hesitation. ''Nobody thinks it does. The American College of OB-GYNs doesn't think it does.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic, Jackson Women's Health Organization, has long negotiated measures intended by the Legislature to discourage women from obtaining abortions and to make it difficult for providers to operate. They include the requirement that doctors warn patients about a link between breast cancer and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says ''scientific evidence does not support the notion.''\par \par \uc2 Now the culmination of those legislative efforts -- a state law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy -- has thrust the clinic into the center of a case that may lead to one of the most consequential rulings on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in decades.\par \par \uc2 Jackson Women's Health Organization sued Mississippi over the new restrictions, and lawyers will argue the case before the Supreme Court on Wednesday. The implications will most likely be felt far beyond Jackson. The law was conceived as a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade, which established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and blocks states from banning the procedure before fetal viability, which most experts estimate at about 23 or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The law, which includes exceptions for medical emergencies or severe fetal abnormalities, reflected the efforts of the state's leaders to make ''Mississippi the safest place in America for an unborn child,'' Phil Bryant, the former Republican governor, said as he signed the legislation in 2018.\par \par \uc2 If the justices overturn Roe completely, the ruling will trigger laws in about a dozen states -- including Mississippi -- that will make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlawful almost immediately. Even if Roe is not overturned entirely, the court could give states greater latitude to limit how and when abortions are performed.\par \par \uc2 Although the Jackson clinic's survival hangs in the balance, it has never been busier. In recent months, it has expanded its hours of operation from three days a week to five. The phone rings constantly with people trying to make appointments, calling from across Mississippi and more and more from Texas -- an influx attributed to the new law there that is the most restrictive in the country. The clinic now sees about 300 women a month.\par \par \uc2 ''Our patient load has almost doubled,'' said Shannon Brewer, the clinic's director. ''We are adjusting,'' she added. ''We just know it's got to be done.''\par \par \uc2 The weight of the ruling's implications has been inescapable. In recent weeks, camera crews have crowded into Ms. Brewer's office. A Time magazine cover featured a photograph of her staring forlornly into the distance.\par \par \uc2 But taking the time to ruminate on all of that feels like a luxury. ''By the time I get home and get situated, I go to sleep,'' Ms. Brewer said. ''I'm not sitting up thinking about it.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hamlin, who travels to Mississippi about once a month, arrived at the clinic around 8 a.m. on a recent Monday with two and a half packed days ahead of her.\par \par \uc2 The morning started with state-mandated counseling sessions that begin a 24-hour countdown before patients are allowed to complete the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process. About a half-dozen women at a time sat in a back room as Dr. Hamlin gave an overview that vividly illustrated the clash between Mississippi's legal requirements and her professional opinion.\par \par \uc2 ''There are many laws restricting the care that we can provide here,'' Dr. Hamlin said. ''I'd encourage you all -- if this is an issue important to you -- to vote in any upcoming elections.''\par \par \uc2 The women then lined up to talk with her individually. They came from all walks of life but mostly reflected the demographics of Jackson, which has a largely African American population, and Mississippi, one of the poorest states in the country.\par \par \uc2 As one patient sat across from the desk, Dr. Hamlin flipped through a folder with a copy of her sonogram and her medical information. Her blood pressure was somewhat high, she noted.\par \par \uc2 ''It could just be that you're nervous, too,'' Dr. Hamlin said.\par \par \uc2 The woman needed a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dr. Hamlin looked over a calendar. Her only option was to come back the next day or wait another week, which would leave her with a narrow window. The 15-week restriction has been stayed pending a court ruling, but the clinic, restricted by a multitude of other state laws, performs abortions only up to 16 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''I would like to come tomorrow,'' the woman replied, ''but I want to make sure I have all the money.''\par \par \uc2 Surgical procedures range from $650 to $800. Medication abortions cost $600. Patients must pay in cash or by credit card, but some financial assistance is available.\par \par \uc2 ''Why don't I put you down for tomorrow?'' Dr. Hamlin said. ''And if for some reason you feel like you can't make it, just let us know.''\par \par \uc2 ''Because clearly,'' she added, ''the sooner the better.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic, known as the Pink House for the flamingo shade of its stucco exterior, is situated in the lively Fondren neighborhood of Jackson, a small arts district with boutiques, coffee shops and trendy restaurants.\par \par \uc2 It predates the neighborhood's upswing and has made for a sometimes awkward neighbor. Demonstrators for and against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly face off outside, often at high volume.\par \par \uc2 The entrance to the clinic is blocked by a fence covered with black paneling to protect patients' privacy. On this day, a demonstrator walked along the exterior as women went inside: ''You're already a mama and daddy to the baby!'' she yelled. ''Let us help you! You're paying someone to kill your baby!''\par \par \uc2 Derenda Hancock, who leads the Pink House defenders, as the volunteers who escort women in and out of the clinic are known, apologized to a patient on the other side of the fence.\par \par \uc2 ''They're not going to block me,'' the woman replied. ''I've got to live with it. They don't have to live with it.''\par \par \uc2 Just outside the fence, Pam Miller, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protester, waved as cars came and went, trying to hand the women gift bags with Cheez-It crackers, squeeze pouches of apple sauce and a pamphlet about reversing the effects of the first part of a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a practice touted by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups but viewed by many researchers as based on dubious science.\par \par \uc2 ''To me, it is a biblical issue,'' Ms. Miller said, ''because God created people in his image and that means we're special, we're of intrinsic worth just for being.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hamlin had planned to become a veterinarian, but a college guidance counselor nudged her toward medical school. In her first year, she shadowed a doctor who let her watch as a woman gave birth.\par \par \uc2 ''I just thought it was the most miraculous thing I've ever seen in my life,'' she recalled. ''I cried.''\par \par \uc2 She decided to become an OB-GYN. ''I just felt like the doctors were so paternalistic,'' said Dr. Hamlin, who finished her residency in 1992.\par \par \uc2 In the fall of 2016, Dr. Hamlin, a hospital physician in the Boston area, found herself unsettled by Donald J. Trump's election. She realized there was a large chunk of the country she did not understand. She signed up to work at the clinic in Jackson and another in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 During a typical day in Jackson, she sees more than a dozen patients, and most of her counseling sessions last just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 ''This is your first pregnancy?'' Dr. Hamlin asked one patient, scanning her chart. ''Looks like you're healthy. Your blood pressure is good. Blood count is good. All right, anything else?''\par \par \uc2 Afternoons are devoted to surgical procedures and group sessions in which she dispenses pills for medication abortions and instructs women on the process.\par \par \uc2 From her desk, Ms. Brewer, the clinic director, watches a screen showing the view of security cameras posted around the clinic, alert for signs of trouble. There have been threats, and confrontations have erupted outside.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm a very cautious person because of this place,'' said Ms. Brewer, the director for 11 years and an employee for 20.\par \par \uc2 Jackson is a city of roughly 154,000 people that can feel like a small town, which is especially true for the people who work at the clinic. Ms. Brewer used to live down the street from one regular protester. She occasionally sees others in the grocery store. ''They usually seem more startled when they see me out away from this place,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 After the last patient left that day, Dr. Hamlin worked her way through a pile of folders on her desk, reviewing charts and signing paperwork.\par \par \uc2 The clinic has not had a staff doctor in over a decade. Local physicians, whether because of moral opposition or safety concerns, have declined to do such work. Instead, the clinic maintains a house to host doctors from out of state. Dr. Hamlin keeps a pair of running shoes and some clothes there, but her connection to the city is limited. Her job is not without risk, but unlike other women working at the clinic she knows she can return to Massachusetts after each short trip.\par \par \uc2 ''I come and leave,'' she said, ''and they carry on.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Cheryl Hamlin travels once a month from Massachusetts to Mississippi to help women who are seeking abortions.; Jackson Women's Health Organization is Mississippi's only \uc2  clinic. State law bans abortions after 15 weeks.; Near the desk of Shannon Brewer, the director of the Mississippi clinic, is a screen showing security camera footage.; The Pink House defenders, left, volunteer to escort women in and out of the clinic, at times with protesters nearby. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GABRIELA BHASKAR/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211201ehc100052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
