\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 What to Watch in the Supreme Court Oral Arguments on the Right to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  will be streaming the oral arguments and providing live coverage of the proceedings when they begin at 10 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will hear arguments on Wednesday in the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case in a generation, a challenge to a Mississippi law that bars most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The law is at odds with Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prohibited states from banning abortions before fetal viability, the point at which the fetus can survive outside the womb, or around 23 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Mississippi have asked the court to overrule Roe, a move that would allow states to ban abortions at any time or entirely.\par \par \uc2 Where can I listen?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  will be streaming the oral arguments and providing live coverage of the proceedings when they begin at 10 a.m. Eastern. The argument is scheduled to last 70 minutes but will probably approach two hours.\par \par \uc2 What are the key arguments?\par \par \uc2 The two sides say there is no middle ground. Lawyers for the state argue that the Constitution says nothing about a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that Roe is ''egregiously wrong.'' They urge the court to overrule the decision and to return the question of whether and when to allow abortions to the states.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Mississippi's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic say that Roe was a principled decision that has been repeatedly reaffirmed by the Supreme Court, most notably in Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that revised Roe but retained its core holding, that women can choose to have abortions until fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Neither side supports a third possible outcome, one in which the court upholds the Mississippi law but does not overrule Roe in so many words. That would require the court to do away with the fetal-viability cutoff and replace it with a different standard, a task legal experts say presents a daunting challenge.\par \par \uc2 Why is this important?\par \par \uc2 Without Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably become illegal in 22 states. Forty-one percent of women of childbearing age would see the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic close, and the average distance they would have to travel to reach one would be 280 miles, up from 36 miles now. In practical terms, overruling Roe would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  inaccessible to many poor women.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters also say that overruling Roe could damage the legitimacy of the Supreme Court by doing away with what has been a constitutional right for half of a century following a change in the court's membership. Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who believed that women's equality requires access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , died last year and was replaced by Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who has said she opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case before the Supreme Court on Wednesday regarding a Mississippi law is the most important \uc2  case in decades. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Stefani Reynolds for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211201ehc10004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
