\par\par \uc2 SARAH WILDMAN\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 'Her Heart Was Beating Too'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Wildman \par \uc2 1,559 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In 2012, Savita Halappanavar, a 31-year-old dentist in Ireland, appeared at University Hospital Galway in pain. She was 17 weeks pregnant and miscarrying. According to Dr. Halappanavar's husband, hospital staff said that there was no saving the pregnancy, but they refused to intercede because her fetus still had a heartbeat. She was told her only option was to wait.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Halappanavar became feverish. By the time the fetal heartbeat faded away, she was in organ failure. Two and a half days later, she was dead.\par \par \uc2 Nearly three decades earlier, Ireland's leaders created one of the world's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws with an amendment to their nation's Constitution, cementing Ireland's near-total ban on abortions. After that, women who were able left the country for the procedure, while those who couldn't lived with the consequences of the law. The amendment remained in place despite many examples of related brutality during that period, including a suicidal 14-year-old girl who was impregnated through rape. Her family had to plead to the highest court in the nation for her to even travel for a termination. She lost her case at first, but mass protest prompted the court to reconvene, and she was ultimately allowed to travel to Britain for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Halappanavar's story had an even greater impact. When she died, her husband stepped forward immediately and said Ireland's restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were to blame in her death. Her death created a surge of grief and anger that became a focal point of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and women's rights movement in Ireland, and contributed to the drive to overturn the country's constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 ''People would say, 'This is for Savita,' and she came to symbolize all women who had to struggle and suffer because of that ban,'' an Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activist, Ailbhe Smyth, told me. In 2018, Irish voters finally overturned the amendment.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, with Roe v. Wade likely to be largely dismantled, if not overturned, next year as a result of a case being argued before the Supreme Court on Wednesday, it is time to look again at the women whose lives -- and deaths -- changed how the public understands what's at stake when we talk about banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The thing I worry about in the United States is that the rallying cry won't happen until women die, and that's so unnecessary and unfortunate,'' said Kathryn Kolbert, who in 1992 argued the major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case Planned Parenthood v. Casey before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 It should not take a high-profile death to expose just how much is at risk when medicine is hamstrung by politics, religion or culture. And yet, interviewed for The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , women across Ireland described how learning about Dr. Halappanavar's story had woken them up to the reality that their very lives were on the line -- not only if they found themselves facing an unwanted pregnancy, but also if a wanted pregnancy went wrong.\par \par \uc2 When you look at which stories have fueled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activism in other countries, Ms. Kolbert's worry seems well founded. In September a 30-year-old mother identified in the press only as Izabela arrived at a Polish county hospital. According to a lawyer for her family, she was 22 weeks pregnant and the prognosis was poor: There was little or no amniotic fluid, and sonograms showed the fetus bore abnormalities. Still, a heartbeat remained.\par \par \uc2 Last year the Polish high court struck down a provision in the country's already draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that allowed for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of fetal abnormality.\par \par \uc2 Izabela knew that her situation was grim. She sent a text message to her mother from the hospital: ''The baby weighs 485 grams. For now, because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, I have to lie down. They can't do anything. They are going to wait until he dies or something else happens. Oh and also, I could die of septic shock.''\par \par \uc2 In time, the fetus died. Then Izabela died, too.\par \par \uc2 (''Doctors and midwives did everything in their power. They fought a difficult battle for the patient and her child,'' the hospital said in a statement.)\par \par \uc2 Eventually, Izabela's mother made her texts public. Days later, in early November, Polish protesters marched with signs that said, ''Her heart was beating too'' and ''Not one more.'' In apparent response to the protesters, \uc2 Poland's Health Ministry\uc2  ''clarified'' the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, insisting that the procedure remains available to save the life of the pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 Storytelling, Ms. Kolbert pointed out, has always been a tool in the arsenal of the political movement to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, or to win them in the first place. In recent years, the focus among activists in the United States has shifted away from describing dangerous back-alley abortions and become one of empowerment, focused on sharing stories that help remove the stigma and shame that still clings to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But in the years before Roe, clergy members, legislators, media and feminist activists hoped that telling women's stories of victimization, humiliation and death could humanize the need for universal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and bring about legalization. One such story began with a 1964 police photo of a woman's bloodied, lifeless body, facedown on a motel carpet. The woman was Geraldine Santoro, known as Gerri, 28 and a mother of two. Ms. Santoro had been fearful of what her estranged and violent husband would do to her if he discovered she was pregnant with a lover's child. Her boyfriend attempted to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Ms. Santoro, accidentally killing her in the process. (He fled and was later convicted of manslaughter.)\par \par \uc2 That photo of Ms. Santoro was published in Ms. magazine in 1973, under the words ''Never Again.'' The image was blown up on placards carried at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rallies, a visceral illustration of the risks of illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In recent years, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in America has deteriorated, especially for poor women and women of color. But it may be harder to motivate protesters now, in an era where women of reproductive age have spent their entire lives with the protections of the Roe era. The back-alley abortions that motivated the movement in the past are largely someone else's memory.\par \par \uc2 There are other fears now. Today, a person could be charged with a crime after miscarrying or could face legal consequences for ingesting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills ordered on the internet. In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has been whittled down, legal provisions promising to safeguard the life of the pregnant woman are left to interpretation by medical personnel. But this is a space without clear answers, and hospital staffs will inevitably factor their own legal and professional risk into what would otherwise be a decision about the patient's best interest.\par \par \uc2 Texas' law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy has been in effect since September, and already, The Lily has reported, a woman in the state who experienced an ectopic pregnancy said she was turned away for care. Ectopic pregnancies, in which a fertilized egg implants outside the uterus, require immediate termination because they endanger the patient's fertility and her life.\par \par \uc2 In theory, terminating an ectopic pregnancy is not banned under the Texas law. But in this case, according to the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2 's hotline director, who spoke with The Lily, doctors were afraid to intercede, and the woman ended up driving at least 12 hours to New Mexico for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The Texas woman with the ectopic pregnancy survived her ordeal. But as more states consider passing laws like Texas', the next woman might not. What will happen then? Will we know her name? Will she become a rallying cry? Or will she and other women with tragic stories fade into obscurity, their families fearful of coming forward? No one wants to see this happen, but what are we doing to prevent it?\par \par \uc2 I called up Lynn Paltrow, the executive director of the National Advocates for Pregnant Women (who happens to be my cousin by marriage), and asked her: Why does tremendous outcry over restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws come after a woman dies, rather than before? Ms. Paltrow was biting in her response. ''The primary impact of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has not been to stop abortions. It is to dehumanize,'' she said. ''It is martyrdom and the visible suffering and death of a visible woman that reminds people of their humanity and their right to life.''\par \par \uc2 In Texas and elsewhere, Americans shouldn't wait for another woman's heart to stop beating before they demand change.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Wildman is a staff editor and writer in Opinion. She is the author of ''Paper Love: Searching for the Girl My Grandfather Left Behind.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Savita Halappanavar of Ireland became ill and died after being refused an \uc2  as she miscarried. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CLODAGH KILCOYNE/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211201ehc10003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
